00:09:29.305 --> 00:09:30.372
are we recording?

268
00:09:30.372 --> 00:09:31.205
Yeah, we're on.

269
00:09:31.205 --> 00:09:32.038
Okay, yeah, yeah, yeah.

270
00:09:32.038 --> 00:09:34.035
I got a little bit of trouble at Stanford

271
00:09:34.035 --> 00:09:35.143
and then I switched.

272
00:09:35.143 --> 00:09:36.310
Why is that?

273
00:09:38.617 --> 00:09:40.698
Because Dick Jokoms would come to the practice

274
00:09:40.698 --> 00:09:43.356
to coach me and 'cause he knew

275
00:09:43.356 --> 00:09:45.804
like I wanted to do ultra distance swimming so.

276
00:09:45.804 --> 00:09:46.698
Right.

277
00:09:46.698 --> 00:09:49.387
I'd be at the Stanford Master's practice

278
00:09:49.387 --> 00:09:51.761
and Dick would be standing there giving me instruction

279
00:09:51.761 --> 00:09:53.356
and afterwards, the Stanford Master's coach, understandably,

280
00:09:53.356 --> 00:09:54.666
was like, dude, you can't have Dick standing here.

281
00:09:54.666 --> 00:09:55.788
Right.

282
00:09:55.788 --> 00:09:56.713
And I was like,

283
00:09:56.713 --> 00:09:57.671
alright, well then I'm gonna go swim at Menlo.

284
00:09:57.671 --> 00:09:58.825
It's sort of classic Atilla though.

285
00:09:58.825 --> 00:10:00.247
Like you're gonna do a end

286
00:10:00.247 --> 00:10:03.892
run around whatever the protocol is and figure out.

287
00:10:03.892 --> 00:10:05.973
So in deference and respect to the program,

288
00:10:05.973 --> 00:10:07.593
I went and joined Menlo and they were totally cool.

289
00:10:07.593 --> 00:10:08.426
Right.

290
00:10:08.426 --> 00:10:09.503
And it was a much more mellow program.

291
00:10:09.503 --> 00:10:10.336
Yeah, yeah.

292
00:10:10.336 --> 00:10:11.440
I know some of the people there, that's cool.

293
00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.261
But they were like, yeah,

294
00:10:13.261 --> 00:10:14.208
we don't care what you do.

295
00:10:14.208 --> 00:10:16.327
Well, what's interesting is you picked up swimming

296
00:10:16.327 --> 00:10:17.446
later in life.

297
00:10:17.446 --> 00:10:21.385
It's a very difficult thing to do for most people

298
00:10:21.385 --> 00:10:23.911
and then distinguish yourself in some pretty impressive

299
00:10:23.911 --> 00:10:25.350
water performances.

300
00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:28.757
I know that you downplay it and all of that,

301
00:10:28.757 --> 00:10:31.111
but are you not like the only person?

302
00:10:31.111 --> 00:10:32.413
You were the first, right?

303
00:10:32.413 --> 00:10:33.715
I'm sure other people have done it.

304
00:10:33.715 --> 00:10:36.020
The double Maui channel crossing?

305
00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:38.517
Yeah, although I have to be honest with you,

306
00:10:38.517 --> 00:10:40.775
I found out a few years later,

307
00:10:40.775 --> 00:10:41.831
that there might have been somebody

308
00:10:41.831 --> 00:10:43.169
who did it earlier,

309
00:10:43.169 --> 00:10:45.182
but I don't know the details of it, but at the time,

310
00:10:45.182 --> 00:10:46.320
it appeared I was the first to do it.

311
00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:47.451
Yeah.

312
00:10:47.451 --> 00:10:49.201
That's no small feat.

313
00:10:50.419 --> 00:10:52.010
I mean, look, I think you have to put feats

314
00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:54.593
in different categories, right?

315
00:10:56.153 --> 00:10:59.025
I think many more people could have a potential

316
00:10:59.025 --> 00:11:01.521
to do something where the challenge is just,

317
00:11:01.521 --> 00:11:03.276
can you grind it out?

318
00:11:03.276 --> 00:11:04.622
Then the number of people

319
00:11:04.622 --> 00:11:05.616
that have the potential to say,

320
00:11:05.616 --> 00:11:07.683
be the fastest to do something.

321
00:11:07.683 --> 00:11:08.535
I think those are just two different things.

322
00:11:08.535 --> 00:11:10.104
Right.

323
00:11:10.104 --> 00:11:11.583
If you're willing to put in

324
00:11:11.583 --> 00:11:12.978
the unbelievable amount of time

325
00:11:12.978 --> 00:11:14.326
to prepare for something like that,

326
00:11:14.326 --> 00:11:16.301
it's doable essentially, is what you're saying.

327
00:11:16.301 --> 00:11:20.551
Absolutely, it's just about putting your head down

328
00:11:20.551 --> 00:11:23.397
and actually the challenge with the Maui swim,

329
00:11:23.397 --> 00:11:25.475
is I wanted to start at midnight.

330
00:11:25.475 --> 00:11:27.524
So originally my plan was to do something even more

331
00:11:27.524 --> 00:11:31.491
audacious was to go Maui, Lanai, Molokai, Maui,

332
00:11:31.491 --> 00:11:36.334
to swim the triangle, which is a 30 mile circuit.

333
00:11:36.334 --> 00:11:37.561
But there's no way you could do that,

334
00:11:37.561 --> 00:11:40.243
if you didn't start at 10 o'clock at night,

335
00:11:40.243 --> 00:11:43.065
because you can't be out there during the trade winds,

336
00:11:43.065 --> 00:11:46.876
it's just unswimmable and the boat captain,

337
00:11:46.876 --> 00:11:49.005
who's a great captain said,

338
00:11:49.005 --> 00:11:50.897
there's just too many tiger sharks out here.

339
00:11:50.897 --> 00:11:53.142
And when we put a glow stick on your suit,

340
00:11:53.142 --> 00:11:55.515
which we have to be able to do to be able to see you,

341
00:11:55.515 --> 00:11:56.595
you're just gonna be chum.

342
00:11:56.595 --> 00:11:57.428
Yeah.

343
00:11:57.428 --> 00:11:58.261
So he's like,

344
00:11:58.261 --> 00:12:00.638
I can't let you start before 5:30 in the morning.

345
00:12:00.638 --> 00:12:02.838
And that kind of limited how long I could be out there.

346
00:12:02.838 --> 00:12:05.594
So then I said, okay, well, let's do this double,

347
00:12:05.594 --> 00:12:07.963
which was still a great swim, but it.

348
00:12:07.963 --> 00:12:10.009
I mean, there's pretty regular

349
00:12:10.009 --> 00:12:13.097
shark encounters during the Maui channel swim

350
00:12:13.097 --> 00:12:14.294
that they do every year,

351
00:12:14.294 --> 00:12:15.362
that's primarily like a relay race.

352
00:12:15.362 --> 00:12:16.413
Yep.

353
00:12:16.413 --> 00:12:17.674
That they do in conjunction

354
00:12:17.674 --> 00:12:20.125
with the Waikiki open water swim.

355
00:12:20.125 --> 00:12:21.520
I'm constantly hearing,

356
00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.293
I have tons of friends who've done it.

357
00:12:23.293 --> 00:12:24.250
It's all fine,

358
00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:26.812
but like it's not without its shark problems.

359
00:12:26.812 --> 00:12:31.021
Yeah, now there's more sharks in the Molokai Channel.

360
00:12:31.021 --> 00:12:33.771
So that channel is really sharky.

361
00:12:35.142 --> 00:12:37.046
And I've got a friend who's done that in both directions

362
00:12:37.046 --> 00:12:38.842
getting, Forrest Nelson.

363
00:12:38.842 --> 00:12:39.933
I mean, that's a tough channel.

364
00:12:39.933 --> 00:12:41.766
That's about 26 miles.

365
00:12:42.737 --> 00:12:46.641
And I mean, there's nothing between you and the ocean.

366
00:12:46.641 --> 00:12:48.452
The water's.
Way out there.

367
00:12:48.452 --> 00:12:49.560
Oh yeah, it's incredibly rough.

368
00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:51.215
It's all about the currents.

369
00:12:51.215 --> 00:12:52.048
Yeah.

370
00:12:52.048 --> 00:12:52.899
With that kind of thing.

371
00:12:52.899 --> 00:12:55.326
I just know from doing the Alcatraz swim,

372
00:12:55.326 --> 00:12:57.278
it's all about timing the start.

373
00:12:57.278 --> 00:12:58.133
And you can be,

374
00:12:58.133 --> 00:13:00.149
you wanna be doing your cross right at the slack.

375
00:13:00.149 --> 00:13:01.115
Yeah.

376
00:13:01.115 --> 00:13:03.433
Between the ebb and the flow

377
00:13:03.433 --> 00:13:04.966
and the longer that you're out there,

378
00:13:04.966 --> 00:13:06.734
the more susceptible you are

379
00:13:06.734 --> 00:13:09.092
to the current kind of going out towards the bridge.

380
00:13:09.092 --> 00:13:10.253
Yeah, yeah.

381
00:13:10.253 --> 00:13:12.486
I've done Alcatraz a few times and it's actually,

382
00:13:12.486 --> 00:13:14.354
I think it's a harder swim,

383
00:13:14.354 --> 00:13:16.089
to swim into aquatic park,

384
00:13:16.089 --> 00:13:17.826
even though it's a shorter swim,

385
00:13:17.826 --> 00:13:19.422
because you're really threading the needle

386
00:13:19.422 --> 00:13:21.151
to get in between those two points.

387
00:13:21.151 --> 00:13:21.984
Right, exactly.

388
00:13:21.984 --> 00:13:23.565
Whereas when you go to what's it called Crissy Field?

389
00:13:23.565 --> 00:13:24.398
Yes.

390
00:13:24.398 --> 00:13:26.944
Yeah, that's a longer swim, but it's easy.

391
00:13:26.944 --> 00:13:28.303
'Cause the current starts

392
00:13:28.303 --> 00:13:29.136
to actually push you in that direction.

393
00:13:29.136 --> 00:13:30.303
Push you in that direction, yeah, yeah, yeah.

394
00:13:30.303 --> 00:13:31.254
So you tack straight

395
00:13:31.254 --> 00:13:33.663
and you just naturally gravitate towards

396
00:13:33.663 --> 00:13:36.600
that finish point, interesting.

397
00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:39.208
In addition, a couple Catalina crossings,

398
00:13:39.208 --> 00:13:42.094
but now you don't swim at all.

399
00:13:42.094 --> 00:13:43.371
I don't swim at all 'cause I'm three months

400
00:13:43.371 --> 00:13:44.921
outta shoulder surgery.

401
00:13:44.921 --> 00:13:45.754
So yes, I definitely don't swim at all.

402
00:13:45.754 --> 00:13:46.695
Is that the only reason though?

403
00:13:46.695 --> 00:13:48.063
No, I had stopped swimming,

404
00:13:48.063 --> 00:13:51.216
but I'm gonna get back to it.

405
00:13:51.216 --> 00:13:53.545
I think, I'll never do the, I don't think,

406
00:13:53.545 --> 00:13:54.785
I mean the only condition under

407
00:13:54.785 --> 00:13:56.064
which I would do a marathon again

408
00:13:56.064 --> 00:13:58.989
is if one of my kids wanted to.

409
00:13:58.989 --> 00:14:01.638
When I was in the ocean, last time we were in Hawaii,

410
00:14:01.638 --> 00:14:05.143
I think my son was four and we were,

411
00:14:05.143 --> 00:14:07.755
we were at the sort of Eastern tip, no,

412
00:14:07.755 --> 00:14:09.005
the Western tip

413
00:14:09.927 --> 00:14:11.763
of Maui and you can see Molokai.

414
00:14:11.763 --> 00:14:12.792
Right.

415
00:14:12.792 --> 00:14:13.625
And he was like, what's that?

416
00:14:13.625 --> 00:14:15.972
And I told him and he's like,

417
00:14:15.972 --> 00:14:16.805
he's really interested in that.

418
00:14:16.805 --> 00:14:18.504
And I was like, buddy,

419
00:14:18.504 --> 00:14:20.982
if you ever decide you wanna swim there,

420
00:14:20.982 --> 00:14:24.236
I will come outta retirement and we will do it together.

421
00:14:24.236 --> 00:14:25.488
That could be an incredible bonding experience.

422
00:14:25.488 --> 00:14:26.571
Yeah, yeah.

423
00:14:28.101 --> 00:14:30.057
Well, this is a good kind of way

424
00:14:30.057 --> 00:14:31.663
to segue into one of the main

425
00:14:31.663 --> 00:14:35.177
topics that I want to get into with you,

426
00:14:35.177 --> 00:14:39.804
which is this notion of the Centenarian decathlon.

427
00:14:39.804 --> 00:14:42.671
And I have a very peaked interest in this moment

428
00:14:42.671 --> 00:14:44.770
around all of these ideas

429
00:14:44.770 --> 00:14:47.351
that have become kind of at the forefront

430
00:14:47.351 --> 00:14:50.136
of what you talk about and write about

431
00:14:50.136 --> 00:14:54.027
through my own experience of dealing with lower back pain.

432
00:14:54.027 --> 00:14:57.233
Suddenly, I'm in a position where I can't run without pain.

433
00:14:57.233 --> 00:14:59.961
And even if I'm on the bike for more than a couple hours,

434
00:14:59.961 --> 00:15:01.449
my back becomes intolerable.

435
00:15:01.449 --> 00:15:04.101
I've got an L4 that's kind of out of whack

436
00:15:04.101 --> 00:15:05.289
and sciatic pain.

437
00:15:05.289 --> 00:15:06.622
So I was like, alright, well, I'll swim,

438
00:15:06.622 --> 00:15:07.747
I'll get in the pool.

439
00:15:07.747 --> 00:15:09.914
This should be supportive.

440
00:15:11.023 --> 00:15:14.522
And I signed up for the Key West 20 kilometer swim,

441
00:15:14.522 --> 00:15:15.904
which was just this past weekend,

442
00:15:15.904 --> 00:15:18.162
with the intention of competing in that.

443
00:15:18.162 --> 00:15:21.573
But when the yardage started to stack up

444
00:15:21.573 --> 00:15:23.031
around 6,000 or 7,000

445
00:15:23.031 --> 00:15:25.538
yards per workout, like my back just couldn't deal with it.

446
00:15:25.538 --> 00:15:26.371
And I don't know if that's.

447
00:15:26.371 --> 00:15:28.694
Was that in open water or with flip turns?

448
00:15:28.694 --> 00:15:30.120
Well, it was, yeah, it was flip turns.

449
00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:32.211
So yeah, so I think it was, it's all the turns.

450
00:15:32.211 --> 00:15:33.061
Yeah.

451
00:15:33.061 --> 00:15:36.091
I just couldn't do a flip turn anymore without pain.

452
00:15:36.091 --> 00:15:38.591
And so now I'm sidelined in a way

453
00:15:38.591 --> 00:15:41.809
that I haven't been in the history of my experiences

454
00:15:41.809 --> 00:15:43.633
of being an athlete

455
00:15:43.633 --> 00:15:45.764
and I'm now compelled out of pain

456
00:15:45.764 --> 00:15:48.139
to confront and deal with this

457
00:15:48.139 --> 00:15:51.196
situation in a meaningful way.

458
00:15:51.196 --> 00:15:52.707
And it's been really humbling.

459
00:15:52.707 --> 00:15:56.232
And so as I think I messaged to you like the videos

460
00:15:56.232 --> 00:15:59.121
that you're putting up about mobility

461
00:15:59.121 --> 00:16:01.124
and like glued activation and all of these things,

462
00:16:01.124 --> 00:16:03.560
have been like the most important thing in my life

463
00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:07.223
as I slowly kind of acclimate to a new way

464
00:16:07.223 --> 00:16:11.723
of thinking about fitness through movements, etcetera.

465
00:16:13.149 --> 00:16:15.528
So I think it would be instructive to explain

466
00:16:15.528 --> 00:16:17.745
how this became of interest to you.

467
00:16:17.745 --> 00:16:20.251
And it's obviously, it's part and parcel with your interest

468
00:16:20.251 --> 00:16:24.755
in longevity, but maybe kind of walk us through that.

469
00:16:24.755 --> 00:16:26.412
I mean, there's a story that you probably know,

470
00:16:26.412 --> 00:16:29.603
but I think it might be helpful to just explain

471
00:16:29.603 --> 00:16:32.149
an unfortunate reality of our species,

472
00:16:32.149 --> 00:16:34.780
which is we tend to respond best to pain.

473
00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:35.613
A hundred percent.

474
00:16:35.613 --> 00:16:37.336
I mean, any change I've ever made

475
00:16:37.336 --> 00:16:39.583
is only because I've been forced to out of extreme pain.

476
00:16:39.583 --> 00:16:40.416
Yeah.

477
00:16:40.416 --> 00:16:42.167
So my first brush with that

478
00:16:42.167 --> 00:16:45.315
came in my last year of medical school.

479
00:16:45.315 --> 00:16:49.673
So it's June of 2000 at our Alma mater, right?

480
00:16:49.673 --> 00:16:53.531
So I'm just on my bike riding to the gym

481
00:16:53.531 --> 00:16:55.559
back when it was the Arriaga Center there,

482
00:16:55.559 --> 00:16:56.987
I get off my bike,

483
00:16:56.987 --> 00:16:59.297
getting ready to lock it up and I'm like,

484
00:16:59.297 --> 00:17:01.824
God, something does not feel right in my back,

485
00:17:01.824 --> 00:17:03.907
like a really weird pain.

486
00:17:05.365 --> 00:17:06.813
And so bad in fact that I decided,

487
00:17:06.813 --> 00:17:07.737
you know what?

488
00:17:07.737 --> 00:17:10.350
I'm gonna get back on my bike and ride back

489
00:17:10.350 --> 00:17:11.391
to wherever I live.

490
00:17:11.391 --> 00:17:13.693
I think I lived in Escondido Village or something.

491
00:17:13.693 --> 00:17:15.303
So I do that.

492
00:17:15.303 --> 00:17:16.346
And I'm thinking, you know what, I'm gonna be fine.

493
00:17:16.346 --> 00:17:18.078
I just need to kind of rest today,

494
00:17:18.078 --> 00:17:19.266
which is very unusual for me.

495
00:17:19.266 --> 00:17:21.907
Like I'm not that guy who takes a day off.

496
00:17:21.907 --> 00:17:24.490
Well, the next day I can't actually get out of bed.

497
00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:27.361
So I actually call my roommate, two separate lines.

498
00:17:27.361 --> 00:17:29.747
I'm like, dude, you gotta get me up.

499
00:17:29.747 --> 00:17:31.293
And to make a long story short,

500
00:17:31.293 --> 00:17:35.363
that transitioned into kind of two weeks of debilitating

501
00:17:35.363 --> 00:17:37.780
back pain that ultimately led

502
00:17:38.890 --> 00:17:41.206
to what I didn't realize at the time was a free fragment.

503
00:17:41.206 --> 00:17:44.482
So a fragment of my L5S1 disc broke off,

504
00:17:44.482 --> 00:17:46.181
was about four centimeters long

505
00:17:46.181 --> 00:17:49.546
and it just parked itself on my S1 nerve route.

506
00:17:49.546 --> 00:17:53.619
So now I just had the sensation of skin

507
00:17:53.619 --> 00:17:56.417
being peeled off the bottom of my left foot.

508
00:17:56.417 --> 00:17:59.667
So I kind of sucked this up for a week.

509
00:18:00.896 --> 00:18:02.526
And then finally the Dean of the medical school

510
00:18:02.526 --> 00:18:04.371
saw me limping and he's like, what's going on?

511
00:18:04.371 --> 00:18:07.200
And I told him, and he said, we gotta get you an MRI now.

512
00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:09.312
He took me, got an MRI.

513
00:18:09.312 --> 00:18:11.799
The next morning I was in surgery

514
00:18:11.799 --> 00:18:13.778
and it was just, everything went wrong.

515
00:18:13.778 --> 00:18:15.171
So it was a botched operation.

516
00:18:15.171 --> 00:18:16.855
The guy operated on the wrong side,

517
00:18:16.855 --> 00:18:19.070
I came out with a foot drop on the right leg.

518
00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:19.903
So my right leg.

519
00:18:19.903 --> 00:18:20.828
At Stanford hospital?

520
00:18:20.828 --> 00:18:21.661
Yeah.
Wow.

521
00:18:21.661 --> 00:18:24.664
So my right foot had a drop.

522
00:18:24.664 --> 00:18:28.334
So now I couldn't walk 'cause I'm dragging the right foot.

523
00:18:28.334 --> 00:18:30.815
This turned into an unbelievably complicated story

524
00:18:30.815 --> 00:18:32.917
that I won't bore everybody with,

525
00:18:32.917 --> 00:18:35.494
but the punchline is I needed multiple trips

526
00:18:35.494 --> 00:18:39.411
back to the operating room to fix this problem.

527
00:18:40.690 --> 00:18:42.867
And I was probably three months

528
00:18:42.867 --> 00:18:44.272
of only able to lay on a floor.

529
00:18:44.272 --> 00:18:45.105
Wow.

530
00:18:45.105 --> 00:18:46.716
Or bed, like those are my only two positions.

531
00:18:46.716 --> 00:18:48.328
Mom had to fly down to take care of me,

532
00:18:48.328 --> 00:18:50.997
to feed me, didn't lay down.

533
00:18:50.997 --> 00:18:54.438
Went from I'm not sure I'll be able to be a surgeon,

534
00:18:54.438 --> 00:18:56.339
I'm not sure I'll graduate on time,

535
00:18:56.339 --> 00:18:57.680
to I'm not sure I'll ever walk again.

536
00:18:57.680 --> 00:18:58.704
Wow.

537
00:18:58.704 --> 00:19:00.584
And then an amazing doctor there,

538
00:19:00.584 --> 00:19:01.507
actually a guy who I'd like

539
00:19:01.507 --> 00:19:04.166
to have on my podcast at some point, his name's Sean Mackey,

540
00:19:04.166 --> 00:19:05.286
he's a pain specialist.

541
00:19:05.286 --> 00:19:08.644
He was kind of the guy that broke this cycle

542
00:19:08.644 --> 00:19:10.757
and began the treatments that were,

543
00:19:10.757 --> 00:19:14.828
that got me outta pain enough to then do the rehab.

544
00:19:14.828 --> 00:19:17.670
And amazingly within a year of that incident,

545
00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:21.165
I was at Hopkins standing on my feet, operating,

546
00:19:21.165 --> 00:19:23.082
as a surgical resident.

547
00:19:24.188 --> 00:19:26.163
But that was like kind of an aha moment.

548
00:19:26.163 --> 00:19:28.618
And that's also what led to me getting into swimming.

549
00:19:28.618 --> 00:19:30.652
So three years later when I'm, so that was,

550
00:19:30.652 --> 00:19:31.932
I was 28 when that happened.

551
00:19:31.932 --> 00:19:34.729
So by the age of 31 I decided,

552
00:19:34.729 --> 00:19:35.941
I'm gonna mix things up a little bit

553
00:19:35.941 --> 00:19:39.375
and try this other thing, swimming.

554
00:19:39.375 --> 00:19:41.031
So that experience stuck with me.

555
00:19:41.031 --> 00:19:43.122
And I do think of that as kind of the best,

556
00:19:43.122 --> 00:19:44.193
worst experience of my life,

557
00:19:44.193 --> 00:19:48.089
'cause it gave me a really close up view

558
00:19:48.089 --> 00:19:51.193
of what it means to lose your body.

559
00:19:51.193 --> 00:19:52.989
And I think most people,

560
00:19:52.989 --> 00:19:54.907
unfortunately don't figure that out,

561
00:19:54.907 --> 00:19:58.048
until it's too late to do anything about it.

562
00:19:58.048 --> 00:20:00.364
And I got a second chance, right?

563
00:20:00.364 --> 00:20:03.565
Which is not to say that at that moment I conceived

564
00:20:03.565 --> 00:20:05.718
of what I'm doing today, I certainly didn't.

565
00:20:05.718 --> 00:20:07.635
I would spend the next,

566
00:20:08.964 --> 00:20:13.345
18 years pursuing other performance metrics,

567
00:20:13.345 --> 00:20:16.538
but it's never left my mind and it's now,

568
00:20:16.538 --> 00:20:17.604
completely shaped how I think

569
00:20:17.604 --> 00:20:20.791
about taking care of patients and myself.

570
00:20:20.791 --> 00:20:23.277
What's unique and interesting about you

571
00:20:23.277 --> 00:20:25.099
and the work that you do,

572
00:20:25.099 --> 00:20:28.217
is that you're perhaps the only person in the world

573
00:20:28.217 --> 00:20:31.009
or at least the only person that I can think of,

574
00:20:31.009 --> 00:20:32.626
that has such an in depth level

575
00:20:32.626 --> 00:20:36.109
of expertise in both performance,

576
00:20:36.109 --> 00:20:39.276
athleticism and kind of the mechanisms

577
00:20:40.311 --> 00:20:44.604
behind how to optimize that and longevity,

578
00:20:44.604 --> 00:20:46.871
like how to promote that effectively

579
00:20:46.871 --> 00:20:50.609
through evidence based medicine and science.

580
00:20:50.609 --> 00:20:53.653
And it seems like these two worlds are orthogonal to each

581
00:20:53.653 --> 00:20:56.495
other and you've seem to find a way

582
00:20:56.495 --> 00:20:59.122
to kind of synthesize the best of those.

583
00:20:59.122 --> 00:21:01.685
So how do these two worlds intersect

584
00:21:01.685 --> 00:21:03.227
and maybe that's a way

585
00:21:03.227 --> 00:21:05.563
to kind of segue into your

586
00:21:05.563 --> 00:21:08.883
kind of real interest and fascination and longevity.

587
00:21:08.883 --> 00:21:11.186
I think you're astute to pick up on that, Rich,

588
00:21:11.186 --> 00:21:12.976
not the part of my expertise,

589
00:21:12.976 --> 00:21:14.295
'cause I don't think I actually have much expertise

590
00:21:14.295 --> 00:21:15.804
on the performance side,

591
00:21:15.804 --> 00:21:17.959
but I think you're astute to pick up on the notion

592
00:21:17.959 --> 00:21:20.835
that these are often at odds

593
00:21:20.835 --> 00:21:23.192
and it's for that reason that I get contacted

594
00:21:23.192 --> 00:21:25.550
a lot by professional athletes

595
00:21:25.550 --> 00:21:26.694
and by professional sports teams,

596
00:21:26.694 --> 00:21:28.093
probably at least once a month,

597
00:21:28.093 --> 00:21:32.836
you know, coach of an NBA team or something or performance,

598
00:21:32.836 --> 00:21:36.275
some high end performance entity reaches out

599
00:21:36.275 --> 00:21:38.818
and outside of the physiologic sports

600
00:21:38.818 --> 00:21:40.246
like swimming, running, cycling,

601
00:21:40.246 --> 00:21:42.604
where I think I do actually have a pretty good sense

602
00:21:42.604 --> 00:21:44.126
of the physi, especially cycling.

603
00:21:44.126 --> 00:21:45.202
That's probably the one

604
00:21:45.202 --> 00:21:48.031
that has the least technique and the most pure physiology.

605
00:21:48.031 --> 00:21:51.630
That's probably where I would have the most insight,

606
00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:54.470
but what it takes to be exceptional there

607
00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:56.634
and what it takes to live a really long life,

608
00:21:56.634 --> 00:21:59.483
I wouldn't say they're completely orthogonal,

609
00:21:59.483 --> 00:22:00.642
but they're not.

610
00:22:00.642 --> 00:22:01.743
They're certainly nowhere near colinear.

611
00:22:01.743 --> 00:22:02.576
Hm hm.

612
00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:06.554
So for that reason, I just say, look,

613
00:22:06.554 --> 00:22:08.543
I'm not the guy to help you achieve those goals.

614
00:22:08.543 --> 00:22:10.792
I'm probably the guy to help you when you retire

615
00:22:10.792 --> 00:22:12.907
and you wanna work towards a different goal.

616
00:22:12.907 --> 00:22:14.776
I would assume though,

617
00:22:14.776 --> 00:22:17.870
that in the Venn diagram between those two worlds,

618
00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:20.219
that the intersection has to do

619
00:22:20.219 --> 00:22:22.133
with promoting kind of the longevity

620
00:22:22.133 --> 00:22:23.631
of one's career, right?

621
00:22:23.631 --> 00:22:26.056
There are certain things that you can do

622
00:22:26.056 --> 00:22:28.175
as a professional athlete, chasing greatness.

623
00:22:28.175 --> 00:22:29.113
Absolutely.

624
00:22:29.113 --> 00:22:30.303
That can extend the link.

625
00:22:30.303 --> 00:22:32.480
I'm sure like a lot of the people that reach out to you

626
00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:35.187
are in the kind of twilight of their career.

627
00:22:35.187 --> 00:22:36.583
And it's like, how can I eek out

628
00:22:36.583 --> 00:22:38.072
a couple more years doing this while now

629
00:22:38.072 --> 00:22:41.696
we have to open ourselves up to these other protocols.

630
00:22:41.696 --> 00:22:42.818
That's absolutely correct.

631
00:22:42.818 --> 00:22:43.700
And I think,

632
00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:45.730
I have a friend who works most,

633
00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:47.702
exclusively with kind of NFL guys

634
00:22:47.702 --> 00:22:50.737
and his take is from the day you sign

635
00:22:50.737 --> 00:22:53.192
your first contract after the NFL combine,

636
00:22:53.192 --> 00:22:55.508
after you've shown everybody how fast you can run a 40

637
00:22:55.508 --> 00:22:57.861
and how much you can bench press and all that stuff.

638
00:22:57.861 --> 00:23:00.688
Like you stop caring about those metrics forever.

639
00:23:00.688 --> 00:23:04.626
The only metric now that matters is not getting injured.

640
00:23:04.626 --> 00:23:06.467
That's effectively going to determine

641
00:23:06.467 --> 00:23:08.385
your lifetime earnings.

642
00:23:08.385 --> 00:23:11.862
And so it's not really about how much faster can you get,

643
00:23:11.862 --> 00:23:13.995
it's how much safer can you continue to run fast.

644
00:23:13.995 --> 00:23:14.828
Right.

645
00:23:14.828 --> 00:23:16.635
Even if it means running a little bit less.

646
00:23:16.635 --> 00:23:19.124
So, you're absolutely correct.

647
00:23:19.124 --> 00:23:22.150
And in that sense, look, again,

648
00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:26.064
I don't think I'm particularly insightful

649
00:23:26.064 --> 00:23:28.303
or brilliant on those.

650
00:23:28.303 --> 00:23:29.772
I think my insight is how you combine

651
00:23:29.772 --> 00:23:31.096
all of these things, right?

652
00:23:31.096 --> 00:23:34.513
It's how do you think about aerobic base,

653
00:23:35.392 --> 00:23:38.128
anaerobic peak, stability, strength

654
00:23:38.128 --> 00:23:39.730
and how do those things

655
00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:41.480
matter when you're 90

656
00:23:42.668 --> 00:23:43.846
and what what's the standard

657
00:23:43.846 --> 00:23:45.158
we should hold ourselves to?

658
00:23:45.158 --> 00:23:47.637
What's a realistic aspiration

659
00:23:47.637 --> 00:23:49.227
for someone in their marginal decade,

660
00:23:49.227 --> 00:23:52.223
which is the way we talk about the last decade of your life.

661
00:23:52.223 --> 00:23:55.216
And the way that you kind of construct an architecture

662
00:23:55.216 --> 00:23:57.470
around how to think and approach this,

663
00:23:57.470 --> 00:23:58.303
is by working backwards.

664
00:23:58.303 --> 00:23:59.136
Yeah.

665
00:23:59.136 --> 00:24:01.251
From a perspective of what your values

666
00:24:01.251 --> 00:24:02.333
and your goals are

667
00:24:02.333 --> 00:24:05.482
or what you anticipate you're gonna wanna be able to do

668
00:24:05.482 --> 00:24:08.561
when you're 90 or 80 or 70.

669
00:24:08.561 --> 00:24:12.125
And then working back from there to start doing the things

670
00:24:12.125 --> 00:24:14.733
that will put you in a position

671
00:24:14.733 --> 00:24:16.658
to kind of have that capacity.

672
00:24:16.658 --> 00:24:17.491
Exactly.

673
00:24:20.403 --> 00:24:22.914
We would call it forecasting if you say where you are today.

674
00:24:22.914 --> 00:24:27.105
So if you say, okay, I'm 50 today and when I'm 55,

675
00:24:27.105 --> 00:24:29.689
I want to be able to do this.

676
00:24:29.689 --> 00:24:34.171
That works reasonably well for short term goals,

677
00:24:34.171 --> 00:24:35.661
but for really complicated problems,

678
00:24:35.661 --> 00:24:38.215
it seems easier to reverse engineer them.

679
00:24:38.215 --> 00:24:40.588
And I've borrowed the term backcasting from Annie Duke,

680
00:24:40.588 --> 00:24:41.798
'cause I think it's just a better way

681
00:24:41.798 --> 00:24:44.764
to contrast forecasting and backcasting.

682
00:24:44.764 --> 00:24:48.856
And I just don't see how you can forecast 40 years.

683
00:24:48.856 --> 00:24:50.099
It just becomes too vague.

684
00:24:50.099 --> 00:24:52.543
You have to anchor to what you're talking about

685
00:24:52.543 --> 00:24:53.626
40 years out.

686
00:24:55.288 --> 00:24:58.128
And for me that is the sort of defining principle

687
00:24:58.128 --> 00:24:59.167
of health span, right?

688
00:24:59.167 --> 00:25:00.641
Is sort of understanding cognitively,

689
00:25:00.641 --> 00:25:02.914
physically and emotionally what you want in the last decade

690
00:25:02.914 --> 00:25:05.537
of your life and making sure

691
00:25:05.537 --> 00:25:06.591
that you work backwards from there.

692
00:25:06.591 --> 00:25:08.444
And again, it literally feeds into everything.

693
00:25:08.444 --> 00:25:10.137
Like we'll use an emotional example

694
00:25:10.137 --> 00:25:12.312
'cause people don't think about that.

695
00:25:12.312 --> 00:25:14.537
If your vision of the last decade of your life

696
00:25:14.537 --> 00:25:17.290
is to be surrounded by family

697
00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:19.933
who love you and wanna be around you.

698
00:25:19.933 --> 00:25:23.747
But 40 years earlier, you have no relationships.

699
00:25:23.747 --> 00:25:26.471
Your kids hate you because you're a jerk

700
00:25:26.471 --> 00:25:28.416
and you're working all the time.

701
00:25:28.416 --> 00:25:29.563
There's a disconnect.

702
00:25:29.563 --> 00:25:31.557
Like why do you really think your kids

703
00:25:31.557 --> 00:25:33.244
are gonna be around you in 40 years?

704
00:25:33.244 --> 00:25:34.898
If they don't wanna be around you right now?

705
00:25:34.898 --> 00:25:35.923
Right.

706
00:25:35.923 --> 00:25:38.614
So every one of those things has to have a congruency

707
00:25:38.614 --> 00:25:39.447
that comes back.

708
00:25:39.447 --> 00:25:40.304
Now with the emotional stuff,

709
00:25:40.304 --> 00:25:42.597
it's not as metric driven,

710
00:25:42.597 --> 00:25:43.694
but when it comes to the physical stuff,

711
00:25:43.694 --> 00:25:45.709
it's very metric driven

712
00:25:45.709 --> 00:25:48.158
because we know exactly what these things mean.

713
00:25:48.158 --> 00:25:51.961
If someone lays out an aspiration, you can say,

714
00:25:51.961 --> 00:25:54.904
well, that will require a VO2 of this many mills per minute,

715
00:25:54.904 --> 00:25:55.987
per kilogram.

716
00:25:57.028 --> 00:25:59.085
You have to be able,

717
00:25:59.085 --> 00:26:00.178
your muscles have to be able to consume

718
00:26:00.178 --> 00:26:01.943
this much oxygen per minute.

719
00:26:01.943 --> 00:26:03.695
If you actually tell me that you wanna be able to hike up

720
00:26:03.695 --> 00:26:07.249
that canyon in that many minutes,

721
00:26:07.249 --> 00:26:09.894
if you tell me that you wanna be able to stand up

722
00:26:09.894 --> 00:26:13.010
and do X, Y, and Z, it will require this much strength.

723
00:26:13.010 --> 00:26:16.141
And we know the rate at which those things decline

724
00:26:16.141 --> 00:26:17.651
and therefore we project back.

725
00:26:17.651 --> 00:26:19.060
Right.

726
00:26:19.060 --> 00:26:20.591
So typically somebody's gonna say,

727
00:26:20.591 --> 00:26:22.740
I want to be able to play with my grandkids.

728
00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:24.740
I want to be able to go on a hike.

729
00:26:24.740 --> 00:26:27.589
I want to be able to get up off the ground

730
00:26:27.589 --> 00:26:31.336
or get up off a chair and get up out of bed.

731
00:26:31.336 --> 00:26:33.420
Typical type of stuff.

732
00:26:33.420 --> 00:26:36.037
So how do you think about,

733
00:26:36.037 --> 00:26:38.434
or basically what are the pillars of this?

734
00:26:38.434 --> 00:26:40.948
I know you have buckets in how do you think about this?

735
00:26:40.948 --> 00:26:43.015
What are those?

736
00:26:43.015 --> 00:26:44.256
And then from there,

737
00:26:44.256 --> 00:26:45.860
what are the things that

738
00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:47.560
somebody of my age or just

739
00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:50.410
kind of in the middle age sector of life,

740
00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:51.928
what should we be thinking about

741
00:26:51.928 --> 00:26:53.462
and what are some of the practices

742
00:26:53.462 --> 00:26:55.187
that we should be starting to adopt?

743
00:26:55.187 --> 00:26:57.115
I mean, we would start by being

744
00:26:57.115 --> 00:26:59.100
more specific in the marginal decade.

745
00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:01.242
So the one thing we don't know is when your marginal decade

746
00:27:01.242 --> 00:27:02.533
is going to be.

747
00:27:02.533 --> 00:27:03.835
So I don't, you know, I don't,

748
00:27:03.835 --> 00:27:06.842
I can't tell looking at somebody, is it gonna be 80 to 90?

749
00:27:06.842 --> 00:27:08.700
Is it gonna be 74 to 84?

750
00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:10.542
Is it gonna be 89 to 99?

751
00:27:10.542 --> 00:27:11.675
We don't know when that is.

752
00:27:11.675 --> 00:27:13.579
And that does play a role,

753
00:27:13.579 --> 00:27:16.370
but I think it's worth budgeting for it being later,

754
00:27:16.370 --> 00:27:17.906
because that will force you to hold yourself

755
00:27:17.906 --> 00:27:19.453
to a higher standard,

756
00:27:19.453 --> 00:27:21.822
but we begin with much more specificity.

757
00:27:21.822 --> 00:27:25.376
So I think we have a little over 50 questions

758
00:27:25.376 --> 00:27:30.043
we ask our patients and this is absurdly specific stuff.

759
00:27:31.648 --> 00:27:33.417
And some of it is like things you wouldn't think about,

760
00:27:33.417 --> 00:27:36.178
like do you wanna be able to have sex, right?

761
00:27:36.178 --> 00:27:37.957
Because if the answer is yes,

762
00:27:37.957 --> 00:27:39.936
like there is a certain level of fitness,

763
00:27:39.936 --> 00:27:42.727
mobility and strength that's required to do that.

764
00:27:42.727 --> 00:27:45.496
Do you want to be able to walk up

765
00:27:45.496 --> 00:27:47.805
this many flights of stairs?

766
00:27:47.805 --> 00:27:48.972
Do you want to be able to carry

767
00:27:48.972 --> 00:27:50.172
this many pounds of groceries?

768
00:27:50.172 --> 00:27:51.843
Do you want to be able to place something

769
00:27:51.843 --> 00:27:53.839
of this weight above your head?

770
00:27:53.839 --> 00:27:55.022
Do you want to be able to pick up

771
00:27:55.022 --> 00:27:58.272
a child of this weight from the ground?

772
00:27:59.513 --> 00:28:02.434
And we just sort of force people to go through that exercise

773
00:28:02.434 --> 00:28:05.184
and you check off the things that don't matter to you

774
00:28:05.184 --> 00:28:08.114
and check off the things that do.

775
00:28:08.114 --> 00:28:13.114
And what that does is it turns into basically a consolidated

776
00:28:13.148 --> 00:28:16.398
set of metrics for strength and fitness

777
00:28:17.303 --> 00:28:19.133
and other things that are a bit foreign to people

778
00:28:19.133 --> 00:28:20.966
like scapular control.

779
00:28:22.364 --> 00:28:24.600
Lower leg variability.

780
00:28:24.600 --> 00:28:26.888
A big part of what troubles people

781
00:28:26.888 --> 00:28:29.794
when they get older and it gets mistaken a bit for balance,

782
00:28:29.794 --> 00:28:31.842
but a lot of it has to do with not just balance,

783
00:28:31.842 --> 00:28:35.465
but with lower leg variability and you know,

784
00:28:35.465 --> 00:28:38.736
not being able to kind of feel with your feet very well.

785
00:28:38.736 --> 00:28:39.929
Right.

786
00:28:39.929 --> 00:28:40.960
And so falls as an example,

787
00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:44.861
become an enormous cause of morbidity.

788
00:28:44.861 --> 00:28:46.111
I've quoted the stat many times,

789
00:28:46.111 --> 00:28:50.551
but if you're over 65 and you fall and break your hip,

790
00:28:50.551 --> 00:28:52.462
your 12 month mortality,

791
00:28:52.462 --> 00:28:53.898
meaning the probability you'll be dead

792
00:28:53.898 --> 00:28:55.187
within the next 12 months,

793
00:28:55.187 --> 00:28:57.259
depending on the study is 30% to 40%.

794
00:28:57.259 --> 00:28:58.326
Right.

795
00:28:58.326 --> 00:29:00.747
So just starting from that as a baseline,

