which if I get there is great,

1051
00:39:07.315 --> 00:39:08.398
which is I'm gonna be able to get up

1052
00:39:08.398 --> 00:39:09.551
without using my hands still.

1053
00:39:09.551 --> 00:39:10.384
Yeah.

1054
00:39:10.384 --> 00:39:11.622
Which I can do now, obviously,

1055
00:39:11.622 --> 00:39:13.562
but even if I can just do it on one point of support,

1056
00:39:13.562 --> 00:39:15.562
that would be fantastic.

1057
00:39:16.953 --> 00:39:18.400
I love archery.

1058
00:39:18.400 --> 00:39:20.949
It is such a big part of my life.

1059
00:39:20.949 --> 00:39:23.547
And even though now I'm pulling a 75 pound bow,

1060
00:39:23.547 --> 00:39:26.129
I think to be able to pull a 50 pound bow,

1061
00:39:26.129 --> 00:39:30.224
in my marginal decade would be fantastic.

1062
00:39:30.224 --> 00:39:32.127
I love driving race cars.

1063
00:39:32.127 --> 00:39:33.456
Now, Paul Newman drove a race car

1064
00:39:33.456 --> 00:39:35.692
until probably a year or two before the end of his life.

1065
00:39:35.692 --> 00:39:36.525
Hm hm.

1066
00:39:36.525 --> 00:39:37.820
And so for me,

1067
00:39:37.820 --> 00:39:39.535
that's something that I would love to be able

1068
00:39:39.535 --> 00:39:41.101
to continue to do.

1069
00:39:41.101 --> 00:39:43.278
And that requires not just the strength

1070
00:39:43.278 --> 00:39:44.965
and the coordination and the mental acuity to do it.

1071
00:39:44.965 --> 00:39:47.364
You have to be able to tolerate insanely hot temperatures.

1072
00:39:47.364 --> 00:39:49.963
It gets so hot inside of a race car

1073
00:39:49.963 --> 00:39:51.399
that you have to kind of have

1074
00:39:51.399 --> 00:39:52.615
the kind of cardiovascular fitness to tolerate that.

1075
00:39:52.615 --> 00:39:54.014
Right.

1076
00:39:54.014 --> 00:39:56.528
But having clarity about what those things

1077
00:39:56.528 --> 00:39:59.237
are allows you to then backcast

1078
00:39:59.237 --> 00:40:01.829
to now and start to build the foundation

1079
00:40:01.829 --> 00:40:03.177
for that capability.

1080
00:40:03.177 --> 00:40:04.687
Yeah, like we were talking about earlier.

1081
00:40:04.687 --> 00:40:06.689
I mean, that's a big part of why I do what you do,

1082
00:40:06.689 --> 00:40:08.936
which is I do some of my activity.

1083
00:40:08.936 --> 00:40:11.602
Like I rock four or five days a week,

1084
00:40:11.602 --> 00:40:13.042
carrying a weighted backpack,

1085
00:40:13.042 --> 00:40:15.575
but I always do it at like five or 6:00 p.m.

1086
00:40:15.575 --> 00:40:17.597
So in Austin, that's about the hottest time of day.

1087
00:40:17.597 --> 00:40:18.563
Right.

1088
00:40:18.563 --> 00:40:21.019
So it's a hundred degrees.

1089
00:40:21.019 --> 00:40:23.186
Sometimes it's a 105, 106,

1090
00:40:24.073 --> 00:40:25.981
and you got a 60 pound pack on your back

1091
00:40:25.981 --> 00:40:28.477
and you go walk three miles in the hills,

1092
00:40:28.477 --> 00:40:30.236
your ability to tolerate heat.

1093
00:40:30.236 --> 00:40:33.170
You go to somebody's barbecue at 11 o'clock when it's 90

1094
00:40:33.170 --> 00:40:34.003
and everybody's dying.

1095
00:40:34.003 --> 00:40:34.836
You're like, this is nothing.

1096
00:40:34.836 --> 00:40:36.004
Right.

1097
00:40:36.004 --> 00:40:37.286
Yeah, you can acclimate to that.

1098
00:40:37.286 --> 00:40:38.185
Yeah.

1099
00:40:38.185 --> 00:40:42.428
You just have to engage with it and confront it.

1100
00:40:42.428 --> 00:40:45.827
One of the things that you've talked about quite a bit

1101
00:40:45.827 --> 00:40:49.754
is noticing how natural it is for your children

1102
00:40:49.754 --> 00:40:51.235
to do all of these movements

1103
00:40:51.235 --> 00:40:54.390
that then become so difficult for us

1104
00:40:54.390 --> 00:40:56.177
as adults because we've unlearned them.

1105
00:40:56.177 --> 00:41:00.468
And our sedentary lifestyles kind of detach us from

1106
00:41:00.468 --> 00:41:02.009
our kind of natural blueprint

1107
00:41:02.009 --> 00:41:04.417
and ability to do those things.

1108
00:41:04.417 --> 00:41:06.027
So how can we,

1109
00:41:06.027 --> 00:41:08.173
as most people listening or watching this,

1110
00:41:08.173 --> 00:41:09.813
probably have to sit in a chair

1111
00:41:09.813 --> 00:41:12.693
for their work and we're sitting in chairs right now.

1112
00:41:12.693 --> 00:41:14.969
What are some of the practices

1113
00:41:14.969 --> 00:41:18.069
that could be adopted to help keep us

1114
00:41:18.069 --> 00:41:20.797
connected to that natural ability

1115
00:41:20.797 --> 00:41:23.163
that is kind of innate in our unconscious.

1116
00:41:23.163 --> 00:41:24.875
I mean, for me, I've taken a very deliberate

1117
00:41:24.875 --> 00:41:26.342
approach to that.

1118
00:41:26.342 --> 00:41:28.463
So four or five years ago when I got introduced

1119
00:41:28.463 --> 00:41:30.900
to this pattern of movement

1120
00:41:30.900 --> 00:41:32.229
called dynamic neuromuscular stable.

1121
00:41:32.229 --> 00:41:33.302
Yeah, talk about that.

1122
00:41:33.302 --> 00:41:34.201
Super interesting.

1123
00:41:34.201 --> 00:41:37.457
Yeah, so it kind of grew out of the Czech school.

1124
00:41:37.457 --> 00:41:39.596
So, basically God,

1125
00:41:39.596 --> 00:41:41.002
I wish I knew the history better.

1126
00:41:41.002 --> 00:41:42.392
I've forgotten a lot of it.

1127
00:41:42.392 --> 00:41:44.454
About a hundred years ago,

1128
00:41:44.454 --> 00:41:48.315
a Czech neurologist was trying to understand

1129
00:41:48.315 --> 00:41:50.927
how kids with cerebral palsy

1130
00:41:50.927 --> 00:41:54.260
had such difficult times with movements.

1131
00:41:55.521 --> 00:41:57.472
And he basically formulated a hypothesis,

1132
00:41:57.472 --> 00:42:00.832
which was during the first couple of years of their life,

1133
00:42:00.832 --> 00:42:01.974
they were missing out

1134
00:42:01.974 --> 00:42:04.288
on some developmental milestones of movement.

1135
00:42:04.288 --> 00:42:06.725
And when they didn't get those patterns,

1136
00:42:06.725 --> 00:42:09.136
those neuromuscular patterns weren't developed,

1137
00:42:09.136 --> 00:42:11.260
they could never,

1138
00:42:11.260 --> 00:42:13.709
they became hosed movement wise.

1139
00:42:13.709 --> 00:42:15.190
So he said, well,

1140
00:42:15.190 --> 00:42:16.980
if we can identify what those are

1141
00:42:16.980 --> 00:42:19.243
by looking at kids that don't have CP,

1142
00:42:19.243 --> 00:42:22.561
can we teach them to the older kids with CP?

1143
00:42:22.561 --> 00:42:25.740
And the answer turned out to be, yes, you could.

1144
00:42:25.740 --> 00:42:28.009
So that became the Prague School and that's what they were

1145
00:42:28.009 --> 00:42:31.589
primarily doing and then another guy came along,

1146
00:42:31.589 --> 00:42:32.422
I'm blanking on his name.

1147
00:42:32.422 --> 00:42:33.851
It might have been Vojta

1148
00:42:33.851 --> 00:42:35.538
or Vojta might have been the original guy.

1149
00:42:35.538 --> 00:42:36.775
I can't recall.

1150
00:42:36.775 --> 00:42:38.024
Who basically said, wait a minute,

1151
00:42:38.024 --> 00:42:41.450
this is actually a tool that would be really valuable

1152
00:42:41.450 --> 00:42:44.117
for prehab and rehab for adults.

1153
00:42:46.267 --> 00:42:48.817
And so that kind of morphed the Prague School

1154
00:42:48.817 --> 00:42:49.878
into this field of dynamic

1155
00:42:49.878 --> 00:42:51.366
neuromuscular stabilization,

1156
00:42:51.366 --> 00:42:53.948
which we abbreviate DNS and it's still based

1157
00:42:53.948 --> 00:42:56.810
in the Czech Republic or in Czechoslovakia, I guess,

1158
00:42:56.810 --> 00:42:58.810
whatever we call it now.

1159
00:43:00.411 --> 00:43:01.949
And having now been connected

1160
00:43:01.949 --> 00:43:04.001
to a number of these practitioners,

1161
00:43:04.001 --> 00:43:05.987
I have found this to be

1162
00:43:05.987 --> 00:43:09.249
one of the most important systems that I've incorporated.

1163
00:43:09.249 --> 00:43:10.913
So initially my exposure to it

1164
00:43:10.913 --> 00:43:13.168
was basically to fix problems, right?

1165
00:43:13.168 --> 00:43:16.360
I had two nagging issues four or five years ago.

1166
00:43:16.360 --> 00:43:18.326
One was this elbow issue, which again,

1167
00:43:18.326 --> 00:43:20.410
I couldn't understand what was causing it,

1168
00:43:20.410 --> 00:43:23.224
until I realized it was inability

1169
00:43:23.224 --> 00:43:25.385
to localize my and control my scapula.

1170
00:43:25.385 --> 00:43:28.714
And the other was just my right SI joint nagged.

1171
00:43:28.714 --> 00:43:31.106
It just had this little nagging pain in my right SI joint.

1172
00:43:31.106 --> 00:43:33.727
Certainly didn't ever raise to the level

1173
00:43:33.727 --> 00:43:36.981
of lower back pain, no sciatica, nothing like that.

1174
00:43:36.981 --> 00:43:40.503
But whenever I deadlifted really, really heavy,

1175
00:43:40.503 --> 00:43:42.597
50% of those days,

1176
00:43:42.597 --> 00:43:46.222
I would be punished with some right SI joint pain.

1177
00:43:46.222 --> 00:43:47.704
And again, these two things seemed

1178
00:43:47.704 --> 00:43:48.815
completely unrelated until I got

1179
00:43:48.815 --> 00:43:49.773
under the hood of what was going on

1180
00:43:49.773 --> 00:43:51.085
with my movement patterns.

1181
00:43:51.085 --> 00:43:53.025
And I realized, like every,

1182
00:43:53.025 --> 00:43:56.282
whatever I was, 45 year old, I didn't know how to move.

1183
00:43:56.282 --> 00:43:59.406
So what started out as kind of rehab

1184
00:43:59.406 --> 00:44:03.376
has then just morphed into life movements.

1185
00:44:03.376 --> 00:44:07.709
So I do an hour a week in person with a DNS teacher.

1186
00:44:09.268 --> 00:44:11.709
So guy named Kyle Brown comes to my house once a week.

1187
00:44:11.709 --> 00:44:15.337
We just hammer DNS movements together

1188
00:44:15.337 --> 00:44:16.578
and then I'll spend 10

1189
00:44:16.578 --> 00:44:19.638
minutes every day doing them on my own.

1190
00:44:19.638 --> 00:44:22.878
And that has been kind of life changing.

1191
00:44:22.878 --> 00:44:25.627
Now, of course, with my shoulder injury,

1192
00:44:25.627 --> 00:44:27.928
Kyler is overseeing the rehab there.

1193
00:44:27.928 --> 00:44:31.782
And so we're now incorporating really good shoulder rehab

1194
00:44:31.782 --> 00:44:34.697
coupled with the DNS principles

1195
00:44:34.697 --> 00:44:36.388
of getting my scapula to relearn how to move.

1196
00:44:36.388 --> 00:44:37.919
Because when you have this type

1197
00:44:37.919 --> 00:44:40.836
of an injury and you have surgery,

1198
00:44:42.335 --> 00:44:45.082
the brain really wants to shut off how this thing works.

1199
00:44:45.082 --> 00:44:46.517
So now we're just gonna basically reprogramming it.

1200
00:44:46.517 --> 00:44:51.425
Right, so what is an example of a DNS movement.

1201
00:44:51.425 --> 00:44:53.045
The first one and the core one.

1202
00:44:53.045 --> 00:44:53.878
I'm trying to understand.

1203
00:44:53.878 --> 00:44:54.872
Yeah, yeah, yeah, visualize it.

1204
00:44:54.872 --> 00:44:55.814
The very first one is something

1205
00:44:55.814 --> 00:45:00.682
called learning how to access intra abdominal pressure.

1206
00:45:00.682 --> 00:45:04.180
So I think most people who think about breathing,

1207
00:45:04.180 --> 00:45:06.395
have a pretty good sense that you

1208
00:45:06.395 --> 00:45:08.653
aren't just breathing into your thoracic cavity, right?

1209
00:45:08.653 --> 00:45:11.076
A good breath has to have the diaphragm going down.

1210
00:45:11.076 --> 00:45:12.746
You have to get into the abdomen.

1211
00:45:12.746 --> 00:45:15.333
What DNS does is it really explains to you

1212
00:45:15.333 --> 00:45:18.514
how a child is breathing, which is,

1213
00:45:18.514 --> 00:45:22.169
if you imagine the waistband of your pants or your shorts,

1214
00:45:22.169 --> 00:45:26.333
when a child is breathing, that thing is fully expanding.

1215
00:45:26.333 --> 00:45:30.965
So they have purely concentric force in their abdomen

1216
00:45:30.965 --> 00:45:32.103
with every breath.

1217
00:45:32.103 --> 00:45:33.382
And again, if you look at a really good

1218
00:45:33.382 --> 00:45:34.457
power lifter or something,

1219
00:45:34.457 --> 00:45:35.630
you'll see the same thing.

1220
00:45:35.630 --> 00:45:38.372
I mean the amount of intra abdominal pressure

1221
00:45:38.372 --> 00:45:40.029
that they have to generate to be able

1222
00:45:40.029 --> 00:45:43.861
to move such heavy loads, is essential.

1223
00:45:43.861 --> 00:45:47.210
And so that was literally the very first thing we worked on

1224
00:45:47.210 --> 00:45:51.506
is just, can you access pelvic floor pressure?

1225
00:45:51.506 --> 00:45:54.241
Can you create enough pressure into your pelvic floor?

1226
00:45:54.241 --> 00:45:56.278
Can you maintain it while you breathe?

1227
00:45:56.278 --> 00:45:57.111
So that first of all,

1228
00:45:57.111 --> 00:45:58.612
it's just hard to do that out of the gate.

1229
00:45:58.612 --> 00:45:59.679
And the second thing is you can do it,

1230
00:45:59.679 --> 00:46:01.038
but you're not relaxed.

1231
00:46:01.038 --> 00:46:02.425
So you're gonna have to be able to do that,

1232
00:46:02.425 --> 00:46:06.164
hold it while you can breathe around it.

1233
00:46:06.164 --> 00:46:08.351
The next things for me that were very important,

1234
00:46:08.351 --> 00:46:11.851
I have a very congenitally narrow C spine.

1235
00:46:12.783 --> 00:46:15.936
And if you look at an MRI of my neck,

1236
00:46:15.936 --> 00:46:18.195
you wouldn't believe what a disaster it looks like,

1237
00:46:18.195 --> 00:46:21.805
even though I've never really had an issue,

1238
00:46:21.805 --> 00:46:24.487
but I'm being very preventative about it.

1239
00:46:24.487 --> 00:46:26.505
And so one of the other things I'm working on is learning

1240
00:46:26.505 --> 00:46:29.149
how to use the deep neck stabilizers.

1241
00:46:29.149 --> 00:46:32.135
So these are muscles that ride on the back.

1242
00:46:32.135 --> 00:46:32.968
They're inside.

1243
00:46:32.968 --> 00:46:33.801
You don't see them.

1244
00:46:33.801 --> 00:46:35.578
So they're not these scaling muscles that are in front.

1245
00:46:35.578 --> 00:46:37.157
They're the muscles that sit

1246
00:46:37.157 --> 00:46:39.694
on the vertebral body sort of deep in my neck.

1247
00:46:39.694 --> 00:46:41.929
And it's learning how to use those muscles

1248
00:46:41.929 --> 00:46:44.167
to keep my head forward.

1249
00:46:44.167 --> 00:46:46.567
So I'm not relying on these muscles here.

1250
00:46:46.567 --> 00:46:47.574
And so that's a lot of muscle.

1251
00:46:47.574 --> 00:46:50.499
That's a lot of movements where I'm on my back.

1252
00:46:50.499 --> 00:46:55.499
Chin is forward using the cue of the eyes looking forward,

1253
00:46:55.912 --> 00:46:58.487
which kind of lights up that circuit

1254
00:46:58.487 --> 00:46:59.717
and using various pelvic positions,

1255
00:46:59.717 --> 00:47:02.774
armed positions and light positions in there.

1256
00:47:02.774 --> 00:47:05.134
So it's very subtle and gentle,

1257
00:47:05.134 --> 00:47:06.237
but it's more about like,

1258
00:47:06.237 --> 00:47:09.570
how do I connect my intention in my mind

1259
00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:13.307
to these muscle groups to get them to just activate.

1260
00:47:13.307 --> 00:47:14.606
The starting positions are now,

1261
00:47:14.606 --> 00:47:16.776
it starts to get very elaborate.

1262
00:47:16.776 --> 00:47:20.373
Basically, someone Googles sort of a DNS progression,

1263
00:47:20.373 --> 00:47:22.303
you'll see how complicated it will get,

1264
00:47:22.303 --> 00:47:23.419
'cause you're basically walking

1265
00:47:23.419 --> 00:47:25.169
through the first two years of development.

1266
00:47:25.169 --> 00:47:29.586
So the stuff I just described is the fetus basically,

1267
00:47:31.743 --> 00:47:33.538
and you start moving into a three month position,

1268
00:47:33.538 --> 00:47:34.625
a four month, five month,

1269
00:47:34.625 --> 00:47:37.285
six month position where now a child is learning

1270
00:47:37.285 --> 00:47:38.464
how to roll.

1271
00:47:38.464 --> 00:47:40.528
Well, they roll in a very connected way.

1272
00:47:40.528 --> 00:47:43.508
So when they're reaching for something with this arm,

1273
00:47:43.508 --> 00:47:47.624
they're completely connected across their oblique sling,

1274
00:47:47.624 --> 00:47:50.228
and they're gonna initiate that movement

1275
00:47:50.228 --> 00:47:51.954
and down to their foot.

1276
00:47:51.954 --> 00:47:53.469
This is the other stuff that was just like,

1277
00:47:53.469 --> 00:47:55.426
when I look at videos of me four years ago

1278
00:47:55.426 --> 00:47:57.063
and videos of me now,

1279
00:47:57.063 --> 00:47:58.622
I just don't know what the hell I was doing before.

1280
00:47:58.622 --> 00:47:59.455
Wow.

1281
00:47:59.455 --> 00:48:01.353
Like how I was getting away with the nonsense

1282
00:48:01.353 --> 00:48:03.553
of being completely disconnected,

1283
00:48:03.553 --> 00:48:06.037
scapula's all over the place

1284
00:48:06.037 --> 00:48:07.429
and all that sort of stuff.

1285
00:48:07.429 --> 00:48:08.985
And here's the other thing,

1286
00:48:08.985 --> 00:48:12.475
the best athletes in the world kind of naturally do this.

1287
00:48:12.475 --> 00:48:14.994
That's another thing I've learned is that there are

1288
00:48:14.994 --> 00:48:16.949
some really exceptional athletes out there

1289
00:48:16.949 --> 00:48:20.177
who have never once spent a minute learning DNS.

1290
00:48:20.177 --> 00:48:22.521
But when you look at them,

1291
00:48:22.521 --> 00:48:24.942
they have somehow managed to preserve

1292
00:48:24.942 --> 00:48:26.965
those neuromuscular pathways.

1293
00:48:26.965 --> 00:48:28.753
And that is essentially a big part

1294
00:48:28.753 --> 00:48:30.560
of what their athletic gift is.

1295
00:48:30.560 --> 00:48:32.669
And I would imagine it's pretty good

1296
00:48:32.669 --> 00:48:34.843
for injury prevention, right?

1297
00:48:34.843 --> 00:48:37.033
If you have the ability to recruit all of these muscle

1298
00:48:37.033 --> 00:48:40.116
groups you're inherently more stable

1299
00:48:41.462 --> 00:48:44.944
and resistant to a lot of the injuries

1300
00:48:44.944 --> 00:48:47.016
that are occurring from those imbalances.

1301
00:48:47.016 --> 00:48:49.459
Yeah, and that's why for me,

1302
00:48:49.459 --> 00:48:51.494
it's a once a week,

1303
00:48:51.494 --> 00:48:53.790
do the lesson once a week, you know, push, push,

1304
00:48:53.790 --> 00:48:56.187
how much further can I get in those things?

1305
00:48:56.187 --> 00:48:58.423
And then it's part of what we call our dailies.

1306
00:48:58.423 --> 00:48:59.607
So for our patients,

1307
00:48:59.607 --> 00:49:00.670
we program things called dailies.

1308
00:49:00.670 --> 00:49:03.561
Like this is gonna, whether today's a zone two day,

1309
