like that's a very common athlete problem.

1356
00:50:50.540 --> 00:50:52.133
Beth has names for,

1357
00:50:52.133 --> 00:50:53.637
kind of Beth has broken people down

1358
00:50:53.637 --> 00:50:55.941
into three phenotypes and I can't remember

1359
00:50:55.941 --> 00:50:56.774
what they all are.

1360
00:50:56.774 --> 00:50:58.995
There's kind of the Lucy goosey one.

1361
00:50:58.995 --> 00:51:00.422
But then there's the like locked and loaded one.

1362
00:51:00.422 --> 00:51:01.314
Right.

1363
00:51:01.314 --> 00:51:03.540
So that's me, that's Lance, like that's,

1364
00:51:03.540 --> 00:51:08.042
our natural state is chest is up, pelvis is forward.

1365
00:51:08.042 --> 00:51:09.102
Back is arched.

1366
00:51:09.102 --> 00:51:10.898
Back is arched.

1367
00:51:10.898 --> 00:51:15.384
So excess lordosis and learning how to relax

1368
00:51:15.384 --> 00:51:17.384
my ribs has been insane.

1369
00:51:19.233 --> 00:51:20.272
Yeah.

1370
00:51:20.272 --> 00:51:21.610
The guy who does my tissue work,

1371
00:51:21.610 --> 00:51:23.319
who's been working on me for, I don't know,

1372
00:51:23.319 --> 00:51:26.064
probably 10 years, eight years,

1373
00:51:26.064 --> 00:51:27.568
he's shown me pictures of what my lower back

1374
00:51:27.568 --> 00:51:30.474
looked like five years ago.

1375
00:51:30.474 --> 00:51:31.585
And I don't recognize it.

1376
00:51:31.585 --> 00:51:33.575
So you have these two,

1377
00:51:33.575 --> 00:51:36.035
erector spinae muscles that run down your spine.

1378
00:51:36.035 --> 00:51:38.665
If you look at my erector spinae five years ago

1379
00:51:38.665 --> 00:51:40.059
and you look at them now, you can't believe it.

1380
00:51:40.059 --> 00:51:42.109
Like right now, my back is just flat.

1381
00:51:42.109 --> 00:51:47.109
Like there is no bulging musculature adjacent to my spine.

1382
00:51:47.329 --> 00:51:50.087
And before it was just, that was always on fire.

1383
00:51:50.087 --> 00:51:52.681
I had these huge muscles running up my back

1384
00:51:52.681 --> 00:51:54.749
that were just holding me in that locked position.

1385
00:51:54.749 --> 00:51:55.582
Yeah.

1386
00:51:55.582 --> 00:51:58.229
And that's all from being able to just do this.

1387
00:51:58.229 --> 00:52:00.412
This is exactly where I'm at right now.

1388
00:52:00.412 --> 00:52:01.504
Yeah.

1389
00:52:01.504 --> 00:52:03.479
It's so locked up back here.

1390
00:52:03.479 --> 00:52:05.705
And when I lie flat and I try to do

1391
00:52:05.705 --> 00:52:10.699
pelvic mobility exercises, it's so incredibly limited,

1392
00:52:10.699 --> 00:52:15.032
like that pivot is just, I have no range whatsoever.

1393
00:52:16.432 --> 00:52:18.089
I mean, you'll get it.

1394
00:52:18.089 --> 00:52:19.931
Once you learn to use the hamstrings,

1395
00:52:19.931 --> 00:52:22.120
once you learn how to activate those hamstrings

1396
00:52:22.120 --> 00:52:24.236
and we started doing it single leg at a time.

1397
00:52:24.236 --> 00:52:27.719
this is where Beth's real brilliance shines,

1398
00:52:27.719 --> 00:52:31.359
is just being able to come up with these exercises.

1399
00:52:31.359 --> 00:52:34.561
If you just put your foot on a one inch block and elevate

1400
00:52:34.561 --> 00:52:36.062
the right foot more than the left

1401
00:52:36.062 --> 00:52:38.081
and take turns with the activation,

1402
00:52:38.081 --> 00:52:42.835
all of those things start to make it easier for you

1403
00:52:42.835 --> 00:52:45.349
to initially get that signal

1404
00:52:45.349 --> 00:52:46.828
from your brain to those muscles.

1405
00:52:46.828 --> 00:52:47.661
Mm.

1406
00:52:50.967 --> 00:52:52.993
I wish I learned this when I was 18,

1407
00:52:52.993 --> 00:52:55.807
but I also realize I was too dumb when I was 18.

1408
00:52:55.807 --> 00:52:56.882
And there's probably no way I would've done it.

1409
00:52:56.882 --> 00:52:57.715
Yeah.

1410
00:52:59.466 --> 00:53:01.645
It's that kind of age old question.

1411
00:53:01.645 --> 00:53:02.879
What would you tell your younger self,

1412
00:53:02.879 --> 00:53:04.965
but that presupposes that your younger self

1413
00:53:04.965 --> 00:53:06.568
would be at all open

1414
00:53:06.568 --> 00:53:08.731
to like any of this type of advice.

1415
00:53:08.731 --> 00:53:12.162
There's simply no way the younger version would of.

1416
00:53:12.162 --> 00:53:14.169
Well beyond this kind of realm,

1417
00:53:14.169 --> 00:53:15.212
when you think about longevity

1418
00:53:15.212 --> 00:53:16.695
and when we talk about longevity,

1419
00:53:16.695 --> 00:53:18.286
we're really talking about health span.

1420
00:53:18.286 --> 00:53:21.002
What are the other buckets?

1421
00:53:21.002 --> 00:53:22.810
And I guess a question that I have

1422
00:53:22.810 --> 00:53:25.977
is how does this kind of line up with,

1423
00:53:27.034 --> 00:53:28.653
the ideas of the blue zones

1424
00:53:28.653 --> 00:53:31.433
and these other kind of principles that guide,

1425
00:53:31.433 --> 00:53:34.210
how to think about setting ourselves up

1426
00:53:34.210 --> 00:53:36.113
for longevity success?

1427
00:53:36.113 --> 00:53:39.056
Well, as you said, lifespan is sort of a,

1428
00:53:39.056 --> 00:53:43.245
lifespan and health span are the two vectors of longevity.

1429
00:53:43.245 --> 00:53:44.810
But I do think most people think

1430
00:53:44.810 --> 00:53:46.871
more about the health span one.

1431
00:53:46.871 --> 00:53:49.910
And the good news is they're not independent vectors, right?

1432
00:53:49.910 --> 00:53:51.450
These are actually not orthogonal vectors.

1433
00:53:51.450 --> 00:53:55.651
Even though we represent them orthogonally on a graph,

1434
00:53:55.651 --> 00:54:00.341
if you take all of the steps to live a better life,

1435
00:54:00.341 --> 00:54:04.840
they're just invariably going to also lead to a longer life.

1436
00:54:04.840 --> 00:54:06.612
Now there are exceptions to this rule.

1437
00:54:06.612 --> 00:54:09.145
There are certain trade offs one might make,

1438
00:54:09.145 --> 00:54:10.964
if you were really purely optimizing on one or the other,

1439
00:54:10.964 --> 00:54:12.893
but the overlap is so powerful.

1440
00:54:12.893 --> 00:54:14.722
So I usually tell people not to worry about it.

1441
00:54:14.722 --> 00:54:16.087
In other words, if they say,

1442
00:54:16.087 --> 00:54:16.920
I don't care how long I live,

1443
00:54:16.920 --> 00:54:18.172
I just wanna live the best life possible.

1444
00:54:18.172 --> 00:54:19.005
It's like, great.

1445
00:54:19.005 --> 00:54:21.506
You're gonna live longer by the way.

1446
00:54:21.506 --> 00:54:23.340
So I think of them, as I said, in those three buckets,

1447
00:54:23.340 --> 00:54:24.675
sort of a cognitive bucket,

1448
00:54:24.675 --> 00:54:29.675
a physical structural bucket and an emotional bucket.

1449
00:54:29.834 --> 00:54:32.153
And within that physical bucket, just to kinda round it out,

1450
00:54:32.153 --> 00:54:33.795
you have everything we've just spoken about,

1451
00:54:33.795 --> 00:54:36.797
which is the stability component.

1452
00:54:36.797 --> 00:54:38.201
And that's the foundation.

1453
00:54:38.201 --> 00:54:41.132
Everything has to be built upon that.

1454
00:54:41.132 --> 00:54:41.998
And then there are,

1455
00:54:41.998 --> 00:54:43.343
I sort of think of like three pillars that stand

1456
00:54:43.343 --> 00:54:47.248
on the foundation, strength and aerobic base,

1457
00:54:47.248 --> 00:54:51.438
which is effectively a proxy for mitochondrial efficiency

1458
00:54:51.438 --> 00:54:53.337
and exceptional fuel partitioning,

1459
00:54:53.337 --> 00:54:56.958
and then a peak aerobic/anaerobic piece.

1460
00:54:56.958 --> 00:54:58.307
So kind of think of your V02 Max,

1461
00:54:58.307 --> 00:55:00.974
your zone two and your strength.

1462
00:55:02.655 --> 00:55:05.943
And again, I think people have a sort of intuitive sense

1463
00:55:05.943 --> 00:55:08.525
of what those look like,

1464
00:55:08.525 --> 00:55:10.008
the devil is in the details and you know,

1465
00:55:10.008 --> 00:55:11.093
how much time should you be spending

1466
00:55:11.093 --> 00:55:12.167
on one versus the other.

1467
00:55:12.167 --> 00:55:13.186
And it's really kind of a function

1468
00:55:13.186 --> 00:55:15.345
of how much time does is one willing to devote

1469
00:55:15.345 --> 00:55:16.325
to this craft.

1470
00:55:16.325 --> 00:55:17.158
Right.

1471
00:55:18.154 --> 00:55:19.237
I think we,

1472
00:55:21.341 --> 00:55:24.169
for understandable reasons probably tell people that they

1473
00:55:24.169 --> 00:55:25.632
don't need to do that much exercise,

1474
00:55:25.632 --> 00:55:28.488
'cause we're talking to the average person who's doing none.

1475
00:55:28.488 --> 00:55:32.047
And it's true that if you're doing no exercise,

1476
00:55:32.047 --> 00:55:34.365
just getting you to do three hours a week,

1477
00:55:34.365 --> 00:55:36.652
has an unbelievable impact on your life.

1478
00:55:36.652 --> 00:55:38.354
Probably more than anything else you could tell

1479
00:55:38.354 --> 00:55:39.854
that person to do.

1480
00:55:40.873 --> 00:55:43.010
But I would hate for the message to end there

1481
00:55:43.010 --> 00:55:44.626
because I think if you could do 10 hours

1482
00:55:44.626 --> 00:55:48.072
a week and it's really well structured,

1483
00:55:48.072 --> 00:55:50.871
the sky's the limit for what you could achieve.

1484
00:55:50.871 --> 00:55:54.296
I wanna put a pin in the zone two discussion.

1485
00:55:54.296 --> 00:55:56.593
I wanna get to that later and go deep on that.

1486
00:55:56.593 --> 00:55:59.811
So maybe set that aside for now

1487
00:55:59.811 --> 00:56:03.411
and we can kind of parse the fitness aspect of this,

1488
00:56:03.411 --> 00:56:05.083
the exercise aspect of this,

1489
00:56:05.083 --> 00:56:08.469
but when it comes to these other areas,

1490
00:56:08.469 --> 00:56:12.491
the emotional area and the cognitive area,

1491
00:56:12.491 --> 00:56:15.689
like how are you thinking about practices

1492
00:56:15.689 --> 00:56:16.726
in that regard?

1493
00:56:16.726 --> 00:56:17.949
I mean the emotional area,

1494
00:56:17.949 --> 00:56:20.141
that gets kind of ephemeral, I suppose,

1495
00:56:20.141 --> 00:56:22.755
but maybe we'll start with the cognitive stuff.

1496
00:56:22.755 --> 00:56:26.166
So the cognitive one really comes down

1497
00:56:26.166 --> 00:56:27.845
to a very strong overlap

1498
00:56:27.845 --> 00:56:29.007
with how we think about the prevention

1499
00:56:29.007 --> 00:56:31.980
of neurodegenerative disease, specifically dementia.

1500
00:56:31.980 --> 00:56:34.506
And so we think of kind of these three areas of cognition,

1501
00:56:34.506 --> 00:56:37.381
executive function, processing speed and memory,

1502
00:56:37.381 --> 00:56:40.218
both short term and long term.

1503
00:56:40.218 --> 00:56:41.381
Different forms of dementia

1504
00:56:41.381 --> 00:56:46.280
will take on a different assault on each of those things.

1505
00:56:46.280 --> 00:56:49.496
And that's become a very interesting study

1506
00:56:49.496 --> 00:56:51.784
in the field of neurology.

1507
00:56:51.784 --> 00:56:52.822
There was a day when we just thought

1508
00:56:52.822 --> 00:56:56.049
of Alzheimer's disease as a disease.

1509
00:56:56.049 --> 00:56:58.872
The joke, is not really a joke,

1510
00:56:58.872 --> 00:57:01.143
but the joke in neurology is if you've seen one patient

1511
00:57:01.143 --> 00:57:02.212
with Alzheimer's disease,

1512
00:57:02.212 --> 00:57:04.961
you've seen one patient with Alzheimer's disease.

1513
00:57:04.961 --> 00:57:06.834
So I think we're now starting to realize

1514
00:57:06.834 --> 00:57:08.866
there's a lot of different subtypes within there,

1515
00:57:08.866 --> 00:57:11.754
but we also realize that there are some commonalities

1516
00:57:11.754 --> 00:57:13.194
with respect to prevention

1517
00:57:13.194 --> 00:57:14.404
and unfortunately or fortunately,

1518
00:57:14.404 --> 00:57:15.739
depending on how you think about it,

1519
00:57:15.739 --> 00:57:18.320
prevention is kind of our best hope when it comes to that.

1520
00:57:18.320 --> 00:57:21.641
This is even more so than cancer

1521
00:57:21.641 --> 00:57:23.113
and certainly cardiovascular disease.

1522
00:57:23.113 --> 00:57:25.385
We really don't have options

1523
00:57:25.385 --> 00:57:26.776
for patients once they have dementia.

1524
00:57:26.776 --> 00:57:28.103
Yeah.

1525
00:57:28.103 --> 00:57:32.146
This has been an area of extensive study

1526
00:57:32.146 --> 00:57:34.603
that has yielded effectively nothing.

1527
00:57:34.603 --> 00:57:35.786
If we're going to be brutally honest,

1528
00:57:35.786 --> 00:57:38.858
it has yielded effectively nothing.

1529
00:57:38.858 --> 00:57:39.923
There are some treatments

1530
00:57:39.923 --> 00:57:43.256
that can probably slow down progression,

1531
00:57:45.295 --> 00:57:46.994
but the reality is you just wanna be in a position where

1532
00:57:46.994 --> 00:57:49.589
you're addressing this even before

1533
00:57:49.589 --> 00:57:52.166
we have what's called mild cognitive impairment.

1534
00:57:52.166 --> 00:57:54.407
So what do we know about this?

1535
00:57:54.407 --> 00:57:57.555
Well, I mean, what we know is exercise

1536
00:57:57.555 --> 00:58:01.922
is the single biggest elixir for brain health.

1537
00:58:01.922 --> 00:58:04.088
And it's amazing how,

1538
00:58:04.088 --> 00:58:05.535
how much more powerful it appears to be

1539
00:58:05.535 --> 00:58:07.877
than nutrition, even sleep

1540
00:58:07.877 --> 00:58:09.114
and those things are very important,

1541
00:58:09.114 --> 00:58:11.603
but exercise is in a league of its own.

1542
00:58:11.603 --> 00:58:13.733
And we studied this question extensively

1543
00:58:13.733 --> 00:58:15.051
about eight years ago

1544
00:58:15.051 --> 00:58:16.466
and I didn't,

1545
00:58:16.466 --> 00:58:18.615
I initially just refused to believe this was the answer,

1546
00:58:18.615 --> 00:58:20.156
'cause it seemed too simplistic.

1547
00:58:20.156 --> 00:58:22.565
I thought there had to be something more powerful.

1548
00:58:22.565 --> 00:58:24.965
I thought DHA or something else

1549
00:58:24.965 --> 00:58:27.033
could have had a bigger role,

1550
00:58:27.033 --> 00:58:29.769
but as important as sleep, nutrition are,

1551
00:58:29.769 --> 00:58:32.876
exercise kind of takes the cake.

1552
00:58:32.876 --> 00:58:34.963
And I suspect it's because it impacts so many systems.

1553
00:58:34.963 --> 00:58:36.050
Right.

1554
00:58:36.050 --> 00:58:39.508
So exercise is going to impact glucose disposal,

1555
00:58:39.508 --> 00:58:42.341
insulin sensitivity, inflammation.

1556
00:58:43.516 --> 00:58:47.266
It produces growth factors for neurons, BDNF.

1557
00:58:51.415 --> 00:58:53.859
You tell a person that the most important thing

1558
00:58:53.859 --> 00:58:55.960
they can do for their brain health is to exercise.

1559
00:58:55.960 --> 00:58:57.566
That's where you get into the hey,

1560
00:58:57.566 --> 00:58:59.065
if you could do three hours a week, that's great.

1561
00:58:59.065 --> 00:59:01.631
If you can do seven hours a week, it's even better.

1562
00:59:01.631 --> 00:59:03.917
Sleep does matter.

1563
00:59:03.917 --> 00:59:06.250
With sleep, I think we have,

1564
00:59:07.313 --> 00:59:10.810
the problem of sort of one, do you have pathology?

1565
00:59:10.810 --> 00:59:12.383
Yes or no, right?

1566
00:59:12.383 --> 00:59:13.216
Do you have sleep apnea?

1567
00:59:13.216 --> 00:59:15.175
I think there's a lot of undiagnosed sleep apnea out there.

1568
00:59:15.175 --> 00:59:17.444
So we kind of have to make sure we're catching those

1569
00:59:17.444 --> 00:59:19.848
patients 'cause they don't all look the same, right?

1570
00:59:19.848 --> 00:59:22.401
The stereotype is somebody with sleep apnea is overweight

1571
00:59:22.401 --> 00:59:23.987
and they're snoring all the time,

1572
00:59:23.987 --> 00:59:26.260
but it turns out that there's a lot of sleep apnea

1573
00:59:26.260 --> 00:59:27.732
that doesn't fit into that bucket.

1574
00:59:27.732 --> 00:59:29.799
So we definitely wanna catch those people.

1575
00:59:29.799 --> 00:59:32.565
And then you get into basically duration and quality.

1576
00:59:32.565 --> 00:59:34.088
So are you sleeping long enough?

1577
00:59:34.088 --> 00:59:37.351
And are you getting appropriate staging?

1578
00:59:37.351 --> 00:59:38.715
Nutrition matters of course,

1579
00:59:38.715 --> 00:59:42.853
but I think mostly as it fits into metabolic health.

1580
00:59:42.853 --> 00:59:45.189
So metabolic health becomes a very important part of this

1581
00:59:45.189 --> 00:59:47.778
and then there's the vascular health piece.

1582
00:59:47.778 --> 00:59:49.171
So all the things that are kind of good

1583
00:59:49.171 --> 00:59:50.004
for the heart are good for the brain.

1584
00:59:50.004 --> 00:59:50.837
Sure.

1585
00:59:50.837 --> 00:59:52.153
And that means kind of having

1586
00:59:52.153 --> 00:59:54.690
the lowest level of Apo B,

1587
00:59:54.690 --> 00:59:56.085
or we could sort of loosely talk

1588
00:59:56.085 --> 00:59:57.565
about that as LDL cholesterol,

1589
00:59:57.565 --> 00:59:59.694
the lower that is the better.

1590
00:59:59.694 --> 01:00:02.355
And the more regulated your blood pressure is the better.

1591
01:00:02.355 --> 01:00:04.387
The brain is a highly vascular organ.

1592
01:00:04.387 --> 01:00:07.284
And it has a lot of the same microvascular structures

1593
01:00:07.284 --> 01:00:08.329
that we see in cardiovascular disease.

1594
01:00:08.329 --> 01:00:11.548
So the overlap between cardiovascular disease

1595
01:00:11.548 --> 01:00:12.606
and dementia is enormous.

1596
01:00:12.606 --> 01:00:15.834
Sure, and whether it's cardiovascular disease

1597
01:00:15.834 --> 01:00:18.056
or some form of dementia,

1598
01:00:18.056 --> 01:00:20.704
these are diseases that are being built

1599
01:00:20.704 --> 01:00:22.612
bit by bit for decades.

1600
01:00:22.612 --> 01:00:23.552
That's right.

1601
01:00:23.552 --> 01:00:26.026
So we can't start thinking about this soon enough,

1602
01:00:26.026 --> 01:00:29.369
the earlier in our life that we're adopting the lifestyle

1603
01:00:29.369 --> 01:00:33.199
measures required to sidestep these things.

1604
01:00:33.199 --> 01:00:35.443
It cannot be overstated.

1605
01:00:35.443 --> 01:00:37.803
Yeah, and that's in my view,

1606
01:00:37.803 --> 01:00:40.107
probably the biggest single failing

1607
01:00:40.107 --> 01:00:41.947
of our medical system today.

1608
01:00:41.947 --> 01:00:44.731
What I call medicine 2.0,

1609
01:00:44.731 --> 01:00:46.756
is medicine 2.0 is largely

1610
01:00:46.756 --> 01:00:49.706
predicated on addressing a risk,

1611
01:00:49.706 --> 01:00:52.067
once it reaches a threshold.

1612
01:00:52.067 --> 01:00:53.470
So in cardiovascular disease,

1613
01:00:53.470 --> 01:00:56.909
we typically use the threshold of 5% at a 10 year

1614
01:00:56.909 --> 01:00:58.904
forward looking window.

1615
01:00:58.904 --> 01:01:02.955
So once your risk of heart attack, stroke or cardiac death

1616
01:01:02.955 --> 01:01:06.664
reaches 5% for the next 10 years,

1617
01:01:06.664 --> 01:01:09.081
you are a treatment priority.

1618
01:01:10.408 --> 01:01:11.377
And I'll give you an example

1619
01:01:11.377 --> 01:01:13.727
of why I think that makes no sense.

1620
01:01:13.727 --> 01:01:15.568
So we know that smoking causes lung cancer.

1621
01:01:15.568 --> 01:01:17.749
There's no ambiguity that there's a causal relationship

1622
01:01:17.749 --> 01:01:19.998
between cigarettes and lung cancer.

1623
01:01:19.998 --> 01:01:23.167
So imagine if we said to all smokers out there,

1624
01:01:23.167 --> 01:01:27.881
the moment we believe your risk of lung cancer exceeds 5%

1625
01:01:27.881 --> 01:01:31.602
over the next decade, we're gonna make you quit smoking.

1626
01:01:31.602 --> 01:01:34.290
Conversely, imagine you just said,

1627
01:01:34.290 --> 01:01:35.972
the moment you pick up a cigarette,

1628
01:01:35.972 --> 01:01:37.522
we're gonna tell you to quit smoking

1629
01:01:37.522 --> 01:01:39.719
because smoking is causally related,

1630
01:01:39.719 --> 01:01:42.657
totally different framework.

1631
01:01:42.657 --> 01:01:43.887
And it's the latter framework that I think

1632
01:01:43.887 --> 01:01:46.670
we have to be thinking about for cardiovascular disease

1633
01:01:46.670 --> 01:01:48.238
and cerebrovascular disease.

1634
01:01:48.238 --> 01:01:50.214
And certainly when you talk about,

1635
01:01:50.214 --> 01:01:51.735
health span or longevity,

1636
01:01:51.735 --> 01:01:54.322
the low hanging fruit here is dementia,

1637
01:01:54.322 --> 01:01:59.123
various forms of dementia, cardiovascular disease, diabetes,

1638
01:01:59.123 --> 01:02:01.609
obesity, high blood pressure.

1639
01:02:01.609 --> 01:02:04.674
You're not gonna have a long fulfilling life,

1640
01:02:04.674 --> 01:02:07.185
if you don't figure out how

1641
01:02:07.185 --> 01:02:09.057
to wend your way around these diseases

1642
01:02:09.057 --> 01:02:10.856
that are really,

1643
01:02:10.856 --> 01:02:13.489
they're the gorillas in the house

1644
01:02:13.489 --> 01:02:15.374
that are killing most people.

1645
01:02:15.374 --> 01:02:18.264
Yeah, we call them the four horsemen.

1646
01:02:18.264 --> 01:02:21.566
So ASCVD, so Atherosclerotic Cardiovascular Disease,

1647
01:02:21.566 --> 01:02:23.477
Cancer Neurodegenerative Disease,

1648
01:02:23.477 --> 01:02:25.090
and then the whole cluster of metabolic diseases,

1649
01:02:25.090 --> 01:02:26.483
which is kind of described it

1650
01:02:26.483 --> 01:02:28.340
as a spectrum from insulin resistance,

1651
01:02:28.340 --> 01:02:30.965
all the way to type two diabetes and everything in between,

1652
01:02:30.965 --> 01:02:32.829
including non-alcoholic fatty liver disease.

1653
01:02:32.829 --> 01:02:34.058
Mm.

1654
01:02:34.058 --> 01:02:36.462
Which is poised to become the number one indication

1655
01:02:36.462 --> 01:02:38.532
for liver transplant in the United States within the decade.

1656
01:02:38.532 --> 01:02:39.723
Wow.

