2026
01:17:44.625 --> 01:17:46.847
you're going from the most metabolically healthy

2027
01:17:46.847 --> 01:17:48.806
to the least metabolically healthy.

2028
01:17:48.806 --> 01:17:50.376
But I haven't really answered the question for you.

2029
01:17:50.376 --> 01:17:51.710
I've just kind of labeled it.

2030
01:17:51.710 --> 01:17:53.675
So what does it actually mean?

2031
01:17:53.675 --> 01:17:55.466
And think the best way that I can explain it

2032
01:17:55.466 --> 01:17:57.714
is through fuel partitioning.

2033
01:17:57.714 --> 01:18:02.714
So fuel partitioning is basically a fancy way of saying,

2034
01:18:02.729 --> 01:18:05.840
how does your body know what to do

2035
01:18:05.840 --> 01:18:08.002
with the energy you put in it?

2036
01:18:08.002 --> 01:18:12.419
Where does it put it and how does it access it later?

2037
01:18:13.308 --> 01:18:15.419
And someone like you, someone like me,

2038
01:18:15.419 --> 01:18:18.642
like our bodies are pretty good at doing this.

2039
01:18:18.642 --> 01:18:23.048
We know when you eat glucose, fat, protein,

2040
01:18:23.048 --> 01:18:26.390
this part we should use, this part we should store.

2041
01:18:26.390 --> 01:18:27.901
And when we go to exercise,

2042
01:18:27.901 --> 01:18:29.538
we know how to access the right one.

2043
01:18:29.538 --> 01:18:32.984
So this is where kind of zone two comes back into it.

2044
01:18:32.984 --> 01:18:35.551
Zone two is basically a test

2045
01:18:35.551 --> 01:18:37.689
of your mitochondrial efficiency.

2046
01:18:37.689 --> 01:18:40.382
And this is one of the hallmarks of aging, right?

2047
01:18:40.382 --> 01:18:42.111
So there are nine hallmarks of aging

2048
01:18:42.111 --> 01:18:43.818
and one of them is mitochondrial dysfunction.

2049
01:18:43.818 --> 01:18:45.594
We just know that as we get older,

2050
01:18:45.594 --> 01:18:48.367
our mitochondria get less and less effective.

2051
01:18:48.367 --> 01:18:51.176
And what you really want are mitochondria

2052
01:18:51.176 --> 01:18:54.438
that can access fat stores at basically higher

2053
01:18:54.438 --> 01:18:56.515
and higher levels of work output.

2054
01:18:56.515 --> 01:18:59.453
So an example of a metabolically healthy person

2055
01:18:59.453 --> 01:19:02.951
to a metabolically unhealthy person,

2056
01:19:02.951 --> 01:19:04.953
is have them go for a walk.

2057
01:19:04.953 --> 01:19:08.210
The metabolically healthy person is accessing exclusively

2058
01:19:08.210 --> 01:19:09.979
fat stores to do that.

2059
01:19:09.979 --> 01:19:12.163
The metabolically unhealthy person is burning through

2060
01:19:12.163 --> 01:19:14.816
glucose and glycogen to make that happen.

2061
01:19:14.816 --> 01:19:17.061
That's a fundamental distinction between them.

2062
01:19:17.061 --> 01:19:18.692
Sure.

2063
01:19:18.692 --> 01:19:19.869
And the trade off of that

2064
01:19:19.869 --> 01:19:22.373
is what's the natural history of that?

2065
01:19:22.373 --> 01:19:25.553
This person is getting fatter and this person

2066
01:19:25.553 --> 01:19:26.761
is having a harder and harder time

2067
01:19:26.761 --> 01:19:28.427
regulating their glucose levels.

2068
01:19:28.427 --> 01:19:29.302
Right.

2069
01:19:29.302 --> 01:19:32.182
So the more dysregulated you are,

2070
01:19:32.182 --> 01:19:34.684
the more likely you are to gain weight.

2071
01:19:34.684 --> 01:19:36.714
It just becomes like a vicious cycle

2072
01:19:36.714 --> 01:19:38.024
that gets worse and worse.

2073
01:19:38.024 --> 01:19:41.011
Yeah, and then it really gets worse and worse

2074
01:19:41.011 --> 01:19:44.063
because the weight gaining part on the surface ,

2075
01:19:44.063 --> 01:19:45.427
is what people kind of get worked up about,

2076
01:19:45.427 --> 01:19:48.185
oh my God, like I'm getting fatter.

2077
01:19:48.185 --> 01:19:51.016
But the reality of it is it's not this subcutaneous fat,

2078
01:19:51.016 --> 01:19:51.972
that's the problem.

2079
01:19:51.972 --> 01:19:55.472
It's not the fat that we see on our waist.

2080
01:19:56.983 --> 01:20:00.033
It's the fat that we don't see, that's the problem.

2081
01:20:00.033 --> 01:20:03.200
Called the sort of the extra,

2082
01:20:03.200 --> 01:20:04.953
so this is called subcutaneous fat.

2083
01:20:04.953 --> 01:20:07.742
So if you take the extra subcutaneous fat depots,

2084
01:20:07.742 --> 01:20:10.300
like visceral fat, the fat that surrounds your organs,

2085
01:20:10.300 --> 01:20:12.656
the fat that accumulates around the pancreas, the kidneys,

2086
01:20:12.656 --> 01:20:15.812
the heart, within the muscle itself,

2087
01:20:15.812 --> 01:20:18.119
and I'm talking right within the muscle cells,

2088
01:20:18.119 --> 01:20:19.953
that's the fat that's incredibly destructive.

2089
01:20:19.953 --> 01:20:23.809
And that can represent a tiny fraction of your total fat.

2090
01:20:23.809 --> 01:20:26.559
If a person has 20 pounds of fat,

2091
01:20:28.644 --> 01:20:33.309
let's say 20 kilos of fat on a 200 per pound person,

2092
01:20:33.309 --> 01:20:34.748
from a body fat perspective,

2093
01:20:34.748 --> 01:20:36.438
probably put them at the 25th percentile,

2094
01:20:36.438 --> 01:20:38.740
they're pretty run of the mill.

2095
01:20:38.740 --> 01:20:40.139
But if only 10% of that fat

2096
01:20:40.139 --> 01:20:42.287
were out of their subcutaneous stores,

2097
01:20:42.287 --> 01:20:44.362
that would place them at the 90th percentile

2098
01:20:44.362 --> 01:20:45.195
for visceral fat.

2099
01:20:45.195 --> 01:20:46.412
Hmm.

2100
01:20:46.412 --> 01:20:47.992
And that's the dangerous fat,

2101
01:20:47.992 --> 01:20:48.825
that's the inflammatory fat,

2102
01:20:48.825 --> 01:20:51.381
that's the fat that's truly wreaking havoc

2103
01:20:51.381 --> 01:20:53.600
with respect to metabolic illness.

2104
01:20:53.600 --> 01:20:56.011
Right, so typically if somebody

2105
01:20:56.011 --> 01:20:57.969
is suffering from metabolic syndrome

2106
01:20:57.969 --> 01:20:59.888
and they go to their general practitioner,

2107
01:20:59.888 --> 01:21:01.702
that person is likely to say

2108
01:21:01.702 --> 01:21:05.074
your blood pressure's high, you're prediabetic.

2109
01:21:05.074 --> 01:21:08.077
We need to put you on this battery of medications.

2110
01:21:08.077 --> 01:21:10.244
You have patients in which

2111
01:21:11.595 --> 01:21:13.648
they're displaying similar characteristics.

2112
01:21:13.648 --> 01:21:16.141
You put them on a lifestyle protocol

2113
01:21:16.141 --> 01:21:19.474
to try to basically reduce these factors

2114
01:21:20.615 --> 01:21:23.153
and get their metabolic health in check.

2115
01:21:23.153 --> 01:21:26.121
So explain a little bit about what that process is

2116
01:21:26.121 --> 01:21:29.822
and just how malleable metabolic health is.

2117
01:21:29.822 --> 01:21:34.046
Fortunately it is quite malleable and there are,

2118
01:21:34.046 --> 01:21:36.025
different ways that we go about doing it

2119
01:21:36.025 --> 01:21:38.241
depending on what we think are the contributing factors.

2120
01:21:38.241 --> 01:21:39.490
So sleep, nutrition,

2121
01:21:39.490 --> 01:21:42.303
exercise and pharmacology,

2122
01:21:42.303 --> 01:21:43.978
would be the main contributing factors.

2123
01:21:43.978 --> 01:21:46.487
Now the fifth thing that we always think about

2124
01:21:46.487 --> 01:21:48.331
is emotional health.

2125
01:21:48.331 --> 01:21:50.533
And for some people that can be an enormously contributing

2126
01:21:50.533 --> 01:21:53.033
factor both directly and also,

2127
01:21:54.109 --> 01:21:56.214
and more commonly indirectly.

2128
01:21:56.214 --> 01:21:58.078
There's a stress a component of that emotional health.

2129
01:21:58.078 --> 01:21:59.216
Yeah.

2130
01:21:59.216 --> 01:22:00.332
And so stress directly

2131
01:22:00.332 --> 01:22:02.293
just through its endocrine function, right?

2132
01:22:02.293 --> 01:22:05.323
So hipercolesterolemia is completely destructive

2133
01:22:05.323 --> 01:22:07.204
to your metabolic health in excess.

2134
01:22:07.204 --> 01:22:08.713
So transient stress, not so much an issue,

2135
01:22:08.713 --> 01:22:11.589
but this chronic elevation of cortisol,

2136
01:22:11.589 --> 01:22:14.705
is incredibly destructive for insulin resistance

2137
01:22:14.705 --> 01:22:16.542
and all sorts of other things.

2138
01:22:16.542 --> 01:22:19.129
But more importantly and more subtly is,

2139
01:22:19.129 --> 01:22:22.789
it's the emotional health bucket that gets in the way

2140
01:22:22.789 --> 01:22:24.623
of making some of the behavioral changes

2141
01:22:24.623 --> 01:22:25.813
in these other buckets.

2142
01:22:25.813 --> 01:22:28.838
Nobody has a hard time taking medicine.

2143
01:22:28.838 --> 01:22:30.761
And the reality is you can fix a lot of numbers

2144
01:22:30.761 --> 01:22:32.572
with the medicine,

2145
01:22:32.572 --> 01:22:34.069
but you're not getting as much of a benefit

2146
01:22:34.069 --> 01:22:38.819
as if you fix them sort of with food, sleep and exercise.

2147
01:22:41.969 --> 01:22:43.847
So when we're trying to evaluate, so let's say,

2148
01:22:43.847 --> 01:22:48.360
the hypothetical patient comes in and everything is wrong.

2149
01:22:48.360 --> 01:22:49.693
So then the next question is why,

2150
01:22:49.693 --> 01:22:51.774
what are the contributing factors?

2151
01:22:51.774 --> 01:22:53.660
Sleep is the first thing we always wanna look at,

2152
01:22:53.660 --> 01:22:58.397
'cause it's the easiest one to assess pretty quickly.

2153
01:22:58.397 --> 01:22:59.642
So again, to your point,

2154
01:22:59.642 --> 01:23:01.496
most of our patients are wearing an Oura Ring.

2155
01:23:01.496 --> 01:23:04.962
So that's provides some information.

2156
01:23:04.962 --> 01:23:09.067
We use some sleep surveys that are pretty well vetted,

2157
01:23:09.067 --> 01:23:11.881
so there's probably four or five of them.

2158
01:23:11.881 --> 01:23:14.154
And depending on what we see on the Oura data

2159
01:23:14.154 --> 01:23:16.344
and what we see in the sleep surveys,

2160
01:23:16.344 --> 01:23:19.488
we then might move to another test to look for,

2161
01:23:19.488 --> 01:23:21.670
like an at home test that can give us

2162
01:23:21.670 --> 01:23:24.230
a very quick view on apnea.

2163
01:23:24.230 --> 01:23:25.763
And depending on what we see there,

2164
01:23:25.763 --> 01:23:28.955
we might move to a more formal sleep study.

2165
01:23:28.955 --> 01:23:30.958
And so we're kind of going through, is there pathology?

2166
01:23:30.958 --> 01:23:32.053
Yes or no?

2167
01:23:32.053 --> 01:23:33.486
Are you over slept or under slept?

2168
01:23:33.486 --> 01:23:34.942
Yes or no.

2169
01:23:34.942 --> 01:23:36.775
If you're under slept,

2170
01:23:38.143 --> 01:23:40.041
are you also dysregulated with staging?

2171
01:23:40.041 --> 01:23:42.415
And we kind of have just an entire rabbit hole.

2172
01:23:42.415 --> 01:23:44.800
We go down with sleep that involves

2173
01:23:44.800 --> 01:23:46.484
sleep hygiene, that involves supplements.

2174
01:23:46.484 --> 01:23:48.059
And sometimes frankly includes medications.

2175
01:23:48.059 --> 01:23:49.444
There's a prescription drug

2176
01:23:49.444 --> 01:23:51.470
called Trazodone that really helps

2177
01:23:51.470 --> 01:23:54.169
people who are dysregulated

2178
01:23:54.169 --> 01:23:57.362
in their sleep because of rumination.

2179
01:23:57.362 --> 01:23:59.470
So it's a non-habit forming amazing drug

2180
01:23:59.470 --> 01:24:00.303
that more importantly,

2181
01:24:00.303 --> 01:24:01.771
preserves sleep architecture.

2182
01:24:01.771 --> 01:24:03.441
Most sleep drugs don't really preserve

2183
01:24:03.441 --> 01:24:05.035
sleep architecture that well.

2184
01:24:05.035 --> 01:24:05.868
Right.

2185
01:24:07.149 --> 01:24:08.468
On the nutrition front,

2186
01:24:08.468 --> 01:24:10.784
we're asking the first order question is,

2187
01:24:10.784 --> 01:24:12.873
are you over nourished or undernourished?

2188
01:24:12.873 --> 01:24:15.157
And are you, obviously you're metabolically unhealthy.

2189
01:24:15.157 --> 01:24:16.150
We wouldn't be having this discussion.

2190
01:24:16.150 --> 01:24:17.393
So over nourished, undernourished,

2191
01:24:17.393 --> 01:24:18.770
usually if you're metabolically unhealthy,

2192
01:24:18.770 --> 01:24:21.272
you're also over nourished.

2193
01:24:21.272 --> 01:24:23.412
And then the next question is, are you over muscled?

2194
01:24:23.412 --> 01:24:25.665
Pardon me under muscled or adequately muscled.

2195
01:24:25.665 --> 01:24:27.830
And that plays a hugely important role for our protein

2196
01:24:27.830 --> 01:24:31.088
strategy and our training strategy.

2197
01:24:31.088 --> 01:24:33.137
And then once we sort of go through the well,

2198
01:24:33.137 --> 01:24:35.755
presumably you're over nourished,

2199
01:24:35.755 --> 01:24:37.218
let's say you're also under muscled.

2200
01:24:37.218 --> 01:24:38.228
Let's make it really hard,

2201
01:24:38.228 --> 01:24:40.734
'cause that's a really difficult reconciliation.

2202
01:24:40.734 --> 01:24:43.098
If someone's over nourished and under muscled,

2203
01:24:43.098 --> 01:24:46.534
they have to lose weight and gain muscle.

2204
01:24:46.534 --> 01:24:51.039
So now you need a lot of protein and a lot of training.

2205
01:24:51.039 --> 01:24:53.508
The question then becomes what is the strategy

2206
01:24:53.508 --> 01:24:55.073
for intake reduction?

2207
01:24:55.073 --> 01:24:57.898
You have three strategies, caloric restriction,

2208
01:24:57.898 --> 01:24:59.824
dietary restriction, time restriction,

2209
01:24:59.824 --> 01:25:01.903
and go into all the details

2210
01:25:01.903 --> 01:25:04.653
of the pros and cons of each one.

2211
01:25:05.505 --> 01:25:07.094
On the exercise front,

2212
01:25:07.094 --> 01:25:11.484
it really depends on what they're doing to begin with.

2213
01:25:11.484 --> 01:25:13.685
Each type of exercise has its benefit,

2214
01:25:13.685 --> 01:25:15.535
but if you're talking about a person

2215
01:25:15.535 --> 01:25:18.321
who's starting from zero, we're mostly working on movement,

2216
01:25:18.321 --> 01:25:20.693
stability and zone two.

2217
01:25:20.693 --> 01:25:22.723
I'm not gonna push them into VO2 Max,

2218
01:25:22.723 --> 01:25:23.739
not gonna let them get hurt

2219
01:25:23.739 --> 01:25:27.213
trying to do any significant strength training.

2220
01:25:27.213 --> 01:25:29.535
It's how do we fix the chassis,

2221
01:25:29.535 --> 01:25:31.739
so that we can make the car drive faster and faster.

2222
01:25:31.739 --> 01:25:33.232
Right.

2223
01:25:33.232 --> 01:25:35.552
And in those patients,

2224
01:25:35.552 --> 01:25:38.428
literally just putting them on a treadmill and walking them,

2225
01:25:38.428 --> 01:25:41.574
up an incline can be very beneficial.

2226
01:25:41.574 --> 01:25:44.894
So assuming high patient adherence to these protocols,

2227
01:25:44.894 --> 01:25:47.461
what is the timeframe in which you can take somebody

2228
01:25:47.461 --> 01:25:51.913
who is fairly dysregulated into a zone of relative safety?

2229
01:25:51.913 --> 01:25:53.788
Yeah, again, it's all comes down to adherence.

2230
01:25:53.788 --> 01:25:56.152
I think back to a patient that joined our practice

2231
01:25:56.152 --> 01:25:59.402
about a year ago, liver function tests,

2232
01:26:00.957 --> 01:26:02.608
on arrival were,

2233
01:26:02.608 --> 01:26:03.441
3X, 4X, normal,

2234
01:26:03.441 --> 01:26:06.407
demonstrating lots of fatty liver disease,

2235
01:26:06.407 --> 01:26:10.285
very high degree of insulin resistance.

2236
01:26:10.285 --> 01:26:12.244
We do something called an oral glucose tolerance test

2237
01:26:12.244 --> 01:26:13.787
with everyone that comes in the door.

2238
01:26:13.787 --> 01:26:16.213
So they drink a big glucose drink.

2239
01:26:16.213 --> 01:26:18.828
And then we measure glucose and insulin spikes

2240
01:26:18.828 --> 01:26:20.934
for the next couple of hours.

2241
01:26:20.934 --> 01:26:24.130
I think this patient probably within six months

2242
01:26:24.130 --> 01:26:25.426
had those things reversed

2243
01:26:25.426 --> 01:26:27.754
and then this patient was incredibly diligent.

2244
01:26:27.754 --> 01:26:31.837
This patient was doing everything that was asked.

2245
01:26:34.702 --> 01:26:37.618
I'm less wed to how long it takes and more wed

2246
01:26:37.618 --> 01:26:41.936
to can we create sustainable things that if it takes a year,

2247
01:26:41.936 --> 01:26:43.574
but you found a groove that is sustainable.

2248
01:26:43.574 --> 01:26:44.407
Right.

2249
01:26:44.407 --> 01:26:45.240
That's more important to me

2250
01:26:45.240 --> 01:26:46.150
than trying to fix it in three months.

2251
01:26:46.150 --> 01:26:47.817
Yeah, interesting.

2252
01:26:48.762 --> 01:26:52.784
I've had just some interesting revelations

2253
01:26:52.784 --> 01:26:54.525
in my own experimentation

2254
01:26:54.525 --> 01:26:56.525
wearing a CGM, noticing,

2255
01:26:57.721 --> 01:27:01.138
when and how I go out of my sort of zone.

2256
01:27:04.328 --> 01:27:06.497
I noticed that when I was experimenting with one meal a day,

2257
01:27:06.497 --> 01:27:07.487
that was a disaster,

2258
01:27:07.487 --> 01:27:11.110
especially when that one meal a day comes very late.

2259
01:27:11.110 --> 01:27:14.272
Disregulates my sleep and the spikes were crazy

2260
01:27:14.272 --> 01:27:15.311
and they don't come down quickly.

2261
01:27:15.311 --> 01:27:16.977
And I was like, and I've been doing that for a while.

2262
01:27:16.977 --> 01:27:19.039
And I was like, I have to stop doing this.

2263
01:27:19.039 --> 01:27:22.077
So there's been really helpful kind of feedback

2264
01:27:22.077 --> 01:27:23.687
that I've gotten from this.

2265
01:27:23.687 --> 01:27:25.717
But I also admit to not really knowing

2266
01:27:25.717 --> 01:27:29.182
how to interpret this data, like when I spike,

2267
01:27:29.182 --> 01:27:30.586
if it comes down relatively quickly,

2268
01:27:30.586 --> 01:27:32.370
should I be concerned about that?

2269
01:27:32.370 --> 01:27:35.495
Or what should I, and I think this is like a,

2270
01:27:35.495 --> 01:27:40.302
a danger with a lot of these trackable types of things,

2271
01:27:40.302 --> 01:27:41.626
is it gives us all this data,

2272
01:27:41.626 --> 01:27:45.002
but there's less focus on like what to do with the data

2273
01:27:45.002 --> 01:27:47.907
and really to kind of figure out,

2274
01:27:47.907 --> 01:27:50.598
what is meaningful in all of this.

2275
01:27:50.598 --> 01:27:52.514
So what should I be thinking about

2276
01:27:52.514 --> 01:27:54.566
and what are some of the common misconceptions

2277
01:27:54.566 --> 01:27:57.733
or misinterpretation of wearable data?

2278
01:27:59.220 --> 01:28:01.868
So the short answer is we don't know

2279
01:28:01.868 --> 01:28:03.980
because we haven't had these wearables for long.

2280
01:28:03.980 --> 01:28:07.128
So it's a really new phenomenon to be able to send people

2281
01:28:07.128 --> 01:28:11.448
into the wild and measure their glucose continuously.

2282
01:28:11.448 --> 01:28:13.900
So one has to acknowledge that and then say,

2283
01:28:13.900 --> 01:28:15.331
well, based on that,

2284
01:28:15.331 --> 01:28:17.730
how can we extrapolate from what is known?

2285
01:28:17.730 --> 01:28:21.324
And so our team did some amazing work on this,

2286
01:28:21.324 --> 01:28:23.146
about a year and a half ago.

2287
01:28:23.146 --> 01:28:26.692
It was about six months worth of effort

2288
01:28:26.692 --> 01:28:28.906
to look at all of the literature around three issues

2289
01:28:28.906 --> 01:28:33.573
of glucose, outside of the setting of type two diabetes.

2290
01:28:34.636 --> 01:28:36.893
So we absolutely know in the setting of type two diabetes

2291
01:28:36.893 --> 01:28:39.948
that a higher average glucose is worse.

2292
01:28:39.948 --> 01:28:41.739
That is the definition of the metric

2293
01:28:41.739 --> 01:28:43.750
that we use to make the diagnosis of the disease.

2294
01:28:43.750 --> 01:28:45.653
The diagnosis is made based on something

2295
01:28:45.653 --> 01:28:49.761
called the hemoglobin A1C, which is a crude,

2296
01:28:49.761 --> 01:28:53.345
but not horrible way to estimate the backwards

2297
01:28:53.345 --> 01:28:55.553
looking average blood glucose of an individual.

2298
01:28:55.553 --> 01:28:59.250
So if a person's hemoglobin A1C is 5.0%,

2299
01:28:59.250 --> 01:29:01.137
that tells you,

2300
01:29:01.137 --> 01:29:02.614
that's giving you an estimate of how much glucose

2301
01:29:02.614 --> 01:29:04.128
has stuck to their red blood cells,

2302
01:29:04.128 --> 01:29:05.469
that tells you over the past three months,

2303
01:29:05.469 --> 01:29:09.357
their average glucose was about 98 milligrams per deciliter,

2304
01:29:09.357 --> 01:29:10.242
which is excellent.

2305
01:29:10.242 --> 01:29:12.067
That's your average blood glucose

2306
01:29:12.067 --> 01:29:14.460
over all your ins and outs.

2307
01:29:14.460 --> 01:29:18.976
Once that average, once that hemoglobin A1C reaches 6.5%,

2308
01:29:18.976 --> 01:29:20.666
that tells us that the estimated

2309
01:29:20.666 --> 01:29:22.169
looking back over the past three month

2310
01:29:22.169 --> 01:29:25.644
average glucose is 140 milligrams per deciliter.

2311
01:29:25.644 --> 01:29:28.732
Now you have type two diabetes, congratulations.

2312
01:29:28.732 --> 01:29:31.222
Now, of course, to me this is a continuum.

2313
01:29:31.222 --> 01:29:33.506
This is another example of where I think it makes

2314
01:29:33.506 --> 01:29:36.197
no sense to wait 'til someone has type two diabetes

2315
01:29:36.197 --> 01:29:37.780
to sound the alarm.

2316
01:29:38.717 --> 01:29:40.480
So in our practice.

2317
01:29:40.480 --> 01:29:41.313
I'll back up for a second.

2318
01:29:41.313 --> 01:29:43.986
So then the question became,

2319
01:29:43.986 --> 01:29:46.608
what do we know about the difference between having a blood

2320
01:29:46.608 --> 01:29:51.025
sugar that averages 100 versus one that averages 130,

2321
01:29:52.273 --> 01:29:54.800
neither of those people have type two diabetes?

2322
01:29:54.800 --> 01:29:56.686
Is there a difference?

2323
01:29:56.686 --> 01:29:58.026
And I think if you look at the data,

2324
01:29:58.026 --> 01:29:59.594
the answer is unambiguously clear.

2325
01:29:59.594 --> 01:30:00.927
Lower is better.

2326
01:30:01.800 --> 01:30:06.800
The next question became what can be said about variability.

2327
01:30:09.152 --> 01:30:11.976
So if, I don't know if the levels one does this,

2328
01:30:11.976 --> 01:30:14.597
but on the Dexcom meter, which is the one we prefer,

2329
01:30:14.597 --> 01:30:17.309
just because you can calibrate it and it's more accurate.

2330
01:30:17.309 --> 01:30:20.143
Is that the one that's with Libre?

2331
01:30:20.143 --> 01:30:22.172
No. Libre, I think is the Abbott one,

2332
01:30:22.172 --> 01:30:23.589
which I'm not particularly fond of.

2333
01:30:23.589 --> 01:30:24.422
Okay.

2334
01:30:24.422 --> 01:30:26.017
Because you can't calibrate it.

2335
01:30:26.017 --> 01:30:28.902
I just think it's a less accurate meter,

2336
01:30:28.902 --> 01:30:30.268
but the next thing you can look at

2337
01:30:30.268 --> 01:30:31.956
is not just your average glucose,

2338
01:30:31.956 --> 01:30:33.419
but you can look at the standard deviation.

2339
01:30:33.419 --> 01:30:36.345
So you can look back and say over the past day,

2340
01:30:36.345 --> 01:30:38.748
the past week, the past month, the past 90 days,

2341
01:30:38.748 --> 01:30:40.925
how much variability existed?

2342
01:30:40.925 --> 01:30:42.695
And so then again, we looked at that question,

2343
01:30:42.695 --> 01:30:44.329
which is can the literature tell us

2344
01:30:44.329 --> 01:30:47.496
anything about variability of glucose?

2345
01:30:48.462 --> 01:30:50.188
And the answer appears to be yes,

2346
01:30:50.188 --> 01:30:52.438
less variability is better.

2347
01:30:53.343 --> 01:30:54.350
And then the final question,

2348
01:30:54.350 --> 01:30:56.173
which is probably the hardest to ask,

2349
01:30:56.173 --> 01:30:58.828
and I don't think we know an answer to this is,

2350
01:30:58.828 --> 01:31:02.045
are there downsides of transient spikes.

2351
01:31:02.045 --> 01:31:04.727
Now, a spike is inevitable.

2352
01:31:04.727 --> 01:31:06.992
Meaning anytime you eat glucose

2353
01:31:06.992 --> 01:31:08.492
or any form of carbohydrate,

2354
01:31:08.492 --> 01:31:10.883
your glucose has to go up.

2355
01:31:10.883 --> 01:31:14.525
So the question is, does it matter how high it goes?

2356
01:31:14.525 --> 01:31:15.807
I don't think we know the answer

2357
01:31:15.807 --> 01:31:18.224
to that question, truthfully.

2358
01:31:20.596 --> 01:31:23.407
So, we've basically said, look,

2359
01:31:23.407 --> 01:31:25.487
what if we could just arbitrarily say,

2360
01:31:25.487 --> 01:31:28.766
keeping your spikes below 140 to 150,

2361
01:31:28.766 --> 01:31:30.412
is better than keeping them below 200,

2362
01:31:30.412 --> 01:31:32.562
because the reality of it is,

2363
01:31:32.562 --> 01:31:34.589
I'm not really aware of a scenario,

2364
01:31:34.589 --> 01:31:36.452
whereby you eat good food and your glucose

2365
01:31:36.452 --> 01:31:39.796
goes to 200, but I'm aware of how to make,

2366
01:31:39.796 --> 01:31:41.828
I could make my glucose go to 200 like that if you gave me

2367
01:31:41.828 --> 01:31:43.552
enough Raisinettes,

2368
01:31:43.552 --> 01:31:47.359
but I can't really make it happen by eating beans.

2369
01:31:47.359 --> 01:31:51.192
And is that more important or less important

2370
01:31:52.194 --> 01:31:54.456
than how quickly it comes back down to baseline?

2371
01:31:54.456 --> 01:31:57.381
I think of the three metrics that we care about,

2372
01:31:57.381 --> 01:32:01.165
average glucose, variability of glucose

2373
01:32:01.165 --> 01:32:03.603
and just the number or frequency of spikes,

2374
01:32:03.603 --> 01:32:04.925
I would say that the frequency of spikes

2375
01:32:04.925 --> 01:32:06.718
is probably the least important.

2376
01:32:06.718 --> 01:32:07.761
In other words,

2377
01:32:07.761 --> 01:32:11.058
I don't think there's any physiologic harm

2378
01:32:11.058 --> 01:32:14.452
that comes from a transient spike to 200 by itself.

2379
01:32:14.452 --> 01:32:16.045
So if I could just sort of robotically say

2380
01:32:16.045 --> 01:32:17.380
your glucose went to 200

2381
01:32:17.380 --> 01:32:19.080
and then it came back to a hundred in an hour.

2382
01:32:19.080 --> 01:32:19.913
Fine.

2383
01:32:19.913 --> 01:32:21.672
The reality is it doesn't do that though.

2384
01:32:21.672 --> 01:32:23.907
What happens is if it goes to 200, it's gonna come,

2385
01:32:23.907 --> 01:32:25.136
you're gonna overshoot,

2386
01:32:25.136 --> 01:32:26.855
'cause you're gonna have secreted a pound

2387
01:32:26.855 --> 01:32:28.603
of insulin into your circulation.

2388
01:32:28.603 --> 01:32:29.436
When it comes down,

2389
01:32:29.436 --> 01:32:30.269
it doesn't stop at a hundred.

2390
01:32:30.269 --> 01:32:32.168
Yeah, it's gonna go to 40 probably not 40,

2391
01:32:32.168 --> 01:32:34.483
probably go to 60.

2392
01:32:34.483 --> 01:32:36.295
And what's the effect of that on your appetite.

2393
01:32:36.295 --> 01:32:38.971
What's the effect of that on the bounce back.

2394
01:32:38.971 --> 01:32:41.363
And that's why in my experience,

2395
01:32:41.363 --> 01:32:43.325
and this is just clinical experience,

2396
01:32:43.325 --> 01:32:47.046
just banging away with patients over the last six years,

2397
01:32:47.046 --> 01:32:50.349
when we suggest to patients that our metric

2398
01:32:50.349 --> 01:32:53.278
is avoid spikes over 140 to 150,

2399
01:32:53.278 --> 01:32:55.441
try to keep your average at a hundred,

2400
01:32:55.441 --> 01:32:58.451
try to keep your standard deviation below 15.

2401
01:32:58.451 --> 01:33:00.028
Eat around that.

2402
01:33:00.028 --> 01:33:04.350
Let that determine your carbohydrate tolerance.

2403
01:33:04.350 --> 01:33:06.152
People just do really, really well.

2404
01:33:06.152 --> 01:33:06.985
Right, right, right.

2405
01:33:06.985 --> 01:33:09.199
So this is very empirical

2406
01:33:09.199 --> 01:33:10.197
and everyone will have a different

2407
01:33:10.197 --> 01:33:11.919
carbohydrate tolerance, right?

2408
01:33:11.919 --> 01:33:13.636
It's a function of your sleep, your stress,

2409
01:33:13.636 --> 01:33:15.423
your metabolic health, your exercise.

2410
01:33:15.423 --> 01:33:17.816
It is interesting how much sleep comes into play.

2411
01:33:17.816 --> 01:33:19.715
Like if I don't sleep well.

2412
01:33:19.715 --> 01:33:21.163
Oh, your numbers are horrible.

2413
01:33:21.163 --> 01:33:22.024
It's terrible.
Yeah.

2414
01:33:22.024 --> 01:33:22.857
All over the map.

2415
01:33:22.857 --> 01:33:26.408
And I thought, well, is that because of the poor sleep

2416
01:33:26.408 --> 01:33:31.325
or is the lack of stability contributing to the poor sleep?

2417
01:33:33.156 --> 01:33:36.952
If you look at the experimental data,

2418
01:33:36.952 --> 01:33:38.479
I'm trying to blank out a name.

2419
01:33:38.479 --> 01:33:40.812
I think it's Eve van Cauter,

2420
01:33:41.833 --> 01:33:43.516
she's a researcher at the University of Chicago.

2421
01:33:43.516 --> 01:33:45.262
Did a pretty elegant experiment.

2422
01:33:45.262 --> 01:33:47.732
Took a group of people and I'm probably gonna get

2423
01:33:47.732 --> 01:33:48.713
a few of these details wrong.

2424
01:33:48.713 --> 01:33:51.038
'Cause it's been a few reason since I looked at it.

2425
01:33:51.038 --> 01:33:53.215
So did what's called a Euglycemic clamp

2426
01:33:53.215 --> 01:33:54.976
So this is the gold standard

2427
01:33:54.976 --> 01:33:57.332
for measuring glucose, disposal and insulin sensitivity.

2428
01:33:57.332 --> 01:34:00.174
So people are run with a line

2429
01:34:00.174 --> 01:34:02.507
that runs insulin and glucose into them.

2430
01:34:02.507 --> 01:34:04.708
And they're given glucose and insulin

2431
01:34:04.708 --> 01:34:06.936
such that they have to maintain a Euglycemic

2432
01:34:06.936 --> 01:34:09.360
or normal glycemic response.

2433
01:34:09.360 --> 01:34:11.527
So there's just no blips on a flat line.

2434
01:34:11.527 --> 01:34:13.077
Right, and then the question

2435
01:34:13.077 --> 01:34:14.355
is how much insulin do you require to do that?

2436
01:34:14.355 --> 01:34:15.821
So that now tells you exactly

2437
01:34:15.821 --> 01:34:18.374
how insulin sensitive a person is.

2438
01:34:18.374 --> 01:34:20.871
And this is pinpoint accuracy

2439
01:34:20.871 --> 01:34:21.911
because that's basically telling

2440
01:34:21.911 --> 01:34:23.538
you in real time,

2441
01:34:23.538 --> 01:34:26.137
how effective are this person's muscles

2442
01:34:26.137 --> 01:34:29.135
at sensing insulin and taking glucose out of circulation,

2443
01:34:29.135 --> 01:34:31.511
'cause your muscles are what take your glucose out

2444
01:34:31.511 --> 01:34:34.173
of circulation in the short run.

2445
01:34:34.173 --> 01:34:38.494
So you do this test on people and then they were sleep,

2446
01:34:38.494 --> 01:34:40.175
it was a crossover group.

2447
01:34:40.175 --> 01:34:41.989
So a control and then a crossover.

2448
01:34:41.989 --> 01:34:44.348
And the intervention was,

2449
01:34:44.348 --> 01:34:47.941
I wanna say two weeks of sleep deprivation

2450
01:34:47.941 --> 01:34:51.046
to four hours a night, which by the way,

2451
01:34:51.046 --> 01:34:53.225
like that was my residency for five years.

2452
01:34:53.225 --> 01:34:56.418
So that's not an unreasonable intervention.

2453
01:34:56.418 --> 01:34:58.525
And then repeat the glycemic clamp

2454
01:34:58.525 --> 01:35:01.102
and glucose disposal fell by 50%.

2455
01:35:01.102 --> 01:35:01.935
Wow.

2456
01:35:03.152 --> 01:35:07.235
So in two weeks of admittedly really bad sleep,

2457
01:35:08.161 --> 01:35:10.526
you're taking somebody halfway to diabetes.

2458
01:35:10.526 --> 01:35:13.143
So now imagine a person whose sleep

2459
01:35:13.143 --> 01:35:14.770
is compromised for years.

2460
01:35:14.770 --> 01:35:17.950
So that's why going back to what we said at the outset,

2461
01:35:17.950 --> 01:35:20.747
if you're not looking at a person's sleep

2462
01:35:20.747 --> 01:35:22.367
with real seriousness,

2463
01:35:22.367 --> 01:35:23.998
before you try to address their metabolic health,

2464
01:35:23.998 --> 01:35:27.488
you could be missing a wolf standing at your door.

2465
01:35:27.488 --> 01:35:29.042
But the good news, of course,

2466
01:35:29.042 --> 01:35:33.046
being this malleability piece that it can be corrected.

2467
01:35:33.046 --> 01:35:36.463
And you know, in the context of a culture

2468
01:35:37.309 --> 01:35:39.900
where it appears that by 2030,

2469
01:35:39.900 --> 01:35:41.606
something like 50% of Americans are gonna be either

2470
01:35:41.606 --> 01:35:44.433
prediabetic diabetic or diabetic,

2471
01:35:44.433 --> 01:35:46.158
is this not the most important thing

2472
01:35:46.158 --> 01:35:49.339
to be thinking about tracking and talking about?

2473
01:35:49.339 --> 01:35:51.754
Yeah, look, I've gotten into some real battles

2474
01:35:51.754 --> 01:35:53.814
with people over this 'cause I've

2475
01:35:53.814 --> 01:35:56.320
been a very vocal advocate for the use of continuous glucose

2476
01:35:56.320 --> 01:35:59.791
monitors and everything from trolls on Twitter.

2477
01:35:59.791 --> 01:36:01.870
There's a weird Twitter war going on over this,

2478
01:36:01.870 --> 01:36:03.977
which is confusing to me.

2479
01:36:03.977 --> 01:36:06.823
Yeah, and there was an article written in Gamma Health

2480
01:36:06.823 --> 01:36:08.772
a year ago that came down on the side

2481
01:36:08.772 --> 01:36:10.703
of there's no role for using

2482
01:36:10.703 --> 01:36:13.860
continuous glucose monitors in non-diabetics,

2483
01:36:13.860 --> 01:36:16.360
and again, I think people are not understanding

2484
01:36:16.360 --> 01:36:18.405
how to use these things clinically.

2485
01:36:18.405 --> 01:36:20.028
Right, I mean that's part of why I'm asking.

2486
01:36:20.028 --> 01:36:24.069
I do think that there is a lot of room that remains

2487
01:36:24.069 --> 01:36:25.711
in the education space

2488
01:36:25.711 --> 01:36:27.509
to help people like myself understand

2489
01:36:27.509 --> 01:36:28.397
how to interpret the data.

2490
01:36:28.397 --> 01:36:31.664
Yeah, and in that sense, I'm actually empathetic.

2491
01:36:31.664 --> 01:36:35.235
And I understand why saying like let's slow down a little

2492
01:36:35.235 --> 01:36:36.557
bit and let's make sure,

2493
01:36:36.557 --> 01:36:38.377
'cause again, I think our patients

2494
01:36:38.377 --> 01:36:40.336
have a very different experience.

2495
01:36:40.336 --> 01:36:41.473
I've been using one of these things

2496
01:36:41.473 --> 01:36:45.652
since 2015 on myself, almost continuously.

2497
01:36:45.652 --> 01:36:47.924
You can imagine how many reps I have

2498
01:36:47.924 --> 01:36:49.079
and by extension our patients.

2499
01:36:49.079 --> 01:36:53.415
And so we know how to use this data to do two things,

2500
01:36:53.415 --> 01:36:56.359
one to gain insights into how to change,

2501
01:36:56.359 --> 01:36:58.290
people's eating and two,

2502
01:36:58.290 --> 01:37:01.188
to use it as a behavior tool

2503
01:37:01.188 --> 01:37:03.446
to then create compliance over time.

2504
01:37:03.446 --> 01:37:04.560
Those are two different things,

2505
01:37:04.560 --> 01:37:07.060
but they're equally valuable.

2506
01:37:07.060 --> 01:37:08.208
I don't, sorry, go ahead.

2507
01:37:08.208 --> 01:37:10.450
Oh no, I was gonna say, but that said,

2508
01:37:10.450 --> 01:37:12.339
I don't think that that experience is shared

2509
01:37:12.339 --> 01:37:13.172
by somebody who just goes

2510
01:37:13.172 --> 01:37:14.465
and order one of these things online.

2511
01:37:14.465 --> 01:37:16.695
So again, I can understand why it can create

2512
01:37:16.695 --> 01:37:17.797
a lot of confusion for people

2513
01:37:17.797 --> 01:37:19.476
if they don't have that insight.

2514
01:37:19.476 --> 01:37:21.719
Yeah, I mean, it does.

2515
01:37:21.719 --> 01:37:23.119
There's a weird psychological thing

2516
01:37:23.119 --> 01:37:24.392
that happens 'cause you're

2517
01:37:24.392 --> 01:37:26.935
essentially gamifying your nutrition, right?

2518
01:37:26.935 --> 01:37:28.853
And so you look at this,

2519
01:37:28.853 --> 01:37:32.173
this interface and you see where you're supposed to be

2520
01:37:32.173 --> 01:37:33.950
and you ate this thing and you're out of it

2521
01:37:33.950 --> 01:37:35.451
and you're like, you make a metal note.

2522
01:37:35.451 --> 01:37:37.167
Okay, I won't eat that thing.

2523
01:37:37.167 --> 01:37:39.640
And then it's become like, how flat can I make my curve?

2524
01:37:39.640 --> 01:37:40.839
And like how,

2525
01:37:40.839 --> 01:37:44.056
but there is an engagement there that I can't imagine

2526
01:37:44.056 --> 01:37:44.944
is a negative thing.

2527
01:37:44.944 --> 01:37:48.890
Like you are connected to what's happening in your body

2528
01:37:48.890 --> 01:37:52.605
in real time and with the appropriate amount of education.

2529
01:37:52.605 --> 01:37:54.768
But the flip side is,

2530
01:37:54.768 --> 01:37:56.325
and this is a fair criticism.

2531
01:37:56.325 --> 01:37:57.662
So I think when I,

2532
01:37:57.662 --> 01:38:02.156
I wrote a piece on this a little over a year ago

2533
01:38:02.156 --> 01:38:03.920
and one of the people who criticized me,

2534
01:38:03.920 --> 01:38:05.211
who I've since engaged with quite a bit

2535
01:38:05.211 --> 01:38:07.074
and I think he made a fair point,

2536
01:38:07.074 --> 01:38:10.211
which I could have been more explicit

2537
01:38:10.211 --> 01:38:11.900
and should have been,

2538
01:38:11.900 --> 01:38:15.331
which is if a person looks at their CGM data in isolation,

2539
01:38:15.331 --> 01:38:16.865
they can still make a mistake.

2540
01:38:16.865 --> 01:38:18.969
So I'll use an analogy that I,

2541
01:38:18.969 --> 01:38:20.098
I don't know if I've since gone back

2542
01:38:20.098 --> 01:38:21.069
and rewritten about this,

2543
01:38:21.069 --> 01:38:22.909
but I've certainly talked about it.

2544
01:38:22.909 --> 01:38:24.343
If I said to you Rich,

2545
01:38:24.343 --> 01:38:27.775
the only metric that matters in life

2546
01:38:27.775 --> 01:38:29.473
is the number on the scale.

2547
01:38:29.473 --> 01:38:31.650
And lower is better.

2548
01:38:31.650 --> 01:38:34.400
If I program that into your mind,

2549
01:38:35.657 --> 01:38:37.544
there's no reason you wouldn't take up smoking.

2550
01:38:37.544 --> 01:38:40.787
You're probably gonna lose weight, right?

2551
01:38:40.787 --> 01:38:42.120
So in isolation,

2552
01:38:42.978 --> 01:38:45.980
maniacally focusing on one metric

2553
01:38:45.980 --> 01:38:48.019
can easily lead you to do bad things

2554
01:38:48.019 --> 01:38:51.269
along the way and this is no exception.

2555
01:38:53.398 --> 01:38:54.791
So, I think the glib example is,

2556
01:38:54.791 --> 01:38:56.090
well, if I ate,

2557
01:38:56.090 --> 01:38:58.691
if I literally ate nothing but bacon every single day.

2558
01:38:58.691 --> 01:39:01.501
Just nothing but fat, like you'll keep your.

2559
01:39:01.501 --> 01:39:02.981
You'll keep your glucose low.

2560
01:39:02.981 --> 01:39:04.551
I'm not convinced that that's an ideal diet

2561
01:39:04.551 --> 01:39:06.613
Right, sure, sure.

2562
01:39:06.613 --> 01:39:09.777
So that's why I think I would say this has to be viewed

2563
01:39:09.777 --> 01:39:11.577
in the context of everything that you're doing.

2564
01:39:11.577 --> 01:39:13.817
And there has to be kind of a whole set of common sense

2565
01:39:13.817 --> 01:39:17.308
principles that go into this along with just,

2566
01:39:17.308 --> 01:39:18.288
this is the only number that matters.

2567
01:39:18.288 --> 01:39:20.366
Right, got it.

2568
01:39:20.366 --> 01:39:22.071
Let's segue into zone two.

2569
01:39:22.071 --> 01:39:24.956
My favorite topic of all time.

2570
01:39:24.956 --> 01:39:27.703
It's so hilarious that like zone two training

2571
01:39:27.703 --> 01:39:29.823
is now like a thing that people love to talk about.

2572
01:39:29.823 --> 01:39:31.667
Like I've been talking about this forever.

2573
01:39:31.667 --> 01:39:33.336
Yeah, Philip Maffetone has been talking

2574
01:39:33.336 --> 01:39:35.161
about it under different names.

2575
01:39:35.161 --> 01:39:38.015
Yeah, so I caught on to the Maffetone method

2576
01:39:38.015 --> 01:39:41.534
at the beginning of my ultra endurance training

2577
01:39:41.534 --> 01:39:44.496
and I really credit adherence,

2578
01:39:44.496 --> 01:39:48.892
strict, strict adherence to that philosophy

2579
01:39:48.892 --> 01:39:50.807
as a vehicle for me,

2580
01:39:50.807 --> 01:39:53.740
making drastic improvements over the course

2581
01:39:53.740 --> 01:39:55.313
of three year period.

2582
01:39:55.313 --> 01:39:58.451
This stuff doesn't happen overnight

2583
01:39:58.451 --> 01:40:00.812
and I'm constantly preaching the benefits of it.

2584
01:40:00.812 --> 01:40:02.637
And it's so cool and interesting

2585
01:40:02.637 --> 01:40:04.743
to see people experiment with it.

2586
01:40:04.743 --> 01:40:08.631
But I think there's also a lot of confusion

2587
01:40:08.631 --> 01:40:12.218
about what it actually is and why it's effective.

2588
01:40:12.218 --> 01:40:16.251
So nobody is better, except for maybe Iñigo.

2589
01:40:16.251 --> 01:40:17.084
Is that how you say his name?

2590
01:40:17.084 --> 01:40:17.917
Iñigo

2591
01:40:17.917 --> 01:40:18.963
Iñigo.
Yeah.

2592
01:40:18.963 --> 01:40:21.491
At talking about this.

2593
01:40:21.491 --> 01:40:24.606
Please allow me to indulge you

2594
01:40:24.606 --> 01:40:28.329
in a conversation about zone two training.

2595
01:40:28.329 --> 01:40:29.817
So I agree.

2596
01:40:29.817 --> 01:40:34.027
I wish there was a better name for it because as you know,

2597
01:40:34.027 --> 01:40:36.178
'cause you've trained with a power meter on a bike.

2598
01:40:36.178 --> 01:40:39.238
If you're a student of cycling power meters,

2599
01:40:39.238 --> 01:40:43.712
we use seven zones when we're training and cycling.

2600
01:40:43.712 --> 01:40:46.185
And so the first thing I say to anybody who's coming to this

2601
01:40:46.185 --> 01:40:48.335
is please forget everything you know

2602
01:40:48.335 --> 01:40:50.256
about your numbers and your zones.

2603
01:40:50.256 --> 01:40:52.819
And this has nothing to do with FTP

2604
01:40:52.819 --> 01:40:56.402
and the sort of Andrew Cogan sort of stuff.

2605
01:40:57.725 --> 01:40:59.345
So it's just an energy system.

2606
01:40:59.345 --> 01:41:02.177
And the way Sam Milan describes it is really through five

2607
01:41:02.177 --> 01:41:05.344
energy systems and zone two is the one

2608
01:41:07.635 --> 01:41:10.252
that is basically defined

2609
01:41:10.252 --> 01:41:14.325
as the maximal level of aerobic output,

2610
01:41:14.325 --> 01:41:17.965
that you can generate while keeping lactate

2611
01:41:17.965 --> 01:41:18.860
below two millimole.

2612
01:41:18.860 --> 01:41:20.855
So you could argue that's an arbitrary definition,

2613
01:41:20.855 --> 01:41:23.063
although physiologically, it turns out not to be.

2614
01:41:23.063 --> 01:41:24.015
So what is lactate?

2615
01:41:24.015 --> 01:41:25.503
We have to kind of explain this,

2616
01:41:25.503 --> 01:41:27.318
I think in some detail for people

2617
01:41:27.318 --> 01:41:28.769
to understand why this matters,

2618
01:41:28.769 --> 01:41:32.712
because even though most people will do zone two,

2619
01:41:32.712 --> 01:41:36.652
without ever measuring their lactate and that's fin,.

2620
01:41:36.652 --> 01:41:38.765
if you want to be really on the money,

2621
01:41:38.765 --> 01:41:40.659
you do need to measure your lactate at least periodically.

2622
01:41:40.659 --> 01:41:42.220
And I do every day.

2623
01:41:42.220 --> 01:41:43.642
I mean I do zone two, four days a week,

2624
01:41:43.642 --> 01:41:44.849
every one of those four days a week,

2625
01:41:44.849 --> 01:41:45.910
I'm measuring my lactate.

2626
01:41:45.910 --> 01:41:49.453
There's plenty of DIY protocols out there

2627
01:41:49.453 --> 01:41:51.921
that vary in terms of efficacy,

2628
01:41:51.921 --> 01:41:54.918
but I'm always encouraging people to get a proper test

2629
01:41:54.918 --> 01:41:57.403
on a bike trainer or on a treadmill

2630
01:41:57.403 --> 01:42:00.875
with a technician who's drawing lactate at those intervals.

2631
01:42:00.875 --> 01:42:03.457
So you can really dial in on it.

2632
01:42:03.457 --> 01:42:05.004
And I wrote about this in my book

2633
01:42:05.004 --> 01:42:07.004
and I explained that my,

2634
01:42:09.739 --> 01:42:12.994
the top of my zone two, was a heart rate

2635
01:42:12.994 --> 01:42:16.129
of like 145, I think when I was running

2636
01:42:16.129 --> 01:42:18.845
and 130 on a bike and then people will tweet me and like,

2637
01:42:18.845 --> 01:42:20.540
I'm keeping my heart rate at 145.

2638
01:42:20.540 --> 01:42:21.731
I'm like, no,

2639
01:42:21.731 --> 01:42:23.448
this is not what I'm saying.

2640
01:42:23.448 --> 01:42:25.539
I expressly said like, this is me.

2641
01:42:25.539 --> 01:42:26.372
Yeah.

2642
01:42:26.372 --> 01:42:27.424
This is not, you,

2643
01:42:27.424 --> 01:42:29.989
You have to get a proper test to really

2644
01:42:29.989 --> 01:42:32.903
understand your own physiology.

2645
01:42:32.903 --> 01:42:35.136
Yeah, so this gets back to your question

2646
01:42:35.136 --> 01:42:37.086
about metabolic health.

2647
01:42:37.086 --> 01:42:39.181
Well, zone two in many ways

2648
01:42:39.181 --> 01:42:40.858
is the most important functional test

2649
01:42:40.858 --> 01:42:45.054
we have for determining a person's metabolic health.

2650
01:42:45.054 --> 01:42:48.311
So I talk to patients about how we have dynamic

2651
01:42:48.311 --> 01:42:50.675
and static tests for determining their health.

2652
01:42:50.675 --> 01:42:53.322
So a static test is just a blood test where tell me what

2653
01:42:53.322 --> 01:42:57.233
your uric acid is, your glucose, your insulin,

2654
01:42:57.233 --> 01:42:59.793
all of these things, your triglycerides.

2655
01:42:59.793 --> 01:43:02.601
A dynamic test is let me give you a bunch of glucose

2656
01:43:02.601 --> 01:43:05.197
right now and draw your blood over time

2657
01:43:05.197 --> 01:43:06.770
and watch what it does.

2658
01:43:06.770 --> 01:43:11.118
Another dynamic test is, let me ask you to do work,

2659
01:43:11.118 --> 01:43:13.666
ride a bike, run on a treadmill,

2660
01:43:13.666 --> 01:43:17.462
and let me measure a metabolic byproduct called lactate.

2661
01:43:17.462 --> 01:43:20.030
And that will tell me an unbelievable amount,

2662
01:43:20.030 --> 01:43:22.236
perhaps more than anything else,

2663
01:43:22.236 --> 01:43:24.324
about how you function

2664
01:43:24.324 --> 01:43:27.683
and this functional test is so important.

2665
01:43:27.683 --> 01:43:30.523
So when our bodies create ATP,

2666
01:43:30.523 --> 01:43:33.566
they have choices for what fuel they're going to use.

2667
01:43:33.566 --> 01:43:34.576
So for all intents and purposes,

2668
01:43:34.576 --> 01:43:39.481
there are two fuels to make ATP, glucose and fat.

2669
01:43:39.481 --> 01:43:42.256
Now with fat, you can only do one thing with it.

2670
01:43:42.256 --> 01:43:46.089
You can only turn fat into two carbon subunits

2671
01:43:47.345 --> 01:43:49.114
that get put into the mitochondria

2672
01:43:49.114 --> 01:43:52.114
where you very efficiently make ATP,

2673
01:43:53.266 --> 01:43:55.295
carbon dioxide and water vapor.

2674
01:43:55.295 --> 01:43:56.896
So that's why when we're,

2675
01:43:56.896 --> 01:43:59.612
so the input is fat and oxygen

2676
01:43:59.612 --> 01:44:01.878
and the output is ATP carbon dioxide water.

2677
01:44:01.878 --> 01:44:04.468
So we're breathing out CO2 and water vapor.

2678
01:44:04.468 --> 01:44:06.592
We're using the ATP.

2679
01:44:06.592 --> 01:44:09.849
We can do the same thing with glucose.

2680
01:44:09.849 --> 01:44:11.556
Glucose can be turned from,

2681
01:44:11.556 --> 01:44:12.805
glucose into pyruvate,

2682
01:44:12.805 --> 01:44:15.150
and pyruvate goes into that same pathway.

2683
01:44:15.150 --> 01:44:17.995
So glucose and fat can both be churned through that super

2684
01:44:17.995 --> 01:44:20.578
efficient pathway to make ATP .

2685
01:44:21.946 --> 01:44:24.863
As the body's demand for ATP grows,

2686
01:44:25.762 --> 01:44:28.529
it's going to start out stripping

2687
01:44:28.529 --> 01:44:29.693
the capacity of the mitochondria,

2688
01:44:29.693 --> 01:44:31.907
depending on the fitness of the individual,

2689
01:44:31.907 --> 01:44:35.382
depending on the efficiency of the furnace.

2690
01:44:35.382 --> 01:44:37.068
And so it does have an escape valve.

2691
01:44:37.068 --> 01:44:40.522
It does have a manner in which it can produce more ATP,

2692
01:44:40.522 --> 01:44:44.919
absent sufficient oxygen which is basically the thing,

2693
01:44:44.919 --> 01:44:48.990
the utilization of oxygen becomes the bottleneck.

2694
01:44:48.990 --> 01:44:51.349
But you can't do it with fat.

2695
01:44:51.349 --> 01:44:53.032
So once you tap out on your fat stores,

2696
01:44:53.032 --> 01:44:56.199
you don't have a way to over spill fat oxidation

2697
01:44:56.199 --> 01:44:57.085
into this other energy source.

2698
01:44:57.085 --> 01:45:00.051
Instead, you turn that glucose into pyruvate

2699
01:45:00.051 --> 01:45:02.481
but now you turn the pyruvate into lactate

2700
01:45:02.481 --> 01:45:05.309
and that does yield a little bit of ATP,

2701
01:45:05.309 --> 01:45:09.490
literally about 1/16th the ATP you would've received,

2702
01:45:09.490 --> 01:45:11.914
if you had run that glucose through the mitochondria.

2703
01:45:11.914 --> 01:45:12.841
So that's a knock on it.

2704
01:45:12.841 --> 01:45:17.841
That's the trade off is you got a fraction of the energy,

2705
01:45:18.083 --> 01:45:20.847
but the bigger trade off is that you're generating lactate,

2706
01:45:20.847 --> 01:45:25.143
which now pairs with hydrogen and creates an acid,

2707
01:45:25.143 --> 01:45:29.146
that acid effectively makes it very difficult

2708
01:45:29.146 --> 01:45:32.829
for actin, myosin filaments to uncouple.

2709
01:45:32.829 --> 01:45:37.257
And if that lactic acid level gets high enough,

2710
01:45:37.257 --> 01:45:39.736
you start to really feel the inability,

2711
01:45:39.736 --> 01:45:41.989
the burn in the muscle that creates the inability

2712
01:45:41.989 --> 01:45:45.212
for you to continue effectively contracting your muscles.

2713
01:45:45.212 --> 01:45:46.795
So anybody who's done an all out effort knows

2714
01:45:46.795 --> 01:45:47.628
exactly what that feels like.

2715
01:45:47.628 --> 01:45:48.461
Sure.

2716
01:45:48.461 --> 01:45:50.766
The other variability being the exhaustibility

2717
01:45:50.766 --> 01:45:52.253
of the energy source, right?

2718
01:45:52.253 --> 01:45:55.728
You have like something like 45 minutes worth of glucose

2719
01:45:55.728 --> 01:45:59.322
output at that higher zone training rate,

2720
01:45:59.322 --> 01:46:01.640
whereas your fat stores

2721
01:46:01.640 --> 01:46:02.824
are essentially inexhaustible.

2722
01:46:02.824 --> 01:46:04.384
That's right.
Right?

2723
01:46:04.384 --> 01:46:08.301
So if you can develop a vast zone two capacity,

2724
01:46:09.790 --> 01:46:13.381
which essentially means being able to be incredibly

2725
01:46:13.381 --> 01:46:17.214
metabolically efficient at a certain activity,

2726
01:46:18.471 --> 01:46:21.491
at a certain level of exertion,

2727
01:46:21.491 --> 01:46:26.085
that in turn creates mitochondrial efficiency,

2728
01:46:26.085 --> 01:46:29.359
mitochondrial density, and exten,

2729
01:46:29.359 --> 01:46:32.630
basically an inexhaustible source of energy

2730
01:46:32.630 --> 01:46:35.413
to propel you through an ultra endurance event

2731
01:46:35.413 --> 01:46:36.529
or an endurance event.

2732
01:46:36.529 --> 01:46:37.672
Yep.

2733
01:46:37.672 --> 01:46:39.548
And so I thought about this a lot,

2734
01:46:39.548 --> 01:46:42.518
obviously when I was swimming,

2735
01:46:42.518 --> 01:46:44.889
because even more so than cycling,

2736
01:46:44.889 --> 01:46:48.722
fueling in really long swims, as you now know,

2737
01:46:49.578 --> 01:46:50.661
it's a grind,

2738
01:46:51.533 --> 01:46:53.543
especially in the ocean where the salt water

2739
01:46:53.543 --> 01:46:55.126
gets in your mouth.

2740
01:46:56.606 --> 01:46:57.978
On one of my Catalina swims,

2741
01:46:57.978 --> 01:47:01.515
I remember it was so choppy that on one breath,

2742
01:47:01.515 --> 01:47:02.348
I turned to breathe,

2743
01:47:02.348 --> 01:47:03.386
it was the middle of the night.

2744
01:47:03.386 --> 01:47:06.649
So you can't see anything and just ingested

2745
01:47:06.649 --> 01:47:07.635
a gallon of salt water.

2746
01:47:07.635 --> 01:47:08.468
Nausea is a given.

2747
01:47:08.468 --> 01:47:11.013
Yeah, you're just puking your brains out.

2748
01:47:11.013 --> 01:47:14.714
So anything you can do to minimize the requirement

2749
01:47:14.714 --> 01:47:16.470
for having to feed the glucose engine,

2750
01:47:16.470 --> 01:47:20.842
especially when you can't really take in a solution

2751
01:47:20.842 --> 01:47:24.122
in excess of about 6% glucose, 60 grams per liter,

2752
01:47:24.122 --> 01:47:26.375
you're very limited at how much glucose you can take in.

2753
01:47:26.375 --> 01:47:28.352
So, absolutely correct.

2754
01:47:28.352 --> 01:47:29.892
Okay, so now the question becomes

2755
01:47:29.892 --> 01:47:32.317
what separates men from the boys.

2756
01:47:32.317 --> 01:47:36.159
Basically when you look at endurance sports,

2757
01:47:36.159 --> 01:47:39.492
one of the most differentiating factors,

2758
01:47:41.484 --> 01:47:45.338
is how high is a person's zone two threshold.

2759
01:47:45.338 --> 01:47:46.957
Let's just use cycling as an example,

2760
01:47:46.957 --> 01:47:49.139
'cause it's just so easy to metric.

2761
01:47:49.139 --> 01:47:51.507
How many watts can a person generate

2762
01:47:51.507 --> 01:47:54.639
per kilo of body weight, if we wanna normalize it,

2763
01:47:54.639 --> 01:47:55.974
while keeping lactate below two.

2764
01:47:55.974 --> 01:47:58.290
And what is it about that lactate below two?

2765
01:47:58.290 --> 01:48:00.803
If lactate stays below two,

2766
01:48:00.803 --> 01:48:03.510
you are at that equilibrium where we know

2767
01:48:03.510 --> 01:48:05.914
that you are in balance.

2768
01:48:05.914 --> 01:48:08.971
You are not net accumulating lactate yet.

2769
01:48:08.971 --> 01:48:10.618
Once lactate gets above two,

2770
01:48:10.618 --> 01:48:12.931
you will begin to net accumulate lactate,

2771
01:48:12.931 --> 01:48:15.423
meaning you will accumulate lactate

2772
01:48:15.423 --> 01:48:17.247
quicker than you can clear it.

2773
01:48:17.247 --> 01:48:20.817
And that now puts you in a finite strategy of activity.

2774
01:48:20.817 --> 01:48:24.191
Now, when you and I did races and stuff like that,

2775
01:48:24.191 --> 01:48:25.024
like when I was in a time trial,

2776
01:48:25.024 --> 01:48:28.841
my lactate was definitely above two.

2777
01:48:28.841 --> 01:48:32.017
I would finish a race at a lactate of 16

2778
01:48:32.017 --> 01:48:34.331
and probably 10 minutes before the end,

2779
01:48:34.331 --> 01:48:36.677
my lactate was eight and 10 minutes before that my lactate

2780
01:48:36.677 --> 01:48:37.674
was probably four.

2781
01:48:37.674 --> 01:48:41.403
I sort of budgeted to blow up at the end of that race.

2782
01:48:41.403 --> 01:48:42.575
But if you said to me, Peter,

2783
01:48:42.575 --> 01:48:47.575
I want you to go out and do a three hour nonstop ride

2784
01:48:47.732 --> 01:48:48.884
at your maximum.

2785
01:48:48.884 --> 01:48:49.883
Like what's the fastest,

2786
01:48:49.883 --> 01:48:52.032
what's the greatest distance you could cover in three hours.

2787
01:48:52.032 --> 01:48:53.617
I have to keep lactate below two.

2788
01:48:53.617 --> 01:48:56.188
In fact, when you look at Kipchoge

2789
01:48:56.188 --> 01:48:58.796
breaking a two hour marathon record, same thing.

2790
01:48:58.796 --> 01:48:59.930
Even though he's running at a speed,

2791
01:48:59.930 --> 01:49:02.561
most of us can't run for a hundred yards,

2792
01:49:02.561 --> 01:49:05.084
for him, his lactate was below two.

2793
01:49:05.084 --> 01:49:05.917
Sure.

2794
01:49:05.917 --> 01:49:07.746
To be able to do that or else he couldn't have done that.

2795
01:49:07.746 --> 01:49:10.981
Yeah, it's interesting that that terminology

2796
01:49:10.981 --> 01:49:13.503
like VO2 Max gets all the attention.

2797
01:49:13.503 --> 01:49:16.181
And in reality it's so much less important

2798
01:49:16.181 --> 01:49:19.276
than what your Z2 maximum is.

2799
01:49:19.276 --> 01:49:21.547
And a question that I don't know,

2800
01:49:21.547 --> 01:49:23.454
that I've ever heard a good answer for,

2801
01:49:23.454 --> 01:49:25.870
that I'm curious about is,

2802
01:49:25.870 --> 01:49:29.828
is the difference between, like, let's say your,

2803
01:49:29.828 --> 01:49:32.689
let's say the top end of your zone too is like,

2804
01:49:32.689 --> 01:49:36.343
I don't know, 250 Watts on a bike.

2805
01:49:36.343 --> 01:49:39.925
What is the difference between an athlete that can maintain

2806
01:49:39.925 --> 01:49:43.342
that 250 Watts at below two at below two,

2807
01:49:46.353 --> 01:49:48.175
for three hours,

2808
01:49:48.175 --> 01:49:49.682
but then taps out,

2809
01:49:49.682 --> 01:49:54.097
versus the athlete that can maintain that for eight hours?

2810
01:49:54.097 --> 01:49:56.522
At some point it starts to become a question

2811
01:49:56.522 --> 01:49:58.298
of like how much fuel can they,

2812
01:49:58.298 --> 01:49:59.515
how much fuel can they,

2813
01:49:59.515 --> 01:50:01.736
'cause you're still, the three eight hour difference,

2814
01:50:01.736 --> 01:50:02.592
you're gonna have to start ingesting

2815
01:50:02.592 --> 01:50:03.440
some fuel at that point.

2816
01:50:03.440 --> 01:50:07.575
So assume, assume, equality across fueling.

2817
01:50:07.575 --> 01:50:09.214
Yeah, then it probably starts to come down

2818
01:50:09.214 --> 01:50:11.401
to muscle fibers and things like that, at eight hours.

2819
01:50:11.401 --> 01:50:12.925
Mitochondrial density.

2820
01:50:12.925 --> 01:50:13.911
I don't know that.

2821
01:50:13.911 --> 01:50:15.380
I don't think I know the answer to that.

2822
01:50:15.380 --> 01:50:19.130
I think what I would say is you're going to,

2823
01:50:22.347 --> 01:50:24.010
because these are fit athletes,

2824
01:50:24.010 --> 01:50:26.071
like their muscles are actually putting out a lot of power.

2825
01:50:26.071 --> 01:50:28.410
250 Watts is a lot of power.

2826
01:50:28.410 --> 01:50:29.851
And so I think you're now starting

2827
01:50:29.851 --> 01:50:34.222
to get into the limitations of actual fiber damage.

2828
01:50:34.222 --> 01:50:36.866
For example, like when people feel sore

2829
01:50:36.866 --> 01:50:38.978
the day after a super intense

2830
01:50:38.978 --> 01:50:40.896
workout where they had that lactate burn,

2831
01:50:40.896 --> 01:50:42.900
I think they erroneously assume that it's the lactate

2832
01:50:42.900 --> 01:50:44.489
that they're feeling, but they're actually not,

2833
01:50:44.489 --> 01:50:47.539
they're feeling the microscopic damage to the muscle fibers.

2834
01:50:47.539 --> 01:50:49.747
So I suspect that that's probably going to be

2835
01:50:49.747 --> 01:50:51.662
the differentiating factor there.

2836
01:50:51.662 --> 01:50:53.393
So it's some amount of muscle

2837
01:50:53.393 --> 01:50:55.596
protein synthesis and some amount

2838
01:50:55.596 --> 01:50:58.125
of kind of strength that probably factors into it as well.

2839
01:50:58.125 --> 01:50:58.958
Yeah.

2840
01:50:58.958 --> 01:51:00.989
Like actual muscular strength.

2841
01:51:00.989 --> 01:51:04.072
I remember when I was training for,

2842
01:51:05.876 --> 01:51:07.486
for my first Ultraman.

2843
01:51:07.486 --> 01:51:09.212
Prior to that, I'd messed around in

2844
01:51:09.212 --> 01:51:11.110
triathlon without a coach.

2845
01:51:11.110 --> 01:51:13.989
Was pretty fit in my own mind.

2846
01:51:13.989 --> 01:51:15.170
Hired a coach.

2847
01:51:15.170 --> 01:51:16.731
He's like, I can't even tell you anything

2848
01:51:16.731 --> 01:51:17.903
until you get a lactate test.

2849
01:51:17.903 --> 01:51:19.844
Like there's no point in us even talking,

2850
01:51:19.844 --> 01:51:20.948
I need you to go do this.

2851
01:51:20.948 --> 01:51:22.010
I didn't know anything about it.

2852
01:51:22.010 --> 01:51:24.281
And I was like, ah, I'll be good.

2853
01:51:24.281 --> 01:51:27.100
Like I go in, that graph was like pathetic.

2854
01:51:27.100 --> 01:51:30.103
I couldn't believe like how quickly

2855
01:51:30.103 --> 01:51:32.237
I would go out of zone two.

2856
01:51:32.237 --> 01:51:35.397
And then my whole program was built upon,

2857
01:51:35.397 --> 01:51:38.891
this church of zone two where literally for the first year,

2858
01:51:38.891 --> 01:51:40.951
I don't think I did a single workout

2859
01:51:40.951 --> 01:51:42.597
that was above zone two.

2860
01:51:42.597 --> 01:51:45.086
How much did your zone two increase

2861
01:51:45.086 --> 01:51:46.701
during that course of the year?

2862
01:51:46.701 --> 01:51:47.951
Unbelievable.

2863
01:51:48.844 --> 01:51:50.990
The improvement was insane.

2864
01:51:50.990 --> 01:51:52.362
Yeah.

2865
01:51:52.362 --> 01:51:53.823
And that's why I became like,

2866
01:51:53.823 --> 01:51:56.375
I'm such an adherent and a believer in this.

2867
01:51:56.375 --> 01:52:01.375
Without doing any threshold or tempo work whatsoever,

2868
01:52:01.596 --> 01:52:03.222
I was able to go from somebody

2869
01:52:03.222 --> 01:52:06.472
who couldn't run faster than 10 minutes per mile,

2870
01:52:06.472 --> 01:52:08.981
without going into zone three,

2871
01:52:08.981 --> 01:52:11.386
to two years later,

2872
01:52:11.386 --> 01:52:15.183
being able to run like seven fifteens in my zone too,

2873
01:52:15.183 --> 01:52:19.746
without any track work, without any threshold work,

2874
01:52:19.746 --> 01:52:21.937
any interval work, whatsoever.

2875
01:52:21.937 --> 01:52:24.768
Just by building that foundation bit by bit.

2876
01:52:24.768 --> 01:52:29.055
And it's all about the ability to come back the next day

2877
01:52:29.055 --> 01:52:30.633
and the next day and the next day.

2878
01:52:30.633 --> 01:52:34.258
And I think there's something about that consistency,

2879
01:52:34.258 --> 01:52:37.296
such that when you develop that robustness

2880
01:52:37.296 --> 01:52:38.923
and you do have an effort,

2881
01:52:38.923 --> 01:52:41.836
let's say you're attacking a hill or whatever,

2882
01:52:41.836 --> 01:52:44.791
and you're way out of your zone too to do so,

2883
01:52:44.791 --> 01:52:47.021
you come back down to that baseline zone too,

2884
01:52:47.021 --> 01:52:49.729
very quickly, as opposed to like,

2885
01:52:49.729 --> 01:52:52.212
being winded for an extended period of time

2886
01:52:52.212 --> 01:52:55.417
and kind of losing pace as a result.

2887
01:52:55.417 --> 01:53:00.417
Yeah, so when we put our patients through CPET testing,

2888
01:53:00.528 --> 01:53:02.119
we have about a four page dashboard

2889
01:53:02.119 --> 01:53:03.744
that we run them through,

2890
01:53:03.744 --> 01:53:06.747
which is much more extensive than just,

2891
01:53:06.747 --> 01:53:08.452
We show you your VO2 Max,

2892
01:53:08.452 --> 01:53:11.067
and we will tell you why that's important,

2893
01:53:11.067 --> 01:53:14.078
but it's also, what is your fat oxidation?

2894
01:53:14.078 --> 01:53:15.768
How many grams per minute of fat,

2895
01:53:15.768 --> 01:53:19.012
can you oxidize and over what power range

2896
01:53:19.012 --> 01:53:20.809
and what is the shape of your curve?

2897
01:53:20.809 --> 01:53:23.689
And we benchmark this against like athletes.

2898
01:53:23.689 --> 01:53:25.565
So Iñigo's given us data, right?

2899
01:53:25.565 --> 01:53:27.877
So here's the guy who won the tour de France.

2900
01:53:27.877 --> 01:53:29.607
Here's a master's athlete.

2901
01:53:29.607 --> 01:53:32.361
Here's somebody with metabolic syndrome.

2902
01:53:32.361 --> 01:53:33.313
And where do you stack up?

2903
01:53:33.313 --> 01:53:36.336
So you can really start to see how you oxidize fat

2904
01:53:36.336 --> 01:53:37.712
and how many watts per kilo you can put out

2905
01:53:37.712 --> 01:53:41.649
at zone two compared to these varying degrees of spectrum.

2906
01:53:41.649 --> 01:53:44.759
So now all of a sudden, I'm not just saying, yeah,

2907
01:53:44.759 --> 01:53:46.240
here's your glucose and here's your insulin

2908
01:53:46.240 --> 01:53:47.762
and here's your triglyceride levels.

2909
01:53:47.762 --> 01:53:49.847
But I think this is a much more important test

2910
01:53:49.847 --> 01:53:50.928
because of its functionality.

2911
01:53:50.928 --> 01:53:52.268
Right.

2912
01:53:52.268 --> 01:53:54.939
It does require a lot of humility though,

2913
01:53:54.939 --> 01:53:57.596
and you have to play the long game with it.

2914
01:53:57.596 --> 01:53:59.429
And most people like,

2915
01:54:00.429 --> 01:54:01.710
what's your two year plan?

2916
01:54:01.710 --> 01:54:02.543
They're like, well,

2917
01:54:02.543 --> 01:54:03.687
I wanna do this race in a month

2918
01:54:03.687 --> 01:54:05.529
or three months or something like that.

2919
01:54:05.529 --> 01:54:07.550
Like you really have to be patient with it.

2920
01:54:07.550 --> 01:54:08.383
Yeah.

2921
01:54:09.814 --> 01:54:11.868
And again, it fits so well

2922
01:54:11.868 --> 01:54:15.118
into the centenarian decathlon because,

2923
01:54:16.423 --> 01:54:17.590
if you can be,

2924
01:54:18.866 --> 01:54:22.180
if you could be in your eighties and by the way,

2925
01:54:22.180 --> 01:54:24.242
it is theoretically possible to be in your eighties

2926
01:54:24.242 --> 01:54:26.544
and still put out two watts per kilo.

2927
01:54:26.544 --> 01:54:30.019
There are examples of people who do this.

2928
01:54:30.019 --> 01:54:33.720
I think Iñigo has coached one guy he's an 81 year old.

2929
01:54:33.720 --> 01:54:36.725
So he's a world champion at Masters 81.

2930
01:54:36.725 --> 01:54:38.404
He's about 2.9 or three watts per kilo.

2931
01:54:38.404 --> 01:54:39.242
Wow.

2932
01:54:39.242 --> 01:54:40.075
Can you imagine that?

2933
01:54:40.075 --> 01:54:42.525
Mm, we had a guy in here recently,

2934
01:54:42.525 --> 01:54:44.411
Mike Fremont, he's a hundred

2935
01:54:44.411 --> 01:54:46.187
and he owns all these world records at half

2936
01:54:46.187 --> 01:54:49.135
marathon and marathon distance running,

2937
01:54:49.135 --> 01:54:50.822
when he was like 90 and 91.

2938
01:54:50.822 --> 01:54:51.874
It's amazing.

2939
01:54:51.874 --> 01:54:52.707
Yeah.

2940
01:54:52.707 --> 01:54:53.900
That he's able to do that.

2941
01:54:53.900 --> 01:54:58.390
Talk a little bit about the importance of polarity training.

2942
01:54:58.390 --> 01:55:00.241
You talked about the different energy systems

2943
01:55:00.241 --> 01:55:02.574
in the context of longevity.

2944
01:55:04.050 --> 01:55:06.699
So this idea of Z2 and Z5

2945
01:55:06.699 --> 01:55:09.301
'cause what I see and I'm sure you see this all the time

2946
01:55:09.301 --> 01:55:11.641
is people who spend the vast majority

2947
01:55:11.641 --> 01:55:13.474
of their training time

2948
01:55:14.569 --> 01:55:15.904
in that gray zone.

2949
01:55:15.904 --> 01:55:17.750
They're not going slow enough to develop their aerobic

2950
01:55:17.750 --> 01:55:21.132
capacity, their mitochondrial efficiency and density,

2951
01:55:21.132 --> 01:55:24.047
and they're not going hard enough to really reap

2952
01:55:24.047 --> 01:55:27.908
the benefits of strength, power, etcetera.

2953
01:55:27.908 --> 01:55:31.146
Yeah, the no man's land, the garbage training.

2954
01:55:31.146 --> 01:55:34.182
It's an important zone, by the way, depending on your sport.

2955
01:55:34.182 --> 01:55:36.035
Like when I was time trialing,

2956
01:55:36.035 --> 01:55:38.630
we did spend a lot of time at three, four crossover,

2957
01:55:38.630 --> 01:55:41.180
'cause that's basically where you were racing

2958
01:55:41.180 --> 01:55:43.165
for a 20K time trial.

2959
01:55:43.165 --> 01:55:44.505
So you had to spend some time there,

2960
01:55:44.505 --> 01:55:47.422
but not too much time there, right?

2961
01:55:48.767 --> 01:55:50.517
I would say 80%,

2962
01:55:51.611 --> 01:55:53.225
I was probably only spending 70%,

2963
01:55:53.225 --> 01:55:54.550
I think in retrospect I should have been spending

2964
01:55:54.550 --> 01:55:55.914
a little bit more time there.

2965
01:55:55.914 --> 01:56:00.883
And at your peak, what was your zone two threshold watts?

2966
01:56:00.883 --> 01:56:01.901
2 75.

2967
01:56:01.901 --> 01:56:03.646
That's pretty good.

2968
01:56:03.646 --> 01:56:04.957
At 75 kilos.

2969
01:56:04.957 --> 01:56:05.790
Yeah.

2970
01:56:05.790 --> 01:56:06.623
So it was okay.

2971
01:56:06.623 --> 01:56:08.202
And for context, for people

2972
01:56:08.202 --> 01:56:09.071
that are listening or watching,

2973
01:56:09.071 --> 01:56:11.143
like a Tour De France rider is probably

2974
01:56:11.143 --> 01:56:13.264
little above 300, somewhere in that range.

2975
01:56:13.264 --> 01:56:15.248
Yeah, but much lighter than me, right?

2976
01:56:15.248 --> 01:56:18.677
So he would be 320 watts zone two.

2977
01:56:18.677 --> 01:56:21.875
So I mean the best of the best would be, yeah, yeah.

2978
01:56:21.875 --> 01:56:23.595
But they'd be 65.

2979
01:56:23.595 --> 01:56:24.529
140 pounds.

2980
01:56:24.529 --> 01:56:26.620
Yeah, exactly.

2981
01:56:26.620 --> 01:56:30.337
So yeah, I was 275 watts for zone two

2982
01:56:30.337 --> 01:56:32.254
at 75 kilos at my best

2983
01:56:37.464 --> 01:56:39.526
but not a very high FTP, right?

2984
01:56:39.526 --> 01:56:42.668
So that was, my FTP was only 310.

2985
01:56:42.668 --> 01:56:45.593
As time trialer, though, that becomes important.

2986
01:56:45.593 --> 01:56:46.604
Yeah, I mean, for me,

2987
01:56:46.604 --> 01:56:48.200
because I was a Merckx time trialist,

2988
01:56:48.200 --> 01:56:51.967
which is such a silly little niche thing.

2989
01:56:51.967 --> 01:56:54.432
It really, the arbitrage on Merckx's time trialing

2990
01:56:54.432 --> 01:56:56.215
became your CDA.

2991
01:56:56.215 --> 01:56:58.255
It's coefficient drag funnels surface area,

2992
01:56:58.255 --> 01:57:00.165
that was my obsession.

2993
01:57:00.165 --> 01:57:01.085
That's why I was so obsessed with it.

2994
01:57:01.085 --> 01:57:04.002
It was like just figuring out like,

2995
01:57:05.389 --> 01:57:06.781
'cause it wasn't so much that my FTP

2996
01:57:06.781 --> 01:57:07.795
wasn't that high,

2997
01:57:07.795 --> 01:57:11.087
it's that I could hold that at an uncomfortable position.

2998
01:57:11.087 --> 01:57:12.068
'Cause in a time trial

2999
01:57:12.068 --> 01:57:13.696
you're in such an compromised position

3000
01:57:13.696 --> 01:57:17.325
from a vascular return standpoint,

3001
01:57:17.325 --> 01:57:21.220
that if I could hold 300 watts in a tuck

3002
01:57:21.220 --> 01:57:23.933
and really minimize my frontal

3003
01:57:23.933 --> 01:57:27.193
surface area and coefficient drag,

3004
01:57:27.193 --> 01:57:28.895
I could go fast enough.

3005
01:57:28.895 --> 01:57:30.437
You're such a geek.

3006
01:57:30.437 --> 01:57:32.471
I can't imagine.

3007
01:57:32.471 --> 01:57:35.549
Every single day I would come home

3008
01:57:35.549 --> 01:57:36.382
from Fiesta Island.

3009
01:57:36.382 --> 01:57:37.858
Yeah.

3010
01:57:37.858 --> 01:57:39.940
And model calculate based on barometric pressure.

3011
01:57:39.940 --> 01:57:40.773
Right.

3012
01:57:40.773 --> 01:57:43.288
Based on density, like what was my CDA today?

3013
01:57:43.288 --> 01:57:44.692
Like did I get down to 0.2, one?

3014
01:57:44.692 --> 01:57:45.860
How do you have time for anything else?

3015
01:57:45.860 --> 01:57:47.415
That's like a job.

3016
01:57:47.415 --> 01:57:48.248
There's a reason I don't do it anymore.

3017
01:57:48.248 --> 01:57:49.498
Yeah, right.

3018
01:57:50.903 --> 01:57:54.799
It was a huge commitment to be able to do that.

3019
01:57:54.799 --> 01:57:58.701
And you know, I made a joke about this once I was like,

3020
01:57:58.701 --> 01:57:59.915
it kind of occurred to me

3021
01:57:59.915 --> 01:58:02.242
like how little anybody in the world cared.

3022
01:58:02.242 --> 01:58:04.911
Like one day I was having dinner with my wife

3023
01:58:04.911 --> 01:58:09.078
and I was like, do you know what my PR is for 20K?

3024
01:58:10.686 --> 01:58:11.553
Just outta curiosity.

3025
01:58:11.553 --> 01:58:13.069
Yeah.

3026
01:58:13.069 --> 01:58:16.766
Rich, she was off by a factor of two,

3027
01:58:16.766 --> 01:58:18.509
like literally couldn't care less.

3028
01:58:18.509 --> 01:58:21.520
And I was like, that's cool, I get it like,

3029
01:58:21.520 --> 01:58:23.951
It's amazing how much I care about this dumb thing

3030
01:58:23.951 --> 01:58:26.542
that no human on the planet cares about, which is fine.

3031
01:58:26.542 --> 01:58:29.198
There are a lot of things to this day I care about.

3032
01:58:29.198 --> 01:58:30.727
I love precision shooting.

3033
01:58:30.727 --> 01:58:32.541
So it's like,

3034
01:58:32.541 --> 01:58:34.935
to be able to shoot a dinner plate a mile away

3035
01:58:34.935 --> 01:58:37.320
is such an important thing to me.

3036
01:58:37.320 --> 01:58:39.525
It serves no functional purpose on this planet.

3037
01:58:39.525 --> 01:58:42.328
Yeah and the F1 stuff.

3038
01:58:42.328 --> 01:58:43.161
Right.

3039
01:58:43.161 --> 01:58:44.125
Which is like,

3040
01:58:44.125 --> 01:58:47.080
we could talk about that later and that's a whole sidetrack,

3041
01:58:47.080 --> 01:58:49.204
but I think, because I'm an engineer,

3042
01:58:49.204 --> 01:58:51.944
I just think engineers like data

3043
01:58:51.944 --> 01:58:54.141
and I really viewed time trialing

3044
01:58:54.141 --> 01:58:56.080
as purely an engineering problem.

3045
01:58:56.080 --> 01:58:56.969
'Cause it's not,

3046
01:58:56.969 --> 01:58:58.401
if you think about other types of bike racing,

3047
01:58:58.401 --> 01:59:00.551
there's strategy involved, there's nuance,

3048
01:59:00.551 --> 01:59:01.977
there's complexity.

3049
01:59:01.977 --> 01:59:03.691
But time trialing is purely an engineering problem.

3050
01:59:03.691 --> 01:59:07.033
Right, the bike is the perfect instrument

3051
01:59:07.033 --> 01:59:08.064
to extract data from.

3052
01:59:08.064 --> 01:59:08.916
A watt is a watt is a watt.

3053
01:59:08.916 --> 01:59:09.749
Yep.

3054
01:59:10.696 --> 01:59:13.779
And basically it becomes this like,

3055
01:59:14.814 --> 01:59:17.214
perfect situation for self experimentation.

3056
01:59:17.214 --> 01:59:18.142
Yeah, so cool.

3057
01:59:18.142 --> 01:59:19.418
It's crazy.

3058
01:59:19.418 --> 01:59:21.818
Which is interesting, I know that you,

3059
01:59:21.818 --> 01:59:24.313
I saw that you posted you were gonna get

3060
01:59:24.313 --> 01:59:26.445
a GPS watch and you kind of put it out on Twitter,

3061
01:59:26.445 --> 01:59:27.446
which watch should I get?

3062
01:59:27.446 --> 01:59:28.279
Oh yeah, yeah, yeah.

3063
01:59:28.279 --> 01:59:29.619
And you made it very clear

3064
01:59:29.619 --> 01:59:32.428
that you didn't want a heart rate strap.

3065
01:59:32.428 --> 01:59:34.357
And I was like, that's very antithetical

3066
01:59:34.357 --> 01:59:36.707
to your whole thing because wrist heart rate is terrible.

3067
01:59:36.707 --> 01:59:37.540
Exactly.

3068
01:59:37.540 --> 01:59:38.373
Would you not?

3069
01:59:38.373 --> 01:59:39.818
You'll love this story, right?

3070
01:59:39.818 --> 01:59:41.396
So the reason I didn't want chest strap

3071
01:59:41.396 --> 01:59:42.796
is because I only want it for rucking

3072
01:59:42.796 --> 01:59:44.163
and when you're rucking,

3073
01:59:44.163 --> 01:59:46.295
you got a 60 pound thing on your back

3074
01:59:46.295 --> 01:59:48.678
and it's uncomfortable to have a chest strap

3075
01:59:48.678 --> 01:59:50.017
when you've got so much weight 'cause you're stacking.

3076
01:59:50.017 --> 01:59:51.254
But there's nothing comfortable about it.

3077
01:59:51.254 --> 01:59:52.648
How much more uncomfortable are you gonna be?

3078
01:59:52.648 --> 01:59:53.646
Fair point.

3079
01:59:53.646 --> 01:59:55.249
To get that pristine data.

3080
01:59:55.249 --> 01:59:57.968
But it was also like, I didn't want one more dumb thing.

3081
01:59:57.968 --> 01:59:58.801
Right.

3082
01:59:58.801 --> 01:59:59.659
So what did I do?

3083
01:59:59.659 --> 02:00:01.170
I ended up buying four of these watches

3084
02:00:01.170 --> 02:00:04.347
and keep cycling through them only to realize

3085
02:00:04.347 --> 02:00:06.232
that wrist based heart rate sucks.

3086
02:00:06.232 --> 02:00:07.065
It's terrible.

3087
02:00:07.065 --> 02:00:08.301
It doesn't matter which brand you're wearing.

3088
02:00:08.301 --> 02:00:09.217
Sucks.

3089
02:00:09.217 --> 02:00:10.444
It's just the technology.

3090
02:00:10.444 --> 02:00:11.737
So I returned them all

3091
02:00:11.737 --> 02:00:13.445
and got the cheapest one money could buy,

3092
02:00:13.445 --> 02:00:15.155
the Coros Pace 2 for 200 bucks.

3093
02:00:15.155 --> 02:00:16.202
That's a good watch though.

3094
02:00:16.202 --> 02:00:17.035
It's fine.

3095
02:00:17.035 --> 02:00:17.868
And then I got,

3096
02:00:17.868 --> 02:00:20.543
I use on the bike, I use a WAHOO

3097
02:00:20.543 --> 02:00:22.063
arm band and that's so that's what I use now.

3098
02:00:22.063 --> 02:00:22.896
Right.

3099
02:00:22.896 --> 02:00:25.356
So now I exercise with the WAHOO arm band and this watch.

3100
02:00:25.356 --> 02:00:26.961
So I defaulted into what everybody told me

3101
02:00:26.961 --> 02:00:27.939
from the beginning.

3102
02:00:27.939 --> 02:00:28.964
Got it, alright.

3103
02:00:28.964 --> 02:00:30.996
Well we're starting to fray at the edges here,

3104
02:00:30.996 --> 02:00:34.033
but let's kind of put a pin on the zone two,

3105
02:00:34.033 --> 02:00:35.408
zone five thing in terms of what?

3106
02:00:35.408 --> 02:00:36.548
Well, I didn't answer your question actually.

3107
02:00:36.548 --> 02:00:37.656
Yeah, we're still in the.

3108
02:00:37.656 --> 02:00:40.683
Yeah, so I would say if you're,

3109
02:00:40.683 --> 02:00:42.597
if you're training for a specific sport,

3110
02:00:42.597 --> 02:00:44.978
you're gonna have to understand what zone you need to be in

3111
02:00:44.978 --> 02:00:45.939
and spend the appropriate time there.

3112
02:00:45.939 --> 02:00:48.856
But if you're talking about health,

3113
02:00:49.842 --> 02:00:53.592
I think you're talking 90% is zone 2,

3114
02:00:53.592 --> 02:00:54.889
90% to 95%,

3115
02:00:54.889 --> 02:00:56.432
5% to 10% is zone five.

3116
02:00:56.432 --> 02:00:57.841
That's about it.

3117
02:00:57.841 --> 02:01:00.908
So I do one VO2 Max workout a week, that's it.

3118
02:01:00.908 --> 02:01:02.401
Yeah.

3119
02:01:02.401 --> 02:01:04.537
And you're constantly pricking yourself

3120
02:01:04.537 --> 02:01:06.255
and figuring out where your zones are,

3121
02:01:06.255 --> 02:01:07.743
but for the person who's listening,

3122
02:01:07.743 --> 02:01:10.316
who might not have access to a lab

3123
02:01:10.316 --> 02:01:12.946
where they can go and actually do this properly.

3124
02:01:12.946 --> 02:01:14.345
The point of kerolactate device

3125
02:01:14.345 --> 02:01:16.482
is all you really need, right.

3126
02:01:16.482 --> 02:01:17.315
I use something called,

3127
02:01:17.315 --> 02:01:19.054
I think it's called the lactate plus.

3128
02:01:19.054 --> 02:01:20.733
So you can buy these things online.

3129
02:01:20.733 --> 02:01:21.566
They're not cheap.

3130
02:01:21.566 --> 02:01:23.913
The device is probably $200, $300

3131
02:01:23.913 --> 02:01:27.720
and the strips are probably two bucks a piece, so it's.

3132
02:01:27.720 --> 02:01:29.402
But how do you administer the protocol

3133
02:01:29.402 --> 02:01:30.935
when you're actually doing the thing?

3134
02:01:30.935 --> 02:01:33.177
Well, remember zone two, you're not dying, right?

3135
02:01:33.177 --> 02:01:34.885
But you still have to go through all the zones

3136
02:01:34.885 --> 02:01:36.282
to establish.

3137
02:01:36.282 --> 02:01:38.702
Yeah, so it's empirical.

3138
02:01:38.702 --> 02:01:40.377
The first few times I did it,

3139
02:01:40.377 --> 02:01:42.122
it took me a while to be like,

3140
02:01:42.122 --> 02:01:43.390
oh, I'm well below, I'm well below.

3141
02:01:43.390 --> 02:01:44.490
Whoops, I overshot.

3142
02:01:44.490 --> 02:01:45.578
Okay, back off.

3143
02:01:45.578 --> 02:01:47.850
So I would just have a table next to me, set up.

3144
02:01:47.850 --> 02:01:50.714
And the biggest challenge actually is that you have to have

3145
02:01:50.714 --> 02:01:52.998
soapy rags there 'cause lactate

3146
02:01:52.998 --> 02:01:55.260
doesn't wash off with alcohol.

3147
02:01:55.260 --> 02:01:56.356
So you'll contaminate the whole thing

3148
02:01:56.356 --> 02:01:58.129
if your hands aren't clean.

3149
02:01:58.129 --> 02:01:59.278
Nobody's gonna do this.

3150
02:01:59.278 --> 02:02:02.778
I know you do, but I mean hit the protocol

3151
02:02:03.659 --> 02:02:05.042
that I've typically done in a lab and maybe it's different

3152
02:02:05.042 --> 02:02:06.785
from yours is you get on a bike,

3153
02:02:06.785 --> 02:02:09.487
you warm up for a while at maybe a hundred watts.

3154
02:02:09.487 --> 02:02:13.267
And then you basically launch into this interval workout.

3155
02:02:13.267 --> 02:02:14.396
Yeah, yeah.

3156
02:02:14.396 --> 02:02:15.446
Every four minutes you increase the wattage,

3157
02:02:15.446 --> 02:02:16.607
like 10 Watts.

3158
02:02:16.607 --> 02:02:18.610
So we do longer intervals

3159
02:02:18.610 --> 02:02:20.725
because of the lag between lactate

3160
02:02:20.725 --> 02:02:22.537
and the plasma and in the interstitial.

3161
02:02:22.537 --> 02:02:23.537
So we'll do,

3162
02:02:25.267 --> 02:02:29.016
we'll do six to eight minute intervals and yeah,

3163
02:02:29.016 --> 02:02:30.534
we'll just have somebody,

3164
02:02:30.534 --> 02:02:32.344
when our patients go and do the testing,

3165
02:02:32.344 --> 02:02:33.687
it's a prick to the ear to get the lactate.

3166
02:02:33.687 --> 02:02:34.545
Right.

3167
02:02:34.545 --> 02:02:35.648
But here's the other thing.

3168
02:02:35.648 --> 02:02:38.832
So it's heart rate, power, lactate and perceived effort.

3169
02:02:38.832 --> 02:02:40.719
Right, so we try to triangulate on those.

3170
02:02:40.719 --> 02:02:42.738
So if I'm telling a patient to do this at home,

3171
02:02:42.738 --> 02:02:45.344
what I'm basically saying is here's where

3172
02:02:45.344 --> 02:02:46.753
I think you're going to be.

3173
02:02:46.753 --> 02:02:47.962
So the first thing I'm asking

3174
02:02:47.962 --> 02:02:49.524
is what's your resting lactate.

3175
02:02:49.524 --> 02:02:51.219
Tell me that.

3176
02:02:51.219 --> 02:02:53.225
So when you wake up and you haven't eaten anything,

3177
02:02:53.225 --> 02:02:54.605
what's your lactate.

3178
02:02:54.605 --> 02:02:56.467
If that's two,

3179
02:02:56.467 --> 02:02:57.619
then you're metabolically so unhealthy.

3180
02:02:57.619 --> 02:02:58.747
That's screwed.

3181
02:02:58.747 --> 02:03:00.914
Then we're not doing lactate testing in you.

3182
02:03:00.914 --> 02:03:03.334
We're gonna do it all off RPE, purely.

3183
02:03:03.334 --> 02:03:04.167
That's it.

3184
02:03:05.174 --> 02:03:07.007
But let's assume you tell me,

3185
02:03:07.007 --> 02:03:07.840
oh, my morning, lactate is one.

3186
02:03:07.840 --> 02:03:08.673
Great.

3187
02:03:08.673 --> 02:03:10.039
Okay, so then I'm gonna say,

3188
02:03:10.039 --> 02:03:12.061
do you know your actual resting heart, pardon me,

3189
02:03:12.061 --> 02:03:14.270
your actual max heart rate, not predicted,

3190
02:03:14.270 --> 02:03:16.476
but truly achieved.

3191
02:03:16.476 --> 02:03:17.870
So you tell me that,

3192
02:03:17.870 --> 02:03:20.832
then I figure out how fit you are just by a few questions.

3193
02:03:20.832 --> 02:03:24.212
The fitter you are typically the higher a percentage is.

3194
02:03:24.212 --> 02:03:26.559
So your zone two can be as high as 84%, 85% ,

3195
02:03:26.559 --> 02:03:28.581
of your max heart rate if you're really fit.

3196
02:03:28.581 --> 02:03:31.238
If you're not, it's probably closer to 75%.

3197
02:03:31.238 --> 02:03:32.767
So then I tell them, okay, your heart,

3198
02:03:32.767 --> 02:03:35.607
rate's probably going to be here.

3199
02:03:35.607 --> 02:03:37.200
And I don't even try to predict watts.

3200
02:03:37.200 --> 02:03:38.461
So then I say, okay, I want you give 'em the bike.

3201
02:03:38.461 --> 02:03:41.546
And I want you to ride in slow increments until heart rate

3202
02:03:41.546 --> 02:03:45.963
is about here and your RPE is such that you can talk,

3203
02:03:46.830 --> 02:03:47.908
but you don't want to.

3204
02:03:47.908 --> 02:03:49.488
RPE, rate of perceived.

3205
02:03:49.488 --> 02:03:52.264
Exertion.
Exertion, yeah.

3206
02:03:52.264 --> 02:03:53.965
So then I would just say,

3207
02:03:53.965 --> 02:03:55.774
just do that for 45 minutes

3208
02:03:55.774 --> 02:03:58.090
and then do a quick lactate check.

3209
02:03:58.090 --> 02:04:00.264
And if they do that and it's 1.6, I say, great,

3210
02:04:00.264 --> 02:04:02.516
the next, in two days or the next day,

3211
02:04:02.516 --> 02:04:03.503
I want you to go out and do it.

3212
02:04:03.503 --> 02:04:05.770
And I want you to add five to 10 watts.

3213
02:04:05.770 --> 02:04:09.184
And if you come out and your 2.5 back off by 10 watts.

3214
02:04:09.184 --> 02:04:11.658
So we don't try to answer it all in one day,

3215
02:04:11.658 --> 02:04:13.077
but you can answer this question

3216
02:04:13.077 --> 02:04:14.139
over a period of a week.

3217
02:04:14.139 --> 02:04:15.431
Right.

3218
02:04:15.431 --> 02:04:17.882
And how is the adherence with your patients

3219
02:04:17.882 --> 02:04:19.283
when you tell them, okay,

3220
02:04:19.283 --> 02:04:22.061
your heart rate is not allowed to go over X

3221
02:04:22.061 --> 02:04:23.289
and they go out for a walk

3222
02:04:23.289 --> 02:04:25.116
and the minute there's the slightest incline,

3223
02:04:25.116 --> 02:04:26.969
or they want to put a little bit of kick

3224
02:04:26.969 --> 02:04:28.982
into anything they're doing, they're out of zone two.

3225
02:04:28.982 --> 02:04:30.847
And they're like, well, this is.

3226
02:04:30.847 --> 02:04:32.451
I try to keep them separate.

3227
02:04:32.451 --> 02:04:34.188
So I've actually found outdoor walking

3228
02:04:34.188 --> 02:04:37.266
and outdoor running to be very difficult for zone two.

3229
02:04:37.266 --> 02:04:39.922
So just like when I go rucking,

3230
02:04:39.922 --> 02:04:41.534
my heart rate is,

3231
02:04:41.534 --> 02:04:42.625
sometimes I'll hit max heart rate

3232
02:04:42.625 --> 02:04:44.952
if I'm on a steep enough hill.

3233
02:04:44.952 --> 02:04:46.907
So I just consider that a separate form of exercise.

3234
02:04:46.907 --> 02:04:48.613
Like a stationary bike?

3235
02:04:48.613 --> 02:04:50.893
Yeah, as uninteresting as it is.

3236
02:04:50.893 --> 02:04:52.820
I mean, I'm on a stationary bike

3237
02:04:52.820 --> 02:04:54.845
when I do it because I can control it.

3238
02:04:54.845 --> 02:04:56.145
And we try to have patients do it

3239
02:04:56.145 --> 02:04:58.893
on a treadmill stationary bike rowing machine,

3240
02:04:58.893 --> 02:05:01.067
super controlled, swimming is good too,

3241
02:05:01.067 --> 02:05:03.669
because you can really control your pace.

3242
02:05:03.669 --> 02:05:05.423
But outdoor cycling and outdoor walking,

3243
02:05:05.423 --> 02:05:06.256
running are hard.

3244
02:05:06.256 --> 02:05:08.369
Too many variables.

3245
02:05:08.369 --> 02:05:10.100
We don't wanna discourage people from doing those things,

3246
02:05:10.100 --> 02:05:11.027
but we just wanna acknowledge,

3247
02:05:11.027 --> 02:05:13.927
use those for maybe where you get your zone five.

3248
02:05:13.927 --> 02:05:15.181
Interesting.

3249
02:05:15.181 --> 02:05:17.664
Alright, our time's running short here

3250
02:05:17.664 --> 02:05:19.718
and I gotta let you go and get to the airport,

3251
02:05:19.718 --> 02:05:21.208
but I can't let you go without hearing

3252
02:05:21.208 --> 02:05:24.291
a little bit about this crazy TV show

3253
02:05:25.317 --> 02:05:28.130
that's coming out this fall "Limitless" that you did.

3254
02:05:28.130 --> 02:05:32.370
You did with Chris Hemsworth under the direction

3255
02:05:32.370 --> 02:05:33.818
of the great Darren Aronofsky.

3256
02:05:33.818 --> 02:05:35.678
I've seen the trailer.

3257
02:05:35.678 --> 02:05:37.086
I know it's coming out in this fall and I don't know how

3258
02:05:37.086 --> 02:05:42.086
much you can talk about it, but it looks absolutely epic.

3259
02:05:42.310 --> 02:05:44.687
And like quite the adventure.

3260
02:05:44.687 --> 02:05:46.210
It was a ton of fun.

3261
02:05:46.210 --> 02:05:49.203
Darren is a really close friend and you know,

3262
02:05:49.203 --> 02:05:53.870
Darren kind of pitched this idea to me probably in 2018.

3263
02:05:55.796 --> 02:05:56.808
He was like, I've got this idea.

3264
02:05:56.808 --> 02:06:00.054
I'm gonna pitch it to Nat Geo at the time.

3265
02:06:00.054 --> 02:06:02.221
Of course now it's Disney.

3266
02:06:03.162 --> 02:06:04.481
And he said, what do you think?

3267
02:06:04.481 --> 02:06:06.035
And I was like, I think it's a fantastic idea.

3268
02:06:06.035 --> 02:06:10.917
And he said like, who do you think should be the lead actor?

3269
02:06:10.917 --> 02:06:13.364
And we have a very close mutual friend in Hugh Jackman.

3270
02:06:13.364 --> 02:06:14.942
And I was like, what do you think about Hugh?

3271
02:06:14.942 --> 02:06:16.720
And he's like, that's perfect.

3272
02:06:16.720 --> 02:06:19.812
So he went and pitched Hugh to Nat Geo

3273
02:06:19.812 --> 02:06:21.275
and they loved the idea,

3274
02:06:21.275 --> 02:06:23.419
talked to Hugh, Hugh loved the idea.

3275
02:06:23.419 --> 02:06:24.664
And then it just, wasn't gonna work out.

3276
02:06:24.664 --> 02:06:26.299
Hugh had a bunch of other projects

3277
02:06:26.299 --> 02:06:27.719
that he really wanted to work on,

3278
02:06:27.719 --> 02:06:29.905
including "Music Man," and things like that.

3279
02:06:29.905 --> 02:06:31.222
Of course COVID messed everything up.

3280
02:06:31.222 --> 02:06:32.055
Yeah.

3281
02:06:32.997 --> 02:06:34.567
And then I don't know who suggested Chris,

3282
02:06:34.567 --> 02:06:37.160
whether it was Darren or the networks,

3283
02:06:37.160 --> 02:06:39.857
but then Chris became involved

3284
02:06:39.857 --> 02:06:41.577
and that in some ways changed

3285
02:06:41.577 --> 02:06:43.391
I think the focus of the program,

3286
02:06:43.391 --> 02:06:44.224
because I think.

3287
02:06:44.224 --> 02:06:45.057
He's quite a bit younger, obviously.

3288
02:06:45.057 --> 02:06:48.270
Right and Chris is like a superhero

3289
02:06:48.270 --> 02:06:50.776
and Hugh of course is, I mean,

3290
02:06:50.776 --> 02:06:52.765
you wanna talk about a guy with legendary fitness?

3291
02:06:52.765 --> 02:06:54.832
I don't know if you've seen "Music Man."

3292
02:06:54.832 --> 02:06:55.919
Yeah, he's unbelievable.

3293
02:06:55.919 --> 02:06:57.120
Yeah, yeah.

3294
02:06:57.120 --> 02:07:01.632
Not only like in his superhero fitness levels,

3295
02:07:01.632 --> 02:07:02.996
but also his ballet and like,

3296
02:07:02.996 --> 02:07:03.829
Yeah.

3297
02:07:03.829 --> 02:07:06.337
I mean his ability to move his body in space

3298
02:07:06.337 --> 02:07:07.285
and time is unreal.

3299
02:07:07.285 --> 02:07:09.286
Yeah, yeah, we were just in New York

3300
02:07:09.286 --> 02:07:10.570
to watch "Music Man,"

3301
02:07:10.570 --> 02:07:12.790
spend the weekend with him

3302
02:07:12.790 --> 02:07:14.596
and we had dinner after and I was like,

3303
02:07:14.596 --> 02:07:16.838
dude, I'm tired watching you.

3304
02:07:16.838 --> 02:07:18.820
And I don't know how you do that eight times a week.

3305
02:07:18.820 --> 02:07:20.789
Eight times a week he does this two and a half hour

3306
02:07:20.789 --> 02:07:22.636
show for a year.

3307
02:07:22.636 --> 02:07:25.263
What is he, 57 now or something like that?

3308
02:07:25.263 --> 02:07:26.988
Probably a bit younger than that.

3309
02:07:26.988 --> 02:07:29.238
Probably like maybe 53, 54.

3310
02:07:31.126 --> 02:07:32.790
But with Hugh, the idea we had

3311
02:07:32.790 --> 02:07:35.308
was kind of the way we opened our discussion,

3312
02:07:35.308 --> 02:07:38.308
which is you're no longer Wolverine,

3313
02:07:39.586 --> 02:07:41.580
what is it gonna take to be

3314
02:07:41.580 --> 02:07:43.401
the best version of you in your nineties?

3315
02:07:43.401 --> 02:07:44.405
I think with Chris,

3316
02:07:44.405 --> 02:07:48.285
the focus became a little more you're Thor.

3317
02:07:48.285 --> 02:07:50.085
Right.
Right.

3318
02:07:50.085 --> 02:07:52.532
Like what are the limits of that

3319
02:07:52.532 --> 02:07:56.199
and how do they jive with kind of longevity?

3320
02:07:57.743 --> 02:07:59.733
It was an amazing experience.

3321
02:07:59.733 --> 02:08:01.935
It took probably twice as long as anyone expected

3322
02:08:01.935 --> 02:08:03.962
because of COVID.

3323
02:08:03.962 --> 02:08:06.379
We started filming, we did a,

3324
02:08:07.354 --> 02:08:10.088
we did January and February a ton of work in 2020,

3325
02:08:10.088 --> 02:08:12.196
just before COVID hit in Australia

3326
02:08:12.196 --> 02:08:14.465
and in Norway and just by coincidence and by luck,

3327
02:08:14.465 --> 02:08:18.887
I suppose, those were the months I was heavily involved in.

3328
02:08:18.887 --> 02:08:22.563
Much of what came after, I became less involved in.

3329
02:08:22.563 --> 02:08:23.396
Yeah.

3330
02:08:24.357 --> 02:08:26.690
But there are six episodes

3331
02:08:27.749 --> 02:08:28.852
and I don't know what I can say about

3332
02:08:28.852 --> 02:08:31.916
any of them other than I think

3333
02:08:31.916 --> 02:08:33.322
there's some really good stuff in there.

3334
02:08:33.322 --> 02:08:35.896
I think my involvement is the least interesting

3335
02:08:35.896 --> 02:08:37.402
part of it is my personal take.

3336
02:08:37.402 --> 02:08:39.383
Like some of the other content that I've seen,

3337
02:08:39.383 --> 02:08:40.673
I find much more interesting

3338
02:08:40.673 --> 02:08:42.782
than the parts I was involved in.

3339
02:08:42.782 --> 02:08:44.632
And I think people will enjoy it a lot.

3340
02:08:44.632 --> 02:08:48.009
I first found out about it when Ross Edgley

3341
02:08:48.009 --> 02:08:51.048
was sharing some clips from.

3342
02:08:51.048 --> 02:08:52.177
Where are you guys in Norway?

3343
02:08:52.177 --> 02:08:53.010
Yeah.

3344
02:08:53.010 --> 02:08:55.970
Yeah, and swimming in the freezing water and all of that.

3345
02:08:55.970 --> 02:08:57.878
And Ross is like, I'm here with Darren and Chris

3346
02:08:57.878 --> 02:08:59.951
and I'm messaging, I'm friends with Ross.

3347
02:08:59.951 --> 02:09:01.399
And I was like, what are you guys doing?

3348
02:09:01.399 --> 02:09:03.397
How come I was not involved in this?

3349
02:09:03.397 --> 02:09:04.602
Or how come I wasn't invited?

3350
02:09:04.602 --> 02:09:06.814
I can't believe you guys are.

3351
02:09:06.814 --> 02:09:08.439
And he's like, ah, I can't really talk about it,

3352
02:09:08.439 --> 02:09:09.974
but it's cool.

3353
02:09:09.974 --> 02:09:12.774
So I've been joking with him about it

3354
02:09:12.774 --> 02:09:13.784
and I love Ross to death.

3355
02:09:13.784 --> 02:09:16.980
And I know that he worked really hard

3356
02:09:16.980 --> 02:09:19.058
in helping to train Chris for Thor

3357
02:09:19.058 --> 02:09:21.808
and got him in just insane shape.

3358
02:09:22.874 --> 02:09:26.341
But I'm curious on your perspective

3359
02:09:26.341 --> 02:09:30.302
on Ross and his kind of training protocol and philosophy,

3360
02:09:30.302 --> 02:09:31.778
given that he's,

3361
02:09:31.778 --> 02:09:36.080
attempting to do these ultramarathon swim adventures,

3362
02:09:36.080 --> 02:09:38.171
I'm always like confused by his approach.

3363
02:09:38.171 --> 02:09:39.085
I'm like, dude,

3364
02:09:39.085 --> 02:09:41.933
you should lose like 40 pounds of muscle

3365
02:09:41.933 --> 02:09:44.517
and trim down and you would have a better,

3366
02:09:44.517 --> 02:09:46.304
you'll lose some power,

3367
02:09:46.304 --> 02:09:49.809
but what you will gain in flexibility and efficiency

3368
02:09:49.809 --> 02:09:51.040
will completely outweigh that.

3369
02:09:51.040 --> 02:09:55.508
So what did Ross look like when he swam in college?

3370
02:09:55.508 --> 02:09:57.288
I think he was a water polo player in college.

3371
02:09:57.288 --> 02:09:59.355
I don't think he even swam competitively

3372
02:09:59.355 --> 02:10:03.250
and he's always been like compact and strong,

3373
02:10:03.250 --> 02:10:05.332
but there are videos of him like from years

3374
02:10:05.332 --> 02:10:06.588
and years and years ago,

3375
02:10:06.588 --> 02:10:08.819
like he has definitely progressed

3376
02:10:08.819 --> 02:10:13.300
as a kind of body buildingesque type specimen.

3377
02:10:13.300 --> 02:10:15.950
And he has his whole philosophy around it,

3378
02:10:15.950 --> 02:10:19.700
this mezzo phasing of the way that he trains.

3379
02:10:20.860 --> 02:10:23.260
But primarily he's in the gym doing tons of strength

3380
02:10:23.260 --> 02:10:26.632
training and the swimming doesn't come until much later.

3381
02:10:26.632 --> 02:10:30.189
But I just know when I put on upper body bulk,

3382
02:10:30.189 --> 02:10:32.857
I find it very difficult to swim

3383
02:10:32.857 --> 02:10:35.832
any distances without a lot of lactate buildup.

3384
02:10:35.832 --> 02:10:39.964
And when I was at my peak ultra conditioning where,

3385
02:10:39.964 --> 02:10:41.309
when I was training for Ultraman,

3386
02:10:41.309 --> 02:10:42.915
which is really a cycling race with a long run

3387
02:10:42.915 --> 02:10:45.989
at the end of it, I made the choice to sacrifice.

3388
02:10:45.989 --> 02:10:47.899
Ultra is 224 mile race?

3389
02:10:47.899 --> 02:10:51.232
Yeah, it's a three day double Ironman.

3390
02:10:52.126 --> 02:10:55.192
The first day, the first leg though is a kilometer swim.

3391
02:10:55.192 --> 02:10:56.275
Yeah, yeah.

3392
02:10:57.236 --> 02:10:58.815
But I was like, I'll just be like,

3393
02:10:58.815 --> 02:11:01.662
I will sacrifice my swim power to be able to have better

3394
02:11:01.662 --> 02:11:06.329
power to weight on the bike and be efficient on the run.

3395
02:11:07.343 --> 02:11:10.873
I kind of pushed that too far and got too skinny

3396
02:11:10.873 --> 02:11:14.385
where I had very little power per stroke,

3397
02:11:14.385 --> 02:11:15.928
but I was inexhaustible.

3398
02:11:15.928 --> 02:11:18.067
Like I just felt like I could swim forever

3399
02:11:18.067 --> 02:11:19.370
without getting tired.

3400
02:11:19.370 --> 02:11:21.782
And if he's swimming all the way around

3401
02:11:21.782 --> 02:11:23.725
great Britain and these other challenges

3402
02:11:23.725 --> 02:11:25.339
that he's planning on right now,

3403
02:11:25.339 --> 02:11:28.763
like doesn't that seem like a smarter way to go?

3404
02:11:28.763 --> 02:11:30.492
I mean, Ross is an amazing specimen.

3405
02:11:30.492 --> 02:11:31.325
It's incredible.

3406
02:11:31.325 --> 02:11:32.876
Yeah.

3407
02:11:32.876 --> 02:11:35.023
So far be it for me to judge what he's doing,

3408
02:11:35.023 --> 02:11:35.856
'cause it's working for him.

3409
02:11:35.856 --> 02:11:36.822
Yeah, yeah.

3410
02:11:36.822 --> 02:11:37.920
But I just thought like you,

3411
02:11:37.920 --> 02:11:39.641
as somebody who's interested in this kind of stuff,

3412
02:11:39.641 --> 02:11:41.565
whether you had a perspective?

3413
02:11:41.565 --> 02:11:42.982
No, not really.

3414
02:11:44.012 --> 02:11:46.263
One of the most interesting things that Ross and I got

3415
02:11:46.263 --> 02:11:48.892
really fixated on and what was,

3416
02:11:48.892 --> 02:11:49.951
and I don't know if I could talk about it.

3417
02:11:49.951 --> 02:11:51.624
'Cause I don't know if he's announced publicly

3418
02:11:51.624 --> 02:11:54.017
this kind of swim he has an aspiration to do.

3419
02:11:54.017 --> 02:11:55.274
I know about it.

3420
02:11:55.274 --> 02:11:56.784
I don't think that he's announced it publicly.

3421
02:11:56.784 --> 02:11:57.701
Yeah, so.

3422
02:12:01.589 --> 02:12:04.498
What we talked about that interested him was the sleep

3423
02:12:04.498 --> 02:12:05.665
cycling of it.

3424
02:12:06.675 --> 02:12:09.091
So a friend of mine, a cyclist named Mike,

3425
02:12:09.091 --> 02:12:10.016
well runner ultra runner,

3426
02:12:10.016 --> 02:12:13.586
ultra cyclist named Mike Travino lives in San Diego.

3427
02:12:13.586 --> 02:12:14.689
First year, he did RAM.

3428
02:12:14.689 --> 02:12:15.522
He was second.

3429
02:12:15.522 --> 02:12:19.100
He almost won RAM on his first attempt.

3430
02:12:19.100 --> 02:12:22.604
He got really into sort of timing sleep.

3431
02:12:22.604 --> 02:12:24.929
'Cause obviously with RAM for people listening,

3432
02:12:24.929 --> 02:12:26.159
it's this race across America,

3433
02:12:26.159 --> 02:12:28.748
it's a cycling race where basically it's just who can ride

3434
02:12:28.748 --> 02:12:30.310
their bike across America the quickest.

3435
02:12:30.310 --> 02:12:31.143
It's going on right now.

3436
02:12:31.143 --> 02:12:31.976
Yeah.

3437
02:12:31.976 --> 02:12:34.056
These guys are doing this in like eight days.

3438
02:12:34.056 --> 02:12:35.387
So you can imagine they're not sleeping

3439
02:12:35.387 --> 02:12:37.220
a whole heck of a lot.

3440
02:12:38.608 --> 02:12:40.573
And he was talking about how,

3441
02:12:40.573 --> 02:12:43.969
they're now able to sort of try to wake people up

3442
02:12:43.969 --> 02:12:46.308
at the right phase of sleep based on

3443
02:12:46.308 --> 02:12:48.104
rapid eye movement.

3444
02:12:48.104 --> 02:12:49.820
And so Ross was pretty interested in that,

3445
02:12:49.820 --> 02:12:51.201
which was if he was about to embark

3446
02:12:51.201 --> 02:12:52.800
on a really long swim,

3447
02:12:52.800 --> 02:12:55.555
we're talking things that would take weeks

3448
02:12:55.555 --> 02:12:57.550
and probably months,

3449
02:12:57.550 --> 02:12:59.757
could he really optimize his sleep

3450
02:12:59.757 --> 02:13:02.345
'cause what he didn't wanna do is sort of sleep eight hours,

3451
02:13:02.345 --> 02:13:04.698
swim, it's gotta be micros sleeps in there,

3452
02:13:04.698 --> 02:13:06.717
which again, I can't speak to how healthy

3453
02:13:06.717 --> 02:13:07.550
or unhealthy that is.

3454
02:13:07.550 --> 02:13:11.690
It just doesn't strike me as a great, longevity play.

3455
02:13:11.690 --> 02:13:13.622
But in the spirit of sort of pushing

3456
02:13:13.622 --> 02:13:15.526
the extreme limits of what a human can do,

3457
02:13:15.526 --> 02:13:16.818
that would be very interesting

3458
02:13:16.818 --> 02:13:17.906
other than just sort of random,

3459
02:13:17.906 --> 02:13:20.141
like when we used to do sleep sort of swim relays,

3460
02:13:20.141 --> 02:13:23.062
which we did a lot of these.

3461
02:13:23.062 --> 02:13:24.262
You'd sort of sleep three

3462
02:13:24.262 --> 02:13:26.036
and a half hours between your legs.

3463
02:13:26.036 --> 02:13:28.091
And it was just within three days, you're dead.

3464
02:13:28.091 --> 02:13:29.234
Right.

3465
02:13:29.234 --> 02:13:31.623
I feel like we're at the very beginning

3466
02:13:31.623 --> 02:13:33.421
of understanding what's going on with that.

3467
02:13:33.421 --> 02:13:36.145
And it does seem to be very personalized.

3468
02:13:36.145 --> 02:13:37.805
I know in the ultra running world,

3469
02:13:37.805 --> 02:13:42.058
like some people they can do these 15 minute little naps

3470
02:13:42.058 --> 02:13:44.541
and they seem to function at a pretty high output

3471
02:13:44.541 --> 02:13:47.708
and other people just can't manage it.

3472
02:13:50.589 --> 02:13:52.647
I have such respect for that.

3473
02:13:52.647 --> 02:13:55.671
There's a part of me that certainly always will miss

3474
02:13:55.671 --> 02:13:57.755
that type of pushing.

3475
02:13:57.755 --> 02:13:58.660
You're done.

3476
02:13:58.660 --> 02:13:59.723
Yeah, I'm done.

3477
02:13:59.723 --> 02:14:01.048
And I don't.

3478
02:14:01.048 --> 02:14:02.161
I'm really looking forward to kinda spending

3479
02:14:02.161 --> 02:14:04.539
the rest of my life trying to figure out

3480
02:14:04.539 --> 02:14:08.828
how to be a healthy, productive kick ass 90 year old.

3481
02:14:08.828 --> 02:14:09.912
Yeah, excellent.

3482
